5001:
** τάγμα**, ατος, τό,
(τάσσω) **ordinance, command**, νόμου τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 414e}}; ἐκ δυοῖν τ. from a combination of two **ordinances**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1294b6.}}
**II** **fixed assessment** or **payment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 1349a24, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2562.14 (from Crete).}}
**III** **body of soldiers, division, brigade**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.11, “PFrankf.” 7.5 (3rd c.BC), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 96.48, 103.28, “PRein.” 14.31 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.85.3, etc.}}
**III.b** = Latin _manipulus_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.24.5.}}
**III.c** = Latin _legio_, **legion**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.42, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.3.8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 12, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 71.9, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4693 (from Abukir).}}
**IV** **order, rank,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.757 (from Naples)}}; βουλευτικὸν τ. {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4411b5 (from Cilicia); ἱππικὸν τ. [prev. work]2803 (from Aphrodisias)}}; τὸ τ. τῶν γυμνασιάρχων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1252v. 24 (3rd c.AD)}}; τοῦ πρώτου τ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 42(1).81 (Epid., 1st c.AD): _accusative_ τάγμα}} as _adverb_, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3765 (uncertain), compare “IG” 14.748 (from Naples).}}
**V** generally, **arrangement**, of footprints, τίς ὁ τρόπος τοῦ τ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 114}}; **row** of bricks, uncertain in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 153.}}
**V.2** **status**, φύσεως τάγμα ἔχειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.24U.}}; **function**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5 “fragment” 1}}; ἐν τ. γενόμενοι with _infinitive_, being in a **position** to.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.358.7 (2nd c.AD).}}

5003:
** τᾰλαιπωρ-έω**,
_perfect_ τεταλαιπώρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.19, etc.}}:—_passive_, with _future_ _middle_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.438 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: _aorist_ ἐταλαιπωρήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.64, etc.}}: _perfect_ τεταλαιπώρημαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.560}}:—**do hard work, endure hardship** or **distress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 672, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99, 5.74; ὑπὸ χειμῶνος [prev. author] 2.101}}; ἑωυτοῖσι for their own benefit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 16; ἐθέλουσα ταλαιπωρεῖν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.93; τῷ σώματι ἀδύνατος ταλαιπωρεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.12; λυποῦνται καὶ συνεχῶς ταλαιπωροῦσι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.16}}: with _dative_, **suffer by reason of**, ἐλπίσι κεναῖς Polystr.p.31 W.
**II** rarely _transitive_, **distress, trouble**, ὁ πόλεμος πάντας τρόπους τεταλαιπώρηκεν ἡμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.19, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.20}}; ἀνδρὸς.. ὑμᾶς μηδ᾽ ὅσον προπέμψαι ποι αὐτὸν ἀπιόντα.. -ήσαντος who **did** not **trouble** you even to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 56.41}}:—frequently in _passive_, **to be distressed, suffer hardship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 19, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.78 (assuming variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95d, “R.” 372d; ἐν τοῖς ἀγροῖς.. ταλαιπωρουμένους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 224; ἵνα μὴ ταλαιπωροῖτο μηδ᾽ ἄχθος φέροι [prev. author] “Ra.” 24, compare “V.” 967}}; τεταλαιπωρημένοι ὑπὸ τῆς νόσου **worn out** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3; τῷ μήκει τοῦ πολέμου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19; διὰ τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.38}}; τὸ σῶμα ταλαιπωρούμενον **being distressed**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 37; σμικρὰ παιδία.. κρύει ταλαιπωρούμενα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.43; ἐν ταῖς ὁσημέραι πράξεσι πολλὰ -ούμενος [prev. work]471.}}

5004:
** τᾰλαιπωρ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **hard labour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.620,741}}; also simply, **regular use, exercise**, τῆς χειρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 53.}}
**2** **hardship, distress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.117}}; τῇ τοῦ σώματος τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.17}}; ἡ ἐν τοῖς ἔργοις τ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.17.8}}; ἡ περὶ τὸ πρᾶγμα τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.53 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus: _plural_, ταλαιπωρίας ἐνδέκεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11; τετρυμένοι.. ταλαιπωρίῃσί τε καὶ ἡλίῳ [prev. work] 12.}}
**3** **bodily suffering** or **pain**, caused by disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49.}}

5005:
** τᾰλαίπωρος**, ον,
**suffering, distressed, miserable**, Θῆβαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 197; βροτοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 233; ὦ ταλαίπωρ᾽ [prev. work] 317, compare 595 (Lyric poetry), 623, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 14, etc.; ἀνδρῶν γένος [prev. author] “fragment” 945; τ. ἄρα τις σύ γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 302b}}; τὸ τ. **hardihood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 414}}; **a hard life**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}}“Aër.” 19. _adverb_ **-ρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 54, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.4.}}
**2** of things, τ. βίος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 91; πράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 135; πάθος 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 144; ταλαιπωρότερον οὐδέν ἐστι.. τῆς γαστρός 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 60.3.}}

5006:
** τᾰλαντ-ιαῖος**, α, ον,
**worth a talent**, οἶκος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.64; κτῆσις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.4.3}}; νοσήματα τ. **costing a talent**, probably in fee to the physician, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 12.}}
**2** of persons, **worth a talent**, i.e. **possessed of one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 32}}; ἔγγυοι τ. giving surety **to the amount of a talent**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1350a19.}}
**II** **weighing a talent**, ξύλον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 311b3}}; λιθοβόλος τ. an engine throwing stones **of a talent weight**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.41.8 codices}}; πετροβόλος τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 85.2.}}
**II.2** **in which the prize is a talent**, ἀγών {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2810.18 (from Aphrodisias).}}

5007:
** τᾰλαντ-ον**, τό,
**balance**, Ζεὺς.. τὸ τ. ἐπιρρέπει ἄλλοτε ἄλλως {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 157; ζυγὸν ταλάντου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 823 (Lyric poetry); ταλάντῳ μουσικὴ σταθμήσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 797}}: in this sense used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _plural_, **pair of scales**, ἔχον ὥς τε τάλαντα γυνή.., ἥ τε σταθμὸν ἔχουσα καὶ εἴριον ἀμφὶς ἀνέλκει ἰσάζουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.433}}; especially of the **scales** in which Zeus weighed the fortunes of men, χρύσεια πατὴρ ἐτίταινε τ. {{Refs|8.69, 22.209; γνῶ γὰρ Διὸς ἱρὰ τ. 16.658}}; ἐπὴν κλίνῃσι τ. Ζεύς, i.e. when he decides the issue of battle,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|19.223; τ. βρίσας οὐκ ἰσορρόπῳ τύχῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 346}}; of the **scales** of justice, δίκης κατέκειτο τάλαντα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 324, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.267.4 (3rd c.BC(?): Diotimus Epigrammaticus)}}: so in _singular_, δίκας ῥέπει τάλαντον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.25.}}
**2** **tax paid for the use of the public scales**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 4.7 (Cyzicus, 6th c.BC).}}
**II** **anything weighed**,
**II.1** **a definite weight, talent**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of gold, δέκα χρυσοῖς τάλαντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.122; δύω χρ. τ. 18.507; δέκα πάντα τ. ten in all, 19.247, 24.232}}; χρυσοῦ.. εὐεργέος ἑπτὰ τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.202; χρυσοῖο τάλαντον.. τιμήεντος 8.393}}: from the order of the prizes in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.262f}} and other passages its weight was probably not great,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 164.}}
**II.2** in post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus writers, the τάλαντον}} was both a **commercial weight** (differing in different systems), and also the **sum of money** represented by the corresponding weight of gold or silver; τοῖσι μὲν (of the subjects of Darius) ἀργύριον ἀπαγινέουσι εἴρητο Βαβυλώνιον σταθμὸν τ. ἀπαγινέειν, τοῖσι δὲ χρυσίον ἀπαγινέουσι Εὐβοϊκόν· τὸ δὲ Βαβυλώνιον τ. δύναται Εὐβοΐδας (ὀκτὼ καὶ add. Reiz) ἑβδομήκοντα μνέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.89}}; τ. Ἀττικόν, Αἰγιναῖον, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.86; ἐποίησε [ὁ Σόλων] σταθμὰ πρὸς τὸ νόμισμα τρεῖς καὶ ἑξήκοντα μνᾶς τὸ τ. ἀγούσας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 10.2.}}
**II.2.a** of money, τ. ἀργυρίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.28, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.1, etc.; χίλια τ. νομίσματος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.174}}; μνᾶ ἀπὸ τοῦ τ. {{Refs|“IG” 12.220.7, compare 92.37, +others}}
**II.2.b** of weight, τὸ τ. τὸ ἐμπορικόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]22.1013.35; used in weighing lead, [prev. work]12.374.287, 42(1).103.131 (Epid., 4th c.BC), “PMich.Zen.” 9.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; iron, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 144.6 (3rd c.BC), “CPHerm.” p.77 (3rd c.AD)}}; cloth, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 120.8 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}

5009:
** τᾰμῐ-εῖον**, τό,
**treasury**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.96, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416d, 550d, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 18}}; of the Roman **aerarium**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.31.5, “IG” 42(1).63.7 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}; the Imperial **fiscus,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PRyl. 84.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **magazine, storehouse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 4.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1344b33, “PCair.Zen.” 299.7, 472.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; **store-room,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.4.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 14, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.7, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 108.2, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.51, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.9e}}; **reservoir**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.10.}}
**3** **chamber, closet**, {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.43.30, +others, NT.Matt.6.6. (Freq. _contraction_ to ταμεῖον in Papyri, Inscrr., and codices from 1st c.BC, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.3 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (citing 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus [same place]), 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor [same place], “CPR” 1.83 (1st c.AD), “SIG” 1242.6 (Ephesus, 3rd c.AD), etc.}}; also written ταμῖον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.216.22 (1st c.AD), etc.)}}

5010:
** τάξις**, εως,
_Ionic dialect_ ιος, ἡ, (τάσσω) **arranging, arrangement**:
**I** in military sense:
**I.1** **drawing up in rank and file, order** or **disposition of an army**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.68 ([near the start]), 7.5, etc.}}; τὰ ἀμφὶ τάξεις rules for it, **tactics**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.7; τ. καὶ ἀντίταξις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 12.}}
**I.2** **battle array, order of battle**, κατὰ τάξιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86; ἐν τάξει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.72, etc.}}; ἐς τάξιν καθίστασθαι, ἀνάγειν,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 93, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 400 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἵνα μὴ διασπασθείη ἡ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.70}}; of ships, ἀποπλῶσαι ἐκ τῆς τάξιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.14.}}
**I.3** **a single rank** or **line** of soldiers, ἐπὶ τάξιας ὀλίγας γίγνεσθαι to be drawn up a few **lines** deep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 111, compare 9.31; ἐλύθησαν αἱ τ. τῶν Περσῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 191c.}}
**I.4** **body of soldiers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 298, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1311}}; especially at Athens, **the quota of infantry furnished by each** φυλή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ταξίαρχος 11), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.16}}; but frequently of smaller bodies, **company**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.16, 6.5.11, etc.; ἱππέων τ. [prev. work] 1.8.21}}; so of ships, **squadron**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 380}}: generally, **band, company**, φιλία γὰρ ἥδε τ., of the chorus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 128 (Lyric poetry); ἐμφανίσας μοι ἐν ᾗ ἔσομαι τάξει “PCair.Zen.” 409.6 (3rd c.BC).}}
**I.4.b** especially **a contingent of** {{Refs|128}}**men**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.8, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 10.2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 9.3.}}
**I.4.c** in late Gr., **membership of the militia palatina** (compare ταξεώτης), {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 27.17.}}
**I.5** **post** or **place in the line** of battle, ἀξιεύμεθα ταύτης τῆς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26, compare 27; ἐν τῇ τ. εἶχε ἑωυτόν [prev. author] 1.82}}; μένειν ἐν τῇ ἑωυτοῦ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.158; τ. φυλάξων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 664; ἡ τ. φυλακτέα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.43; ᾗ ἕκαστος τὴν τ. εἶχεν [prev. author] “An.” 4.3.29; τῆς πρώτης τ. τεταγμένος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.11, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.68 (near the end)}}; ἐκλιπόντας τὴν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.75, compare 9.21}}; λείπειν τὴν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.34, 15.32, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.159, etc.; παραχωρεῖν τῆς τάξεως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.36, etc.}}; but ἡγεμὼν ἔξω τάξεων officer **on the unattached list,** {{Refs|Arch.Papyrus 3.188, compare “Sammelb.” 599, “OGI” 69 (from Coptos)}}; so οἱ ἔξω τάξεως staff-officers, aides-de-camp, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.22.}}
**II** generally, **arrangement, order**, ἡμερῶν τ. εἰς μηνῶν περιόδους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 809d}}; ἡ τῶν ὅλων τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.22}}; **disposition**, τῆς ψυχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 14}}: Rhet., **disposition**, opposed to λέξις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1414a29; ἡ τ. τοῦ λόγου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.205, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.2, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.18, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ τῆς τάξεως τῶν ἰδίων βιβλίων” 1}}; ὕστερον τῇ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.68, 16.533}}; ἐν τ. εἶναι, ={μένειν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153e; τ. καὶ ἠρεμία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1218a23; εἰ τὰ γυμνάσια ἔχοι τὴν τ. ἐνταῦθα [prev. author] “Pol.” 1331a37}}; different from θέσις or mere position, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 188a24, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 60 (θέσεως τ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.194}}; τ. θέσεως is uncertain reading in 16.709); ἡ κατὰ τ. τινὰ βασιλεία, opposed to ἀόριστος τυραννίς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1366a2}}; καὶ τοῦτο κατὰ τ., ἕως.. and so **on**, until.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.62.}}
**II.2** **order, regularity**, εἰς τ. ἄγειν ἐκ τῆς ἀταξίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 30a; τ. καὶ κόσμος [prev. author] “Grg.” 504a}}; οὔτε νόμος οὔτε τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 875c, compare “R.” 587a; τ. περιόδου 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.42U.; διὰ τάξεως γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 780a}}; τάξιν ἔχειν **to be regular**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.6}}; ἐν τάξει **in an orderly manner**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 637e; so τάξει “SIG” 741.12 (Nysa, 1st c.BC}}, rendering of Latin _ordine_).
**II.3** **ordinance**, κατὰ τὴν τ. τοῦ νόμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 925b}}; παρὰ τὴν τοῦ νομοθέτου τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 305c, etc.}}
**II.3.b** **prescription**, τὴν τοῦ λυσιτελοῦντος τοῖς σώμασι ποιεῖσθαι τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 294e}}; **recipe**, conjecture in {{Refs|“PHolm.” 2.2.}}
**II.4** τ. τοῦ φόρου **assessment** of tribute, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.5, compare “IG” 12.63.2, +others}}; τῶν ὀφειλημάτων περὶ τῆς πράξεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]57.13, cf. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.45}}; τ. τῆς ὑδρείας a **ration** of water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844b.}}
**II.5** **political order, constitution**, τ. Κρητική, Λακωνική, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271b40, compare “Ath.” 3.1, +others}}
**III** _metaphorically_ from {{Refs|1.5}}, **post, rank, position, station**, ὑπὸ χθόνα τάξιν ἔχουσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 396 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἡ τῶν ἀκοντιζόντων τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.7}}; ἰδία τοῦ βίου τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.2}}; ἀνὴρ τῆς πρώτης τ. {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2767.4 (from Aphrodisias)}}; οἰκέτου τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.258, compare “PGnom.” 43, 196 (2nd c.AD), Mitteis “Chr.” 372 see 18 (2nd c.AD)}}; τ. ἔχοντος ἐν τῷ Μουσείῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 6674.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐν τῇ Θετταλῶν τάξει **ranging herself with** the T., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.63}}; ἐν ἐχθροῦ τ. **as** an enemy, {{Refs|[prev. author] 20.81, etc.}}; ἐν ἐπηρείας τάξει by **way** of insult, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.13}}; ἀδύνατον εἶχεν τ. occupied an impossible **position**, i.e. was unthinkable, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator (?) “Oxy.” 1607.60}}; τὴν ὑπὲρ ὑμῶν ἑλόμενον τάξιν πολιτεύεσθαι **championship** of your cause, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.138, compare “Ep.” 3.15}}; ἐγὼ τὴν τῆς εὐνοίας τ... οὐκ ἔλιπον **post** of patriotism, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.173.}}
**IV** **order, class** of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.7}}; **function**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.19.}}
**IV.2** **list, register**, ὅπως ταγῇ αὐτοῦ τὸ ὄνομα ἐν τῇ τῶν τετελευτηκότων τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7359.15, compare 7404.6, “PSI” 9.1064.38, 10.1141.10 (all 2nd c.AD)}}; ἡ τ. τῶν κατοχίμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 318.21 (2nd c.AD)}}; τ. λαογράφων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.182b2 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV.3** **account**, ἰδίας τάξεως {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 61.8 (3rd c.AD),compare “PLond.” 3.1107.26, 30 (3rd c.AD).}}
**IV.4** **payment**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 966.3 (4th c.AD,compare “Arch.Papyrus” 4.533).}}
**IV.5** **category** of land, κατοικικὴ τ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 379.12 (1st c.AD)}}, cf. Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 341.15 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**V** **reduction** of hernia by manipulation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.781.}}
**VI** **degree** of heating power in drugs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 11.571,787, compare 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 14.}}
**VII** **treatise**, ἐν τῇ ὑστέρᾳ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista cited in Zos.Alch. p.153 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**VIII** **fixed point of time, term**, κατ᾽ ἐνιαυτὸν ἢ κατά τινα ἄλλην τ. ἢ χρόνον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1261a34}}; **end** (or perhaps **date fixed for the end**), μέχρι τάξεως αὐτῆς τῆς τρύγης {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5810.15 (4th c.AD).}}

5011:
** τᾰπεινός**, ή, όν,
**low**:
**1** of Place, **low-lying**, χώρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.191, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 352b32 (_comparative_)}}; νῆσος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.21}}; ταπεινὰ νέμεσθαι to live in **low regions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.82; τ. ἕζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1411 (Lyric poetry); ἡ μαῖα καθεζέσθω -οτέρα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.70a, compare 2.61}}; of stature or size, **low**, ὀστᾶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.4; τροχοί [prev. work] 10.6}}; of position in the body, τὰ τ. τοῦ θώρακος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.531}}; of a river, **low**, opposed to μέγας, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.43.3}}; of stars, **low**, i.e. near the horizon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.5, +others}}; but also, **near the earth**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.5}}; of the sun, opposed to ὑψηλός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 8}}; λοξὸς καὶ τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.87.}}
**2** of persons, **humbled, abased in power, pride**, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.14}}; σὺ δ᾽ οὐδέπω τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 322, compare 908; τ. παρέχειν τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.13; τά τοι μέγιστα πολλάκις θεὸς ταπείν᾽ ἔθηκε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 716, compare “Hec.” 245, “Andr.” 979}}; **submissive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 5.4 (_comparative_), etc.}}; αἱ τ. τῶν πόλεων **small, poor, weak**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.95, compare 7.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.69 (_superlative_)}}; τ. δύναμις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.23}}; **of low intelligence**, αἱ τῶν ἀσυνέτων καὶ τ. ἀνθρώπων ψυχαί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.220; τὴν μικρὰν καὶ τ. [ἰατρικὴν θεωρίαν] ὁ Ἱπποκράτης ηὔξησεν [prev. author] 16.550.}} _adverb_, ταπεινῶς (or ταπεινὰ) πράττειν to be **in low estate** or **obscurity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 6; -νῶς ζῆν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 227; ὁμιλεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313b41.}}
**3** of the spirits, **downcast, dejected**, διάνοια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61; τ. καὶ ἔρημοι συνεκάθηντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.23, compare 6.4.16.}}
**4** in moral sense, either bad, **mean, base, abject**, τ. καὶ ἀνελεύθερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 791d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.34, etc.}}; or good, **lowly, humble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.5}}, frequently in **NT,** {{NT|NT.Matt.11.29, NT.2Cor.7.6, +others}}
**5** of things, **mean, low, poor**, τ. καὶ ἄπορος δίαιτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762e, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.48 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: _superlative_, -οτάτη περίστασις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Vit.” p.26 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; θεωρία -οτέρα, opposed to τιμιωτέρα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 639a1}}; of style, **low, poor**, τ. λέξις, opposed to κεκοσμημένη, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1404b6.}} _adverb_, -νῶς λέγειν **in a submissive manner**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1408a19.}}

5013:
** τᾰπειν-όω**,
**lower**, in point of height, **reduce**, σπλῆνα (swollen spleen) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.155}}:—_passive_, πᾶν ὄρος ταπεινωθήσεται {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Isa.40.4; πρόσωπον ἐκ μετεώρου ταπεινούμενον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 208}}; of a plant, **decrease in size**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.13.9}}; of rivers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.36}}; Astrology texts, of a planet, **suffer dejection**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 119.15.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **lessen**, τὸν φθόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 32; τὸ ἐν μέλιτι χολῶδες 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.683 (to explanation κολάζεται in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 59)}}; **disparage, minimize**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.15.7, compare 3.85.7}}:—_passive_, **to be lowered** or **lessened**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72d.}}
**II.2** **humble, abase**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.18; τ. καὶ συστέλλων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 210e; ταπεινώσαντες.. τοὺς νῦν ἐπηρμένους 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.235}}:—_passive_, ταπεινωθεὶς ἕπεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254e; ὑπὸ πενίας [prev. author] “R.” 553c, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.225 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τεταπείνωται ἡ τῶν Ἀθηναίων δόξα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.4; ἐταπεινοῦντο ταῖς ἐλπίσι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.11; τεταπεινωμένων τῶν ἄλλων διὰ τὴν τῶν πραγμάτων κατάστασιν Anonymus “Oxy.” 664.22.}}
**II.2.b** **violate** a woman, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.34.2, LXX.2Ki.13.12, 14, LXX.Eze.22.10, 11.}}
**II.3** in moral sense, **make lowly, humble**, ἑαυτόν {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.38 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, NT.Matt.23.12, +others}}:—_passive_, **humble oneself**, τὴν θεὸν ἐξιλάσαντο τῷ ταπεινοῦσθαι σφόδρα {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 544, cf. LXX.Gen.16.9, LXX.Sir.18.21, NT.1Pet.5.6.}}
**II.4** especially of fasting or abstinence, θεοῦ ᾧ πᾶσα ψυχὴ ἐν τῇ σήμερον ἡμέρᾳ (i.e. on a fast-day) ταπεινοῦται μεθ᾽ ἱκετείας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1181.11 (Rhenea, Jewish, 2nd c.AD)}}; ἐὰν ψυχὴν τεταπεινωμένην ἐμπλήσῃς feed **the hungry**, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.58.10, cf. LXX.Lev.23.27, +others}}; οἶδα ταπεινοῦσθαι, opposed to περισσεύειν, {{NT|NT.Phil.4.12.}}

5014:
** τᾰπείν-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**lowering**, of a movement in dancing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 815a.}}
**2** **lessening**, opposed to αὔξησις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 689a25}}; **reduction** of a swelling, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.816.}}
**3** **humiliation, abasement**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.51 (_plural_), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.33.10; τ. καὶ δουλεία 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.45; δυνάμεώς τινων ποιεῖν -σιν [prev. author] 11.87, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 7}}; ἀγέννεια καὶ τ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1457.4}}; **low estate, low condition**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.29.32, NT.Luke.1.48, +others}}
**4** **lowness of style**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.7a, 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus “Inst.” 8.3.48.}}
**5** **dejection** of a planet (compare ταπείνωμα), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dorotheus Astrologus 86.}}

5015:
** τᾰράσσω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.63, etc.}}; _Attic dialect_ **ταράττω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 902}}; also **θράσσω** (which see): _future_ ταράξω {{Refs|[prev. work] 358, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐτάραξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.291, (συν-) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.579, 8.86}}: _pluperfect_ συν-ετεταράχει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.36: _Epic dialect_}}_perfect_ in _passive_ sense τέτρηχα (see. below {{Refs|111)}}:—_passive_, _future_ ταραχθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 858 (probably), 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 3, etc.}}; _middle_ ταράξομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.43}}: _aorist_ ἐταράχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 386 (_anapaest meter_), etc.:}}_perfect_ τετάραγμαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 388 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.:—**stir, trouble**, in a physical sense, σύναγεν νεφέλας ἐτάραξε δὲ πόντον [Ποσειδῶν] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.291; κύμασιν ταράσσεται πόντος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 54, compare 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 54; τ. πέλαγος ἁλός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 88, compare 692; ὁμοῦ τ. τήν τε γῆν καὶ τὴν θάλατταν εἰκῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 431; τ. καὶ κυκᾶν [prev. author] “Ach.” 688 (troch.), “Eq.” 251 (troch.)}}; οὐ χθόνα ταράσσοντες **troubling** not the earth (by ploughing), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place]; βροντήμασι.. κυκάτω πάντα καὶ ταρασσέτω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 994}}; τ. φάρμακον perhaps **mix**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 18: _metaphorically_, φωνὰν ταρασσέμεν}} **to wag** the tongue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.42}}; πάντα τ., of a speaker, **jumble up**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.93; τὴν τῶν πραγμάτων διδασκαλίαν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.185.}}
**2** **trouble** the mind, **agitate, disturb**, με δεινὸς ὀρθομαντείας πόνος στροβεῖ ταράσσων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1216}}; δεινὰ (adverbial) τ. [με] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 483 (Lyric poetry); ὅταν ταράξῃ Κύπρις ἡβῶσαν φρένα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 969, compare “fragment” 1079.4; Νικίαν ταράξω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 358 (troch.); τ. καρδίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1321}}; especially of fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 289, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 66, etc.}}; ἄν τις φόβος τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.6; τὸ σῶμα τ. τὴν ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66a, compare 103c; so τ. γλῶσσαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1542}}: _absolutely_, **cause confusion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564b, “Hp.Mi.” 373b}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 100d, etc.; περί τι [prev. author] “Sph.” 242c; διά τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.3; ταράσσομαι φρένας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1095}}; ὄμμα σὸν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 253.}}
**3** of an army, etc., **throw into disorder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.125, 9.51, etc.; ἐτάρασσον τοὺς ταρσοὺς τῶν κωπέων [prev. author] 8.12}}:—_passive_, **to be in disorder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.125, 129, 8.16, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.27, etc.}}; ἐν σφίσιν αὐτοῖς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67.}}
**3.b** _metaphorically_, **rout** or **upset**, κριτήριον τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.38 (perhaps variant of 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 24)}}:—_passive_, λόγου ταραχθέντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.136 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; εἰ τὰ σημεῖα ταραχθείη 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.262.}}
**4** τ. τὴν γαστέρα **cause relaxation of** the bowels, of purges, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 12, compare “Acut.” 56, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 864b23, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.667}}:—_passive_, ἐταράχθης τὴν γαστέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 386 (_anapaest meter_); τὸ πνεῦμα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.903; more generally, τεταραγμένον σῶμα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.105.}}
**5** frequently of political agitation, τ. τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 867; τὰ πράγματα [prev. work] 214}}:—_passive_, **to be in a state of disorder** or **anarchy**, ἐν ἀλλήλοις τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.14, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 164.}}
**6** ταράττεσθαι ἐπὶ τῶν ἵππων **to be shaken in one's seat** on horseback, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.17.}}
**7** in Mathematics texts, τεταραγμένη ἀναλογία **disturbed** proportion, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5 “Def.” 18, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.4.}}
**II** **stir up**, _metaphorically_, τ. νεῖκος, πόλεμον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 794 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567a; φόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 797; ἡλίκα πράγματα ταράξασα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.153, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.10.9; τ. δίκας τινὶ πρός τινας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 5}}:—_passive_, πόλεμος ἐταράχθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.151; γόος.. ταραχθείς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 331 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** except in the places mentioned, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only _intransitive_}}_perfect_ τέτρηχα, **to be in disorder** or **confusion, be in an uproar**, τετρήχει δ᾽ ἀγορή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.95; ἀγορὴ τετρηχυῖα 7.346; so τετρηχυῖα θάλασσα “Anthologia Graeca” 7.283 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); τετρηχότος οἴδματος 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1167; τετρηχότα βῶλον [prev. author] 3.1393; τετρηχότι νώτῳ 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 267}}; but ἐκ σέθεν.. ἄλγεα.. τετρήχασι **cruel** woes **arise**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.447, compare 3.276, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philetas 7; in 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 72 τετρήχοντα κλήματα is _falsa lectio_ for{δὲ τρήχοντα}. (Alexandrine and later Poets seem to have thought erroneously that τέτρηχα}}] = **to be rough** (compare τραχύς).) (ταράχψω from ταραχ-ή, τάραχ-ος and these from *θᾰρᾰχ-: _cognate_ with θράσσω from θρᾱχ-yω of which the _Ionic dialect_ _perfect_ is τέτρηχα.)

5016:
** τᾰρᾰχή**,
also **τάρχη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|(5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}, ἡ, **disorder**, physiological **disturbance** or **upheaval**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.105, 2.59; τοῦ πνεύματος [prev. author] 1.46}}; ἡ ἀπὸ τῆς φλεβοτομίας τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.11}}; especially of the bowels, τῆς κοιλίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 205; οὐδὲ θόρυβόν τινα ἢ τ. ἐν τῇ κοιλίᾳ ποιεῖ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.825.}}
**2** of the mind, αἱ φρενῶν ταραχαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.30}}; ἀνωμαλία καὶ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.6; ἐν πολλῇ τ. καὶ φόβῳ ὄντας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.79; τ. παρέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66d, compare “R.” 602c}}; ἐν οἵαις ἦν τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; πολλὴν ἔχει τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b4; τ. μειρακιώδους μεστός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.230; ταραχῆς γέμων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 17, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.56 W.(_plural_)}}; διανοίας ἰσχυρὰ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.46; τὴν τ. τοῦ ὀφθαλμοῦ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 81.}}
**3** of an army or fleet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.77, etc.}}; ἐν τῇ τ. in the **confusion**, in the **mêlée**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.126.}}
**4** **political confusion, tumult**, and in _plural_ **tumults, troubles**, πολλὴ τ. περὶ τῶν τιμέων ἐγένετο {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.162, compare 6.5}}; ἐν τῇ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.150; αἱ τ. γεγενημέναι ἦσαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.53; τ. ἐγγίγνεταί τισι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.5; τ. ποιεῖν τισι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86; ἐς τ. καθιστάναι τινάς [prev. author] 4.75, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.107, etc.; εἰς τ. προκαθεῖναι τὴν πόλιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.5; ἐν τ. καθεστηκέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.233; ἐν ταραχαῖς εἶναι [prev. author] 4.138; ταραχῆς τε καὶ ἀνομίας μεστὴν πολιτείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.146b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.31; τ. καθίστατο τῶν ξυμμάχων πρὸς τὴν Λακεδαίμονα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.25, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.18; τ. ἐμπίπτει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.81}}; τ. διαλύειν, κατασβεννύναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.55}}; of rebellions or civil wars in Egypt, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.20 (from Rosetta), Wilcken “Chr.” 9.11, 167.14, Mitteis “Chr.” 31 see 29 (all 2nd c.BC)}}: = Latin _tumultus_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 33.}}

5019:
** ταρσ-ός**,
_Attic dialect_ **ταρρός**, ὁ: also with irregular _plural_ ταρσά, τά, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.470, “Anacreontea” 9, “APl.” 4.283 (6th c.AD: Leontius Epigrammaticus), 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 1.270, +others}}: (τέρσομαι):— **frame of wicker-work, crate, flat basket**, for drying cheeses on, ταρσοὶ μὲν τυρῶν βρῖθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.219, cf. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.37}}: generally, **basket**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 226.}}
**2** **mat of reeds**, such as were built into brickwork to bind it together, ταρσοὶ καλάμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, “SIG” 245 G 13 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; τ. καλάμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76.}}
**3** **mass of matted roots**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.7.2.}}
**II** of various broad flat surfaces, resembling a ταρσός {{Refs|1.1, as}}
**II.1** τ. ποδός **flat** of the foot. **the part between the toes and the heel**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.377, 388, cf. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 9, 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 6}} (but also, **palm** of the hand, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. passage]); οὐλὴ ταρσῷ ἀριστερῷ “PMich.Teb.” 121r111 i3 (1st c.AD)}}: generally, **foot,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anacreont. 35.4, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.470, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.26 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus), 9.653 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.1.b** **palm** of the hand, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 81, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de signis fracturarum - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 22; see supr. 11.1a.}}
**II.1.c** **ankle**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 3.6}}; but distinguished from σφυρόν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.84.}}
**II.2** τοὺς τ. τῶν κωπέων **the rows of oars** on the sides of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12}}; so τοὺς τ. alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.40: _sing._, “IG” 22.1628.590, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.50.3; ὁ δεξιὸς τ. τῆς νεώς [prev. author] 16.3.12}}: _sing._, **oar**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1346.}}
**II.3** τ. πτερύγων **flat** of the outstretched wing, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.144 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), cf. 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 72.9; ὁ τ. τῶν πτερῶν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.1}}: _absolutely_, **wing,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anacreont. 9, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.287 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus), etc.; in Prose, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.63}}: of a peacock's **tail**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.60}}; ταρσοί **feathers**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.50.}}
**II.4** τ. ὀδόντων the **row** of teeth in a saw, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.202.}}
**II.5** **Pan's pipe**, ταρσῷ Πὰν ὁ μελιζόμενος {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 781.10 (from Cnidus).}}
**II.6** **edge of the eyelid and its lashes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 23, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.69, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 10.7.}}

5020:
** Ταρτᾰρ-όω**,
**cast into Tartarus** or **hell**, {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Acusilaus Historicus 8 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, NT.2Pet.2.4, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 4.158 (_passive_), Scholia T 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.296.}}
** Τάρτᾰρ-ος**, ὁ,
  also ἡ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.15, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 203: irregular _plural_ Τάρταρα, τά, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 119, 841, etc. (_sing._ Τάρταρον, τό, 5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius, Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.312):— “Tartarus”, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.13, 481, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 807, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 336, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 256, 374, etc. (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”)}}; later, **the nether world** generally, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 255}}; ἀπέραντος, κελαινός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 154 (Lyric poetry), 1051 (_anapaest meter_)}}; Ταρτάρου μελαμβαθὴς κευθμών{{LXX|[prev. work] 221; σκότον νέμονται Τάρταρόν θ᾽ ὑπὸ χθονός [prev. author] “Eu.” 72, cf. LXX.Job.40.15, 41.23.}}
**II** personified as husband of Gaia and father of Typhoeus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 822}}; ὦ Γᾶς παῖ καὶ Ταρτάρου, of Cerberus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1574 (Lyric poetry).}}

5021:
** τάσσω**,
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 332, etc.}}; _Attic dialect_ **τάττω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 262e, etc.}}: _future_ τάξω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 285, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔταξα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 986, etc.}}: _perfect_ τέτᾰχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.5, (συν-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 625c}}: _pluperfect_ ἐτετάχει {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.65.7:}} —_middle_, _future_ τάξομαι{{LXX|(in _passive_ sense) LXX.Exo.29.43}}: _aorist_ ἐταξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.83, etc.}}:— _passive_, _future_ ταχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.41, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140, etc.}}; later τᾰγήσομαι (ἐν-) {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.1, (ὑπο-) NT.1Cor.15.28; 3f ut. τετάξομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1046, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 637}}: _aorist_ ἐτάχθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 279, etc.}}; later ἐτάγην [ᾰ] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 708.9 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.965e, Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.25.50, etc.}}: _perfect_ τέταγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.30, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τετάχαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13, (ἀντι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.5}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ ἐτετάχατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.6, 7.4}}:—**draw up in order of battle, form, array, marshal**, both of troops and ships, τὴν στρατιήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.191; τοὺς ὁπλίτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9; νεῶν στῖφος ἐν στίχοις τρισίν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 366; πολεμίων στίχας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 676; τ. εἰς μάχην στρατιάν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.47}}:—_passive_, **to be drawn up**, ἐς μάχην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; οὐδένα κόσμον ταχθέντες [prev. author] 9.69}}; ἐπὶ τεττάρων ταχθῆναι in four lines, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.15}}; ἐπὶ μιᾶς, of ships, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 1.6.29; ἐπὶ κέρως 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 67.4}}; κατὰ μίαν ναῦν τεταγμένοι in line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84}}; ἐπὶ ὀκτώ, of troops, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.67: _absolutely_, τεταγμένοι}} **in rank and file**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.81 (so _metaphorically_, τὸ ἐν τῷ τεταγμένῳ ὄν}} **the rank and file**, opposed to Senators and Equites, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 49.12)}}; στράτευμα τεταγμένον, opposed to ἄτακτον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.7}}:—_middle_, **fall in, form in order of battle**, frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, 1.48, 4.11, etc.; ὡς ἐς μάχην 2.20}}; ἐτάξαντο κύκλον τῶν νεῶν **formed** in a circle, {{Refs|[prev. work] 83, compare 3.78; ἐτάξαντο οὐ πάντες ὁμοίως 5.68; εἴκοσι ναυσὶ ἐτάξαντο 3.77 (but in 2.90 _transitive_, ἐπὶ τεσσάρων ταξάμενοι τὰς ναῦς}} **having drawn up their** ships in four lines,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 664).}}
**2** **post, station**, τὰς καμήλους ἀντία τῆς ἵππου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 749}}; τινὰς ἐπί τινας one group against another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.9 (but τ. τινὰ ἐπὶ τοὺς ἱππέας}} **set** him over them, to command them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 3.4.20)}}; ἑαυτὸν ὑμῖν τάξαι παρέσχεν for enrolment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.9, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 43}}:—_passive_, **to be posted** or **stationed**, τῇ οὐδεὶς ἐτέτακτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 381; ἐς τὸ ὄρος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.212; but ἐς τὸ πεζόν or ἐς π. τετάχθαι to serve among the infantry, [prev. work] 21, 81; ἐς τὸν ναυτικὸν στρατὸν -θέντες [prev. work] 203: with _genitive_, τῆς πρώτης τάξεως (or simply τῆς πρώτης) τεταγμένος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.11, 16.15: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τάξιν τινὰ ταχθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a, etc.; δεξιὸν τεταγμένους κέρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 657: frequently followed by Preps. (compare below 11.1, etc.), ταχθῆναι or τετάχθαι ἐπί τινα}} or τινας **against** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.78, etc.}}; ἐπί τινι or τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 448, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70, 3.13, etc.}}; also, **to be posted** at a place, ἐφ᾽ ἑπτὰ πύλαις ταχθέντες ἴσοι πρὸς ἴσους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 142 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἐπ᾽ εὐωνύμῳ κέρατι **on** the left wing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.19; ἐπὶ τοῦ λαιοῦ κέρως 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.34.4}}; τ. κατά τινα **over against.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.19}}; τ. μετά τινα **behind** him.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 7.2.4 (so ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Lac.” 13.7)}}; μετά τινος **with** him, by his side, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.67.2, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63; σύν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.17, etc.; παρὰ τὸν ποταμόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.15}}; περὶ τὸ Ἥραιον{{Refs|[prev. work] 69; ἀμφὶ τὴν Κέον [prev. author] 8.76.}}
**II** **appoint** to any service, military or civil, the latter being _metaphorically_ from the former, ἄρχοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.24}}; τινὰ ἐπί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.6.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.20, etc.; ἐπὶ τὰς πράξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.151, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209b, etc.; ἀξιῶ σε τάξαι με ἐπί τινος “PCair.Zen.” 447.3 (3rd c.BC): also τ. ἑαυτὸν ἐπί τι}} **undertake** a task, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.71, etc.; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.6}}:—_passive_, οἱ τεταγμένοι βραβῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 709, compare 759; πρέσβεις ταχθέντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.69}}; τετάχθαι ἐπί τινι **to be appointed** to a service, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.191, 2.38, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 298, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1040, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.1; ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 637, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.24, etc.; also ἐπί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109 (ἐπ᾽ οὗ, variant{ὅκου}), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.46; τὸν ἐπὶ τῆς σφαγῆς τεταγμένον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 38, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.12.5}}]; ὁ πρὸς τοῖς γράμμασι τεταγμένος **secretary**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.27.7}}; οἱ πρὸς ταῖς φυλακαῖς (tolls) τετ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 31.15 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **appoint** or **order** one to do or be, τάττετ᾽ ἐμὲ ἡγεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.25, compare “Cyr.” 7.3.1, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 639, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 223, etc.}}:—_passive_, μοῖρα ἡ ταχθεῖσα.. φρουρέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.133, compare 8.13, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 279, 639, etc.; τασσόμενος πορεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.11, etc.}}; τοῦτο τετάγμεθα (i.e. ποιεῖν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 49; also τεταγμένος κίοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 504}}; ὁ ἐπ᾽ Αἴγυπτον ταχθείς (i.e. κῆρυξ) **ordered** to Egypt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62, compare 68, 6.48.}}
**II.3** also τ. τινί with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.124, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.5, etc.: _impersonal_, ἴωμεν.., ἵν᾽ ἡμῖν τέτακται (i.e. ἰέναι) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1181 (Lyric poetry); οἷς ἐτέτακτο παραβοηθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22; τοῖς δὲ ἕπεσθαι τέτακται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 11.6: also with}}_infinitive_ omitted, κόσμον φυλάσσουσ᾽ ὅντιν᾽ ἂν τάξῃ πόλις (i.e. φυλάσσειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 245, compare 460, “Hel.” 1390, etc.}}
**II.4** **assign to** a duty or class of dutiful persons, ἐν πᾶσιν ἐμαυτὸν ἔταττον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.221}}; εἰς ὑπηρετικὴν αὑτοὺς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289e; πρός τινας τάξαι αὑτόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.18; σὺν ἐμοὶ τ. σεαυτήν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.47; τ. ἐμαυτὸν εἰς τάξιν τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8; τινὰς εἰς τοὺς ἀρχικούς [prev. work] 7; εἰς τὴν δουλείαν ἐμαυτόν [prev. work] 11}}; τ. ἑαυτόν τινων εἶναι **range** oneself with.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.302:}} —_passive_, πρὸς τὴν ξυμμαχίαν ταχθῆναι **to join** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.86.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _of things_, **place in a certain order** or **relative position**, χωρὶς ἑκάτερα τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36}}; τίνα μέσον τάξω λόγο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 908}}; πρῶτον καὶ τελευταῖον τὸ κάλλιστον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.9; τὰ τυφλὰ τοῦ σώματος καὶ ἄοπλα ἐναντία τάττειν τοῖς πολεμίοις [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.45; τοὺς πόδας [τοῦ ἐμβρύου] κατ᾽ εὐθὺ τοῦ στομίου τῆς ὑστέρας τάσσειν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.60; μὴ κατὰ ἄνεμον τῶν οἰκημάτων τάττειν τὴν ἅλω “Geoponica” 2.26.1; τάξας.. ἀπὸ μὲν δύσεως μίαν θυρίδα φωτὸς ἕνεκεν [prev. work]14.6.6; [κηρίας] τὴν μεσότητα τάσσειν ὑπὸ τὸ γένειον “PMed.Lond.” 155ii 29, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 25. +others; εἰς ταὐτὸ τ. τὴν εὐτυχίαν τῇ εὐδαιμονίᾳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1099b7}}; Λυδοὺς.. πρὸς ἅπαντας **range** over against, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262e; τὴν σοφιστικὴν περὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔταξεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1026b15, compare “Top.” 125b21}}; with _infinitive_, [Ὅμηρον] ἐν τοῖς.. σοφωτάτοις εἶναι τάττομεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.142; οὐκ εὐλόγως τὸ τοιοῦτον σημεῖον ἐν τοῖς φρενιτικοῖς τάττει 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.521, compare 18(2).238}}; τ. τι ἐπί τινος **apply** a term to a certain sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.21a}}:—_passive_, τετάχθαι κατά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.48; ἔμπροσθεν τ. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 631d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.7, etc.}}
**III.b** with an _infinitive_ and _adjective_, **lay down, rule** to be so and so, ἅπερ ἂν.. αἰσχρὰ εἶναι καὶ κακὰ τάττῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 728a; τά τε δίκαια ταχθέντ᾽ εἶναι καὶ ἄδικα [prev. author] “Plt.” 305b.}}
**III.2** **ordain, prescribe**, τ. τὰ περὶ τὰ τέκνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262b6: _absolutely_, ὁ νόμος οὕτω τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 199a; οὕτω τ. ὁ λόγος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119b17}}:—_passive_, τὸ ταττόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 766; τὸ ταχθὲν τελεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 528; τὰ τεταγμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.5, etc.}}; τὰ τετ. ἄγειν the things **appointed** to them for conveying,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8.5.4; τῆς τροφῆς ἡ βελτίστη τέτακται τοῖς ἐλευθέροις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 744b18}}; ἐν τῷ τεταγμένῳ εἶναι to be fulfilling **one's obligations,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.57.47, 22.116.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.37.}}
**III.3** of taxes or payments, **assess**, τὸν φόρον ταῖς πόλεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.11, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.23, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.209; ταῖσδε ἔταξαν οἱ τάκται “IG” 12.218.45; so τ. τῷ ναύτῃ δραχμήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.4}}: with _infinitive_ added, χρήματα τοῖς πᾶσι τάξαντες φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.19, etc.}} (_passive_, φόρον ἐτάχθησαν φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.97)}}; τάσσειν ἀργυρίου πολλοῦ **fix** a high price, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}:—_passive_, τὸ ταχθὲν τίμημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551b; εἰσφέρειν τὸ τεταγμένον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a14}}:—_middle_, **take a payment on oneself**, i.e. **agree to pay** it, φόρον τάξασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13, 4.35; χρήματα ἀποδοῦναι ταξάμενοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.101}}; χρήματα ταξάμενοι κατὰ χρόνους ἀποδοῦναι **agreeing** to pay by instalments, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 117, compare 3.70; πόλεις αὐταὶ ταξάμεναι “IG” 12.212.72, compare 211 vi 6; also τάξασθαι ἐς τὴν δωρεήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.97 (but also, much like _active_, ἐτάξατο φόρους οἱ προσιέναι [prev. work] 89).}}
**III.3.b** _middle_, generally, **agree upon, settle**, ταξαμένους.. δέχεσθαι μισθὸν τῆς φυλακῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416e; τὰς τιμάς [prev. author] “Lg.” 743e, compare 844b, 844c, +others}}; τέταγμαι ποιμέσιν, οἵ μοι δώσουσιν τιμήν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 56.19 (3rd c.BC)}}; **votum** explained as εὐχή, ὃ τάττεταί τις θεῷ, {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}: with _infinitive_ _future_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 54.5 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.7.7, +others}}
**III.3.c** _middle_, **pay**, τῆς δὲ τιμῆς τάξονται παραχρῆμα τὸ δ μέρος, τὸ δὲ λοιπὸν ἐν ἔτεσι γ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 14.18 (3rd c.BC),compare “PEnteux.” 60.9, 89.7, “PMich.Zen.” 79.9, “PCair.Zen.” 649.16 (all 3rd c.BC), “PAmh.” 2.31.1, 52.1, “Ostr.Bodl.” i 46, 96, +others, “PLond.” 3.1201.1, 1202.1 (all 2nd c.BC).}}
**III.4** **impose** punishments, τ. δίκην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1420, etc.}}; τ. ζημίας, τιμωρίας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 876c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.143; τῷ κλέψαντι θάνατον 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 65}}:— also in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65.}}
**III.4.b** **impose** laws, οὓς [νόμους] ἔταξε αὐτοῖς ὁ νομοθέτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 772c.}}
**III.5** in _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, **fixed, settled, prescribed**, ὁ τεταγμένος χρόνος (like τακτός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41, etc.; ὥρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 723; ἡμέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.4; ἔτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 810b; ἡ τετ. χώρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.40}}; αἱ τετ. θυσίαι the **regular** offerings, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 3.3.4}}; οἱ ἐπὶ τούτῳ τετ. [νόμοι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 50d}}; ἡ τετ. δίαιτα **prescribed**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 404a}}; τὰ τετ. ὀνόματα **received**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.9}}; τετ. τέχνη **regular**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 13.12}}; τεταγμένον, opposed to ἄτακτον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 280a8; νὺξ τὰ τεταγμέν᾽ ἀπέχει “Lyric poetry Alex. Adesp.” 37.6}}; of _geometrical_ figures, **regular**, i.e. **equilateral and equiangular**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 306.2, 8, +others--compare τεταγμένως.}}

5022:
** ταῦρος**, ὁ,
**bull**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.481, etc.}}; as a sacrifice to Poseidon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.728, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.181; also ταῦρος βοῦς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.389}}; ἄπεχε τῆς βοὸς τὸν τ., oracularly of Agamemnon and his wife, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1126 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **priest of Poseidon Taureios**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.425c.}}
**3** a kind of Lycian boat with bull as figurehead, postulated to explain Europa's bull, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.83.}}
**II** **the bull as a sign of the Zodiac**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 167, +others, “IG” 14.1307, “Supp.epigram” 7.363.8 (Dura-Europus, 2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**III** ={κοχώνη}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.173, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.706}}: also **the pudenda muliebria**, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}: **the male** αἰδοῖον, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} (Cf. Latin _taurus_, Lithuanian _tauras_, Slavonic _tur[ucaron]_, etc.)

5023:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5024:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5025:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5026:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5027:
** ταφ-ή**, ἡ,
(see. θάπτω) **burial**, ταφῆς τυχεῖν, κυρῆσαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 112}}: in _plural_, **mode of burial**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.85, 5.8}}: _plural_ also of the **burials** of those who had fallen in battle, δημοσίᾳ ταφὰς ἐποιήσαντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; νόμοι.., οἷς ἐχρῶντο περὶ τὰς ταφάς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 52, compare “OGI” 90.32 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC).}}
**2** _plural_ also, **burial-place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71, 5.63, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1090, 1109}}: later in _singular_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 6028.2, +others (1st c.BC): but σῆς εἰ στερήσομαι ταφῆς}}, of the **urn** supposed to contain the ashes of Orestes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1210}}; so in Egypt, **mummy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 736.13 (1st c.AD), Wilcken “Chr.” 499 (2nd-3rd c.AD), etc.}}; also, **sarcophagus**, δευτέρα τ., of a double sarcophagus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 68.7 (2nd c.AD), “Arch.Papyrus” 4.133 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** **payment for burial, burial-fee**, τὸν τὴν τ. τοῦ πατρὸς οὐκ ἀπειληφότα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.58; ἐνδεεῖς γενόμενοι εἰς τὴν τ. τὴν Φιλίππου “PEnteux.” 32.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὑπὲρ τέλους ταφῆς μιᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Ostr.Bodl.” ii 45 (2nd c.AD).}}

5028:
** τάφος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
(θάπτω)
**A** **funeral-rites**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.619, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.547}}; δαινύναι τάφον to give a **funeral-feast**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.29, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.309}}; τελέσαι τάφον Ἕκτορι δίῳ to perform **the funeral-rites**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.660}}; so ὃν πόλις στυγεῖ, σὺ τιμήσεις τάφ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1051; τάφῳ κτερίζειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 203; τάφον τινὸς θέσθαι [prev. author] “OT” 1447; τ. περιστέλλειν νεκροῦ [prev. author] “Aj.” 1170}}; τάφου τυχεῖν obtain **the rites of burial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 47; τοιόσδε ὁ τ. ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47}}: _plural_ of a single funeral, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414a}}; so of cremation, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Clitarchus Gnomologus 32 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**A.2** **the act of performing the funeral-rites**, τοῦδε τοῦ τ. φήσεις μετασχεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 534.}}
**A.II** **grave, tomb**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 477, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).63, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 686, “Ch.” 168, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1218f, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.136, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, etc. (never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _plural_ of a single grave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 411}}; ὄντες ἐν τάφοις **dead and buried**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 767}}; μέγας γ᾽ ὀφθαλμὸς οἱ πατρὸς τάφοι **his being dead and buried**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 987:— γῦπες ἔμψυχοι τάφοι 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 5a.}}
**A.II.2** ἔμψυχός τις τ. a 'living skeleton', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 6.2.}}
**A.II.3** ={βωμός}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 34 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**A.II.4** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Cypria for φόνος, Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.29.}}

5029:
** τάχᾰ**,
_adverb_, (τᾰχύς) **quickly, presently, forthwith**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who, like 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (“O.” 2.29, 4.83, +others) and 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus (5.89)}}, uses it only of time, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.205, 2.193, +others}}; ἦ τ. **soon** i'faith, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.73, 338}}; of past time, τ. δ᾽ Ἕκτορος ἄγχι γένοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.117: also in Trag.and (more rarely)}} _Attic dialect_Prose and Comedy texts, with _future_, τάχ᾽ εἴσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 261, compare “Ag.” 489, 1649, “Ch.” 305, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 84, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 527, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 247d, etc.}}; ἔοικα θεσπιῳδήσειν τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1161}}; πέμψον πρὸς ἐμὲ τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7356.25 (2nd c.AD)}}; in this sense not found in {{Refs|LXX or in Papyri (except [same place])}}; τάχα ἐπειδάν, ={ἐπειδὰν τάχιστα}, **as soon as**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a.}}
**II** **perhaps**, to express any contingency from a probability to bare possibility, δὶς μὲν γὰρ καὶ τρὶς τ. τεύξεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 401; τ. οὐδὲ τεθέασθε τυραννουμένην πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a, compare “Hp. Ma.” 303b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.17, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.61, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 4.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.685,690: more frequently τάχ᾽ ἄν}}, **probably, perhaps**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70, +others}}, frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_; mostly with _optative_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 314, “Eu.” 512 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 139, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, etc.}}; rarely with _aorist_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256c, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.596}}; with _imperfect_ _indicative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.55}}; with _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 523, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.2}}; with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 10}}; τάχ᾽ ἄν alone, in answers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 255c, “R.” 369a, etc.: strengthened, τάχα.. ἴσως 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 718}}; ἴσως τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.24; τ. τοίνυν ἴσως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.191; τάχ᾽ ἂν ἴσως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 264c; τάχ᾽ ἴσως ἄν [prev. author] “Sph.” 247d; ἴσως τάχ᾽ ἄν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 691, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 38e}}; ἀμφισβητοῦντες προστιθέασιν ἀεὶ τὸ ἴσως καὶ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389b19}}; in this sense only twice in {{LXX|LXX (LXX.Wis.13.6, 14.19).}}
**III** _superlative_ τάχιστα, see at {ταχύς} with {{Refs|11.}}
**IV** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus formed an _accusative_ _plural_ τάχας}}, **perhaps-es, ** “fragment” 869.

5030:
Included with: ** τᾰχύς** [ῠ], εῖα, ύ:
**I** of motion, **swift, fleet**, opposed to βραδύς,
**I.1** of persons and animals, either _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.69, etc.}}; or more fully, πόδας ταχύς {{Refs|13.249, 482, 17.709, etc.}}; ταχὺς ἔσκε θέειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.308}}; θείειν τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.186, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.112}}; κύνες, ἔλαφος, πτώξ, ἵππος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.26, 8.248, 17.676, 23.347, etc.; οἰωνόν, ταχὺν ἄγγελον 24.292, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.526}}; τ. βαδιστής a **quick** walker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1182}}; σφοδροὶ καὶ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.31.}}
**I.2** of things, τ. πόδες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.514, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.261, etc.}}; τ. ἰός, ὀϊστοί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.94, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.3, etc.; πτερά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1453; -ύτατα ἅρματα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.77}}; νῆες, τριήρεις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.23, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.43, etc.; [ἴχνος] τὸ τοῦ ποδὸς μὲν βραδύ, τὸ τοῦ δὲ νοῦ ταχύ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 742.}}
**II** of thought and purpose, **quick, hasty**, φρονεῖν γὰρ οἱ ταχεῖς οὐκ ἀσφαλεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 617: with}}_infinitive_, βλάπτειν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1428; τ. βουλεῦσαί τι ἀνήκεστον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, compare 118, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 12; also τ. πρὸς ὀργήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 1}}; τὸ ταχύ **speed, haste**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 452, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.18, etc.}}
**II.2** of actions, events, etc., **rapid, sudden**, πήδημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 833}}; ᾅδης, μόρος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1047, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.26; πόλεμος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, 6.45; φυγή [prev. author] 4.44; μεταβολή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553d}}; **short**, τ. ἐλπίδες **fleeting** hopes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.83; ἐπαυρέσεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53; ἀτραπός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 127; ταχεῖ σὺν χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1602}}; τ. διήγησις **short, rapid**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1416b30.}}
**B** _adverb_,
**B.1** regular τᾰχέως, **quickly**, opposed to βραδέως, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.365, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 103, etc.}}:—rarely in sense **perhaps** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|compare (τάχα 11), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.25.8.}}
**B.2** the _adverb_ is also expressed by _periphrastic_, διὰ ταχέων **in haste**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, 3.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.9; ἐκ ταχείας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 395; compare τάχος 11.}}
**B.3** _neuter_ ταχύ as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.51, “N.” 1.51, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 349, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 885 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 109, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.665, etc.}}; ἤδη ἤδη τ. τ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 4321.21, “BGU” 956.3 (both 3rd c.AD)}}; ἄρτι ἄρτι τ. τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Arch.Papyrus” 5.393 (2nd c.AD)}}; also τάχα (which see).
**B.4** the _adjective_ ταχύς is frequently construed with Verbs, where we should use the _adverb_, ταχέες δ᾽ ἱππῆες ἄγερθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.287; ταχεῖά γ᾽ ἦλθε χρησμῶν πρᾶξις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 739; ὁρμάσθω ταχύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 526}}; δεῦρ᾽ ἀφίξεται τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 307; τ. χάρις διαρρεῖ [prev. author] “Aj.” 1266, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75, 5.66.}}
**C** Degrees of Comparison:
**C.I** _comparative_:
**C.I.1** the form **τᾰχύτερος**, α, ον, is used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἐποίησα ταχύτερα ἢ σοφώτερα 3.65, compare 7.194}}; also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394b3, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 9.6, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.16}}, but not in good _Attic dialect_; ταχύτερον as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127, 9.101, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 17.}}
**C.I.2** the more usual form is **θάσσων**, _neuter_ θᾶσσον, _genitive_ ονος, _Attic dialect_ **θάττων**, _neuter_ θᾶττον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.570, 13.819 (elsewhere only _neuter_ in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}, etc.:—_neuter_ as _adverb_, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.307, +others}}; θᾶσσον ἂν.. κλύοιμι **sooner**, i.e. **rather**, would I hear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 631}}; θᾶσσον also often stands for the Positive, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.440, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.201, 16.130, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.181, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 506, “V.” 187, “Ra.” 94}}; οὐ θᾶσσον οἴσει; i.e. make haste and bring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1183, compare “OT” 430}}; θᾶττον νοήματος **quicker** than thought, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 824, etc.}}; with a _conjunction_, ὅτι θᾶσσον, like{ὅτι τάχιστα}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.48}}; ἐπειδὴ θᾶττον συνεσκότασεν **as soon as.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.5; ἐπειδὰν θ. συνιῇ τις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325c; ὅταν θ. φθέγγηται ὁ κόκκυξ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 563b17, compare 611a5}}; ἐὰν or ἢν θ. **as soon as.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.20, “An.” 6.5.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.105a}}; ἂν θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 174}}; εἰ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 324b}}; ὡς θ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.66.1, 3.82.1}}; θ. rarely = **sooner than, before**, ἐξήλαυνον μεσημβρίας οὐ πολλῷ τινι θ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 51 (27). 13 (compare τάχιον below 3).}}
**C.I.3** the form **ταχίων** [ῑ], _neuter_ ιον, is frequently in late Prose, as {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Wis.13.9, LXX.1Mac.2.40, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 69.14, 17, 73.23, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 1.20, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.42, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.6, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (see. below), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.240d, NT.John.20.4, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.4; also in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 402.16}}; but condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 58, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Philet.” p.436 P.}}; τὴν ταχίονα τῆς τροφῆς παράθεσιν **earlier, sooner**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.206:}} — _adverb_ τάχιον **earlier**, πλέεται.. περὶ τὸν Σεπτέμβριον μῆνα.., οὐδὲν δὲ κωλύει κἂν τ. {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 24}}; τ. τῆς ὑποσχέσεως **sooner** than they had promised, {{Refs|“Rev.Ét.Gr.” 6.159 (from Iasus)}}; τ. τοῦ παραγγέλματος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.4.2; εἰς μακρὸν αὐτῶν γῆρας καὶ βίου μῆκος ὅμοιον τοῖς τ. ἐπερχομένων [prev. author] “AJ” 1.3.7; ἀποπαύεται οὔτε τ. ἐτῶν τεσσαράκοντα οὔτε βράδιον ἐτῶν πεντήκοντα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.20, compare 48, +others}}; **formerly**, ἐπεσκεύασαν τὸ παρόχιον,.. τ. γενόμενον γυμνάσιον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 3.639 (Lycia, 2nd c.AD), compare 4.1517 (from Sardis), 1632.14 (from Philadelphia), 1665.5 (from Tira), Keil-Premerstein “Dritter Bericht“ p.79 (3rd c.AD), “Hermes” 63.229 (from Callatis)}}; compare above 2 near the end
**C.II** _superlative_:
**C.II.1** the form **ταχύτατος** is rare, ταχύτατα ἅρματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.77}}; ταχύτατα as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.27 codices, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 87 codices}}; but both passages have been corrected.
**C.II.2** the usual form is **τάχιστος**, η, ον, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _neuter_ _plural_ τάχιστα}} as _adverb_, **most quickly, most speedily**, ὅττι τάχιστα **as soon as may be, as soon as possible**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.193, 9.659, +others; ὅτι τάχιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1341 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.31, etc.}}; so ὅσον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 772, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1436, etc.}}; ᾳ (probably) τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.79}}; ὅπως τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 605, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1410, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 167}}; ὡς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.76.23, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.210, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.15, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 147, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14}}: these are elliptically phrases, as may be seen from the following examples, ὡς δυνατόν ἐστι τάχιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 710b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.3}}; ᾗ δυνατὸν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 6.3.6}}; ὡς or ᾗ ἠδύνατο τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.14, “An.” 1.2.4}}; ὡς δύναιτο τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79}}; ὡς or ᾗ ἂν δύνωμαι τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.38, “Cyr.” 7.1.9, compare “IG” 12.106.18.}}
**C.II.2.b** τάχιστα after Particles of Time, **as soon as**, ἐπεὶ (_Ionic dialect_ ἐπεί τε) τάχιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 201, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, 75, 7.163, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.6, “PCair.Zen.” 34.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπειδὴ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.16, etc.}}; ἐπεὰν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, 7.129, 8.144}}; ἐπὰν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.9}}; ἐπειδὰν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.14, “An.” 3.1.9}}; ὅταν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.5.33: also ὡς τ}}. separated by one or more words, ὡς ἡμέρη τ. ἐγεγόνεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, compare 19, 47, 65, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2, “Mem.” 1.2.16, +others; ὡς δὲ τ. ἐξῆλθε.. κόρον ἔτεκε “IG” 42(1).121.4 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ὡς γὰρ τ. εἰσῆλθον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 287; ὡς ἂν τ. λάβῃς τὴν ἐπιστολήν “PCair.Zen.” 241.1 (3rd c.BC); but ὡς τ. γὰρ ἀπεδήμησας [prev. work] 472.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὅπως τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 230:}} —the same notion is sometimes expressed by the _participle_, ἀπαλλαγεὶς τάχιστα, ={ὡς ἀπηλλάγη τ}., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 8, compare 25.}}
**C.II.3** frequently also in Prose, τὴν ταχίστην (in full, τὴν τ. ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.20, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 4)}} as _adverb_, **by the quickest way**, i.e. **most quickly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 73, 81, 86, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 75, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.4, etc. (Cf. Lith. (dialogue)}} **deñgti**, Lett. **diêgt**, both = 'run quickly', Polish **dążyć** 'hurry'.)

