**C.I.3** the form **ταχίων** [ῑ], _neuter_ ιον, is frequently in late Prose, as {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Wis.13.9, LXX.1Mac.2.40, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 69.14, 17, 73.23, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 1.20, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.42, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.6, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (see. below), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.240d, NT.John.20.4, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.4; also in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 402.16}}; but condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 58, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Philet.” p.436 P.}}; τὴν ταχίονα τῆς τροφῆς παράθεσιν **earlier, sooner**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.206:}} — _adverb_ τάχιον **earlier**, πλέεται.. περὶ τὸν Σεπτέμβριον μῆνα.., οὐδὲν δὲ κωλύει κἂν τ. {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 24}}; τ. τῆς ὑποσχέσεως **sooner** than they had promised, {{Refs|“Rev.Ét.Gr.” 6.159 (from Iasus)}}; τ. τοῦ παραγγέλματος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.4.2; εἰς μακρὸν αὐτῶν γῆρας καὶ βίου μῆκος ὅμοιον τοῖς τ. ἐπερχομένων [prev. author] “AJ” 1.3.7; ἀποπαύεται οὔτε τ. ἐτῶν τεσσαράκοντα οὔτε βράδιον ἐτῶν πεντήκοντα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.20, compare 48, +others}}; **formerly**, ἐπεσκεύασαν τὸ παρόχιον,.. τ. γενόμενον γυμνάσιον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 3.639 (Lycia, 2nd c.AD), compare 4.1517 (from Sardis), 1632.14 (from Philadelphia), 1665.5 (from Tira), Keil-Premerstein “Dritter Bericht“ p.79 (3rd c.AD), “Hermes” 63.229 (from Callatis)}}; compare above 2 near the end
**C.II** _superlative_:
**C.II.1** the form **ταχύτατος** is rare, ταχύτατα ἅρματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.77}}; ταχύτατα as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.27 codices, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 87 codices}}; but both passages have been corrected.
**C.II.2** the usual form is **τάχιστος**, η, ον, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _neuter_ _plural_ τάχιστα}} as _adverb_, **most quickly, most speedily**, ὅττι τάχιστα **as soon as may be, as soon as possible**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.193, 9.659, +others; ὅτι τάχιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1341 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.31, etc.}}; so ὅσον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 772, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1436, etc.}}; ᾳ (probably) τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.79}}; ὅπως τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 605, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1410, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 167}}; ὡς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.76.23, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.210, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.15, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 147, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14}}: these are elliptically phrases, as may be seen from the following examples, ὡς δυνατόν ἐστι τάχιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 710b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.3}}; ᾗ δυνατὸν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 6.3.6}}; ὡς or ᾗ ἠδύνατο τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.14, “An.” 1.2.4}}; ὡς δύναιτο τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79}}; ὡς or ᾗ ἂν δύνωμαι τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.38, “Cyr.” 7.1.9, compare “IG” 12.106.18.}}
**C.II.2.b** τάχιστα after Particles of Time, **as soon as**, ἐπεὶ (_Ionic dialect_ ἐπεί τε) τάχιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 201, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, 75, 7.163, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.6, “PCair.Zen.” 34.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπειδὴ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.16, etc.}}; ἐπεὰν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, 7.129, 8.144}}; ἐπὰν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.9}}; ἐπειδὰν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.14, “An.” 3.1.9}}; ὅταν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.5.33: also ὡς τ}}. separated by one or more words, ὡς ἡμέρη τ. ἐγεγόνεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, compare 19, 47, 65, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2, “Mem.” 1.2.16, +others; ὡς δὲ τ. ἐξῆλθε.. κόρον ἔτεκε “IG” 42(1).121.4 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ὡς γὰρ τ. εἰσῆλθον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 287; ὡς ἂν τ. λάβῃς τὴν ἐπιστολήν “PCair.Zen.” 241.1 (3rd c.BC); but ὡς τ. γὰρ ἀπεδήμησας [prev. work] 472.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὅπως τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 230:}} —the same notion is sometimes expressed by the _participle_, ἀπαλλαγεὶς τάχιστα, ={ὡς ἀπηλλάγη τ}., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 8, compare 25.}}
**C.II.3** frequently also in Prose, τὴν ταχίστην (in full, τὴν τ. ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.20, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 4)}} as _adverb_, **by the quickest way**, i.e. **most quickly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 73, 81, 86, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 75, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.4, etc. (Cf. Lith. (dialogue)}} **deñgti**, Lett. **diêgt**, both = 'run quickly', Polish **dążyć** 'hurry'.)

5037:
** τε**,
enclitic Particle, with two main uses (see. infr. A, B).
**A** as a Conjunction,
**A.I** τε.. τε, **both.. and**, joining single words, phrases, clauses, or sentences, the first τε merely pointing forward to the second, ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.544; ἀγαθῶν τε κακῶν τε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 669; δίψῃ τε λιμῷ τε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 491, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 34, 35, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 370, 375; τήν τε νῆσον τήν τε ἤπειρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373b; λυσόμενός τε θύγατρα, φέρων τ᾽ ἀπερείσι᾽ ἄποινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.13; παῖδά τε σοὶ ἀγέμεν Φοίβῳ θ᾽ ἱερὴν ἑκατόμβην ῥέξαι [prev. work] 443}}; the elements joined by τε.. τε are usually short in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, longer in later Gr., e.g. ἐπειδὴ πρόξενοί τέ εἰσιν Ἀθηναίων καὶ εὐεργέται.., ἔν τε τῇ στήλῃ γέγραπται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.103.7; ἥ τε γὰρ γῆ.. εὔυδρός ἐστι, ποταμοί τε δι᾽ αὐτῆς ῥέουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.47}}; χρὴ.. τούς τε πρεσβυτέρους ὁμοιωθῆναι τοῖς πρὶν ἔργοις, τούς τε νεωτέρους.. μὴ αἰσχῦναι κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.25, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.50}}; τά τε γὰρ ληφθέντα πάντ᾽ ἂν σῴζοιτο οἵ τ᾽ ἀδικήσαντες κατ᾽ ἀξίαν λάβοιεν τὰ ἐπιτίμια Aen.{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Tact. 16.8, compare “Geoponica” 2.49.1, 12.3.2-3; τούτου γὰρ γενομένου.. τά τε ἐχφόρια Χρυσέρμῳ δυνήσομαι ἀποδοῦναι, ἐγώ τε ἔσομαι παρὰ σοῦ φιλανθρωπίας τετευχώς “PEnteux.” 60.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; κλείειν τε τὰ βλέφαρα δεομένων ἐλπιζόντων τε κοιμηθήσεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.494}}, compare 495,501; this use is common at all times in οὔτε.. οὔτε, μήτε.. μήτε, εἴτε.. εἴτε (which see); τε may be used three or more times, ἔν τ᾽ ἄρα οἱ φῦ χειρί, ἔπος τ᾽ ἔφατ᾽ ἔκ τ᾽ ὀνόμαζεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.530, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.177, 2.58, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 89s q., 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.19s q., 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.36:}} —ἑνδεκάτη τε δυωδεκάτη τε probably means **the eleventh or twelfth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.374, 4.588:}} —sometimes τε.. τε couples alternatives, ἀπόρως εἶχε δοῦναί τε μὴ δοῦναί τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 56, compare “Heracl.” 153, “El.” 391}}; hence we find τε.. ἢ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143c, “Ion” 535d}}; on ἢ (or ἦ).. τε in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.289 and 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 524 (Lyric poetry) see at {ἦ} I.3.}}]
**A.I.2** the first clause may be negative, the second affirmative, as ἐκκλησίαν τε οὐκ ἐποίει.., τήν τε πόλιν ἐφύλασσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.22}}; but οὔτε.. τε is more frequently, as οὔτε ποσίν εἰμι ταχύς.., γιγνώσκω τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.6 (see. οὔτε 11.4)}}; we also find οὐ.. τε.., as οὐχ ἡσύχαζον.., παρεκάλουν τε τοὺς ξυμμάχους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.67}}; and μὴ.. τε.., as ἵνα μή τι διαφύγῃ ἡμᾶς, εἴ τέ τι βούλει κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95e.}}
**A.I.3** τε (**both**) sometimes corresponds to a following δέ (**and**), or τε (**and**) to a preceding μέν, e.g.
**A.I.3.a** τε.. δὲ.., as κόμισαί τέ με, δὸς δέ μοι ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.359, compare 7.418, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 367, “Tr.” 285, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1625; ἐσθὰς ἀμφότερόν νιν ἔχεν, ἅ τε.. ἐπιχώριος.., ἀμφὶ δὲ παρδαλέᾳ στέγετο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.80; διήκουέ τε.., ἔπειτα δὲ καὶ ἐπῄνεσε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.4.3}}; so with ἅμα δὲ καὶ.., ὡσαύτως δὲ καὶ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186e:—so τε.., ἀτὰρ οὖν καὶ.., [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 295e.}}
**A.I.3.b** μὲν.. τε.., ἄνδρα μὲν.., τρεῖς τε κασιγνήτους [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.291 - 3, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.475 - 6, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.88, 7.88, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 924, “Ch.” 585 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 963 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 337 codices, “Cyc.” 41 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 563 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 266c, “Lg.” 927b: see.{μέν} 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.6c.}}]
**A.I.4** a single τε (**and**) joins a word, phrase, or (especially later) clause or sentence to what precedes, τελευτὴν κεφαλήν τε [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69a; θνητὰ ἀθάνατά τε [prev. work]c; Ζεῦ ἄλλοι τε θεοί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.476; κύνεσσιν οἰωνοῖσί τε πᾶσι 1.5; ῥίγησέν τ᾽ ἂρ ἔπειτα ἄναξ ἀνδρῶν Ἀγαμέμνων variant for{δ᾽ ἂρ} in 11.254}}]; ἕν τε οὐδὲν κατέστη ἴαμα.., σῶμά τε αὔταρκες ὂν οὐδὲν διεφάνη.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51; τά τε ἱερὰ.. νεκρῶν πλέα ἦν.. [prev. work] 52; νόμοι τε πάντες ξυνεταράχθησαν [prev. passage]; δάκνει σ᾽ ἀδελφὸς ὅ τε θανὼν ἴσως πατήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 242, compare 253, 262, +others}}; εἴς τε τὰς ἄλλας.. ἀθροίζεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 3.5; τῶν τε ἀρχόντων.. [prev. work] 6, compare 10.8, +others}}; ὅ τε γραφεὶς κύκλος.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 11 “Def.” 7; πρός τε τούτοις φησὶν.. “PEnteux.” 63.18 (3rd c.BC)}}; χωρίς τε τούτων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.56.13, 61.1, 3.17.7; ταῦτά τ᾽ ἐγίνετο.. [prev. author] 2.43.6, compare 3.70.4; ἀπαιτούμενός τε ὑπ᾽ ἐμοῦ τὰ ἔρια οὐκ ἀποδίδωσί “PEnteux.” 2.6, compare 8.4, +others (3rd c.BC); γράψαι Ἀγαθοκλεῖ τῷ ἐπιστάτῃ διασαφῆσαί τε αὐτῷ [prev. work] 81.21 (3rd c.BC)}}; καθόλου τε.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.19.13, compare 2.2.17; ἀταράχους τήν τε δύναμιν ἀκαθαιρέτους 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.21, compare 24, +others; ὄξει βαφικῷ στυπτηρίᾳ τε “PHolm.” 1.4, compare 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 16.6; χρὴ.. λαχάνων ἅπτεσθαι, κοιλίαν τε λύειν “Geoponica” 1.12.19, compare 2.2.2, +others}}; this τε may be used any number of times, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.149 - 150, 14.75, 158 - 9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 15, 16, 20, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.9.8, NT.Act.2.43, 46, 4.13, 14, +others}}
**A.II** τε.. καὶ.., or τε καὶ.., **both.. **and.., where τε points forward to καί, and usually need not be translated, e.g. Ἀτρείδης τε ἄναξ ἀνδρῶν καὶ δῖος Ἀχιλλεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.7; εἰ δὴ ὁμοῦ πόλεμός τε δαμᾷ καὶ λοιμὸς Ἀχαιούς [prev. work] 61}}; δειλός τε καὶ οὐτιδανὸς καλεοίμην{{Refs|[prev. work] 293; ζωόν τε καὶ ἀρτεμέα 7.308, compare 327, 338, +others}}; τῆς τε γῆς ἐούσης ἐπιτηδέης καὶ τῶν ποταμῶν ἐόντων σφι συμμάχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.47; βούλεταί τε καὶ ἐπίσταται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35; ὁ φύς τε καὶ τραφείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396c; βάσιν τε γὰρ πάλιν τὴν αὐτὴν ἔχουσι τὴν ΖΒ καὶ.. 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.47}}; sometimes the elements joined by τε.. καὶ.. are joined in order to be compared or contrasted rather than simply joined, κάκιστος νῦν τε καὶ πάλαι δοκεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 181; μεσαμβρίη τέ ἐστι καὶ τὸ κάρτα γίνεται ψυχρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181; ἔτυχόν τε ὕσταται ἐξαναχθεῖσαι καί κως κατεῖδον [prev. author] 7.194}}; ἐπαύσατό τε ὁ ἄνεμος καὶ τὸ κῦμα ἔστρωτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 193; ταὐτὰ.. νῦν τε καὶ τότε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 24; χωρὶς τό τ᾽ εἰπεῖν πολλὰ καὶ τὰ καίρια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 808; ὅσον τό τ᾽ ἄρχειν καὶ τὸ δουλεύειν δίχα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 927}}; sometimes (like τε.. τε) even used of alternatives, διάνδιχα μερμήριξεν, ἵππους τε στρέψαι καὶ ἐναντίβιον μαχέσασθαι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.168; ἐν δίκᾳ τε καὶ παρὰ δίκαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.16; θεοῦ τε.. θέλοντος καὶ μὴ θέλοντος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 427; πείσας τε.. καὶ μὴ τυχών 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42:—on οἵ τε ἄλλοι καὶ.., e.g. τοῖς τε ἄλλοις ἅπασι καὶ Λακεδαιμονίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.249, and ἄλλως τε καὶ.., see.{ἄλλος} 11.6, ἄλλως 1.3.}}]
**A.II.2** in this sense τ᾽ ἠδέ is only _Epic dialect_, σκῆπτρόν τ᾽ ἠδὲ θέμιστας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.99, compare 1.400, +others}}; also τε.., ἰδέ, χαλκόν τε ἰδὲ λόφον {{Refs|6.469, compare 8.162.}}
**A.II.3** καὶ.. τε, **both.. and.. **, is occasionally found, as καὶ μητέρα πατέρα τ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 646.}}
**A.II.3.b** καὶ.. τε perhaps means **and.. also** in καὶ ναυτικῷ τε ἅμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; καὶ πρός τε τοὺς Ῥηγίνους [prev. author] 6.44; καὶ αὐτός τε [prev. author] 8.68; see below with 10.}}
**A.II.4** τε.. τε or τε.. καὶ.. sometimes join elements which are not syntactically parallel, especially a _participle_ and a finite verb, ἰοῖσίν τε τιτυσκόμενοι λάεσσί τ᾽ ἔβαλλον (for βάλλοντες) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.80; ἄλλα τε ἐπιφραζόμενος καὶ δὴ καὶ ἐπεπόμφεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; ἀλλῳ τε τρόπῳ πειράζοντες καὶ μηχανὴν προσήγαγον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.100; τῆς τε ὥρας.. ταύτης οὔσης.., καὶ τὸ χωρίον.. χαλεπὸν ἦν [prev. author] 7.47, compare 4.85, 8.81, 95.}}
**A.II.5** the copulative τε{{Refs|becomes rare in later Gr.; it is found about 340 times in LXX, mostly in the Pentateuch and 1- “4 Ma.”, only 3 times in “Ps.”; in the “NT”}} it is found about 150 times in {{NT|NT.Act., 20 times in NT.Heb., and very rarely in the other books.}}
**B** In _Epic dialect_ (more rarely in other dactylic verse, see below {{Refs|11)}} τε stands in general or frequentative statements or in statements of what is well known; such statements are frequently made as justifications of a preceding particular statement or of a preceding exhortation to a particular person or persons; the sense of τε thus approaches that of τοι (compare τοι and τε in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.276 - 7, and compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.115 with 15.203)}}; although associated with numerous particles and other words of particular types (see. below) its meaning remains independent of these and applies to the whole sentence in which it stands: αἶψά τε φυλόπιδος πέλεται κόρος ἀνθρώποισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.221; οὐ γάρ τ᾽ αἶψα θεῶν τρέπεται νόος αἰὲν ἐόντων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.147; θεοὶ δέ τε πάντα ἴσασιν 4.379, compare 5.79, 447, 10.306, 17.485, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.497, 16.688, 17.176, 21.264; ξυνὸς Ἐνυάλιος καί τε κτανέοντα κατέκτα 18.309, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.537, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.526; ἤ τ᾽ ἔβλητ᾽ ἤ τ᾽ ἔβαλ᾽ ἄλλον 11.410; οὐ μὲν γάρ τε κακὸν βασιλευέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.392; οἳ φύλλοισιν ἐοικότες ἄλλοτε μέν τε ζαφλεγέες τελέθουσιν.. ἄλλοτε δὲ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.464; ἄλλος γάρ τ᾽ ἄλλοισιν ἀνὴρ ἐπιτέρπεται ἔργοις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.228, compare 8.169, 170, 15.400; τοῦ γάρ τε ξεῖνος μιμνήσκεται ἤματα πάντα, ἀνδρὸς ξεινοδόκου, ὅς κεν φιλότητα παράσχῃ [prev. work] 54, compare 17.322; ῥεχθὲν δέ τε νήπιος ἔγνω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.32; παθὼν δέ τε νήπιος ἔγνω 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 218; αἰεὶ γάρ τε νεώτεροι ἀφραδέουσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.294}}; δύσζηλοι γάρ τ᾽ εἰμὲν ἐπὶ χθονὶ φῦλ᾽ ἀνθρώπων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 307; τοῦ δέ τε πολλοὶ ἐπαυρίσκοντ᾽ ἄνθρωποι, καί τε πολέας ἐσάωσε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.733 - 4; τοῦ μὲν γάρ τε κακοῦ τρέπεται χρὼς ἄλλυδις ἄλλῃ, ἐν δέ τέ οἱ κραδίη στέρνοισι πατάσσει.., πάταγος δέ τε γίγνετ᾽ ὀδόντων [prev. work] 279 - 83; ὀλίγη δέ τ᾽ ἀνάπνευσις πολέμοιο 18.201; νέῳ δέ τε πάντ᾽ ἐπέοικεν.. κεῖσθαι 22.71; κατέλεξεν ἅπαντα κήδε᾽ ὅσ᾽ ἀνθρώποισι πέλει, τῶν ἄστυ ἁλώῃ· ἄνδρας μὲν κτείνουσι, πόλιν δέ τε πῦρ ἀμαθύνει, τέκνα δέ τ᾽ ἄλλοι ἄγουσι, βαθυζώνους τε γυναῖκας 9.592 - 4, compare 22.492, 495, 499}}; νεμεσσῶμαί γε μὲν οὐδέν· καὶ γάρ τίς τ᾽ ἀλλοῖον ὀδύρεται ἄνδρ᾽ ὀλέσασα.. ἢ Ὀδυσῆ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.265; σχέτλιε, καὶ μέν τίς τε χερείονι πείθεθ᾽ ἑταίρῳ.., αὐτὰρ ἐγὼ θεός εἰμι 20.45, compare 23.118, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.292, 9.632; νῦν δὲ μνησώμεθα δόρπου· καὶ γάρ τ᾽ ἠΰκομος Νιόβη ἐμνήσατο σίτου κτλ. 24.602 (where a general inference is implied)}}; ὃν Βριάρεων καλέουσι θεοί, ἄνδρες δέ τε πάντες Αἰγαίων᾽ {{Refs|1.403, compare 2.814, 5.306, 10.258, 14.290}}; sometimes of repeated action by particular persons, ἄλλοτε μέν τε γόῳ φρένα τέρπομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.102; οὐ μὰ γὰρ Ἀπόλλωνα Διὶ φίλον, ᾧ τε σύ, Κάλχαν, εὐχόμενος.. θεοπροπίας ἀναφαίνεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.86}}; ἡ δὲ.. μ᾽ αἰεὶ.. νεικεῖ, καί τέ μέ φησι μάχῃ Τρώεσσιν ἀρήγειν{{Refs|[prev. work] 521; μήτηρ γάρ τέ μέ φησι θεά, Θέτις ἀργυρόπεζα, διχθαδίας κῆρας φερέμεν θανάτοιο τέλοσδε 9.410.}}
**B.2** in exhortations addressed to an individual, a subsidiary sentence or relative clause in which he is reminded of his special or characteristic sphere of activity is marked by τε, e.g. Ἑρμεία, σοὶ γάρ τε μάλιστά γε φίλτατόν ἐστιν ἀνδρὶ ἑταιρίσσαι καί τ᾽ ἔκλυες ᾧ κ᾽ ἐθέλῃσθα, βάσκ᾽ ἴθι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.334; Ἀτρεΐδη, σοὶ γάρ τε μάλιστά γε λαὸς Ἀχαιῶν πείσονται μύθοισι.., νῦν δ᾽ ἀπὸ πυρκαϊῆς σκέδασον.. 23.156; δεῦρο δὴ ὄρσο, γρηῢ.., ἥ τε γυναικῶν δμῳάων σκοπός ἐσσι.., ἔρχεο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.395, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.249.}}
**B.3** similarly in general and frequentative statements consisting of two clauses (one of which may be a relative clause, frequently containing the _subjunctive_ or _optative_), in which the fulfilment of the condition stated in the subsidiary or subordinate clause is declared to be generally or always followed by the result stated in the principal clause, either or both clauses may contain τε:
**B.3.a** the principal clause alone contains τε, ὅς κε θεοῖς ἐπιπείθηται, μάλα τ᾽ ἔκλυον αὐτοῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.218; ὃς δ᾽ ἂν ἀμύμων αὐτὸς ἔῃ καὶ ἀμύμονα εἰδῇ, τοῦ μέν τε κλέος εὐρὺ διὰ ξεῖνοι φορέουσι πάντας ἐπ᾽ ἀνθρώπους, πολλοί τέ μιν ἐσθλὸν ἔειπον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.333; εἴ περ γὰρ θυμῷ γε μενοινάᾳ πολεμίζειν, ἀλλά τε λάθρῃ γυῖα βαρύνεται.., βλάβεται δέ τε γούνατ᾽ ἰόντι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.165 - 6; ᾧ μέν κ᾽ ἀμμείξας δώῃ Ζεὺς τερπικέραυνος, ἄλλοτε μέν τε κακῷ ὅ γε κύρεται ἄλλοτε δ᾽ ἐσθλῷ 24.530.}}
**B.3.b** the subordinate clause alone contains τε, λάζετο δ᾽ ἔγχος.. τῷ δάμνησι στίχας ἀνδρῶν ἡρώων οἷσίν τε κοτέσσεται ὀβριμοπάτρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.747; ῥεῖα δ᾽ ἀρίγνωτος γόνος ἀνέρος ᾧ τε Κρονίων ὄλβον ἐπικλώση 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.207; ἀντί νυ πολλῶν λαῶν ἐστιν ἀνὴρ ὅν τε Ζεὺς κῆρι φιλήσῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.117, compare 7.298, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.287, 7.74, 8.547, 18.276; with _optative_, ἀλλὰ πολὺ πρώτιστος.. ἕλεσκον ἀνδρῶν δυσμενέων ὅ τέ μοι εἴξειε πόδεσσι 14.221}}: it is probably that τε has been replaced by κε in the text of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.218, 9.510 (compare 508)}}, and some other passages in which κε seems to be used, exceptionally, in general relative clauses.
**B.3.c** both clauses contain τε, ὃς μέν τ᾽ αἰδέσεται κούρας Διὸς ἆσσον ἰούσας, τὸν δὲ μέγ᾽ ὤνησαν καί τ᾽ ἔκλυον εὐχομένοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.508 - 9; εἴ περ γάρ τε χόλον γε καὶ αὐτῆμαρ καταπέψῃ, ἀλλά τε καὶ μετόπισθεν ἔχει κότον 1.82 - 3.}}
**B.4** in the subordinate clause of a collective sentence, in which the principal clause states something to be true of all those (i.e. each individual) to whom the predicate of the subordinate clause applies, ὑπόσχωμαι.. κτήματα.. πάντα μάλ᾽ ὅσσα τ᾽ Ἀλέξανδρος.. ἠγάγετο Τροίηνδ᾽.. δωσέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.115; πάντων ὅσσα τε γαῖαν ἔπι πνείει τε καὶ ἕρπει 17.447, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.131, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.105; βάλλειν ἄγρια πάντα τά τε τρέφει οὔρεσιν ὕλη 5.52, compare 18.485.}}
**B.5** in relative clauses (and in parenthetic principal clauses) which indicate what is customary, ἐπεὶ οὐχ ἱερήϊον οὐδὲ βοείην ἀρνύσθην, ἅ τε ποσσὶν ἀέθλια γίγνεται ἀνδρῶν which are the **usual** prizes.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.160; ἔργ᾽ ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε, τά τε κλείουσιν ἀοιδοί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.338, compare 3.435, 4.85, 13.410, 14.226, 17.423, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.332; κύματος ἐξαναδύς, τά τ᾽ ἐρεύγεται ἤπειρόνδε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.438; μολπή τ᾽ ὀρχηστύς τε, τὰ γάρ τ᾽ ἀναθήματα δαιτός 1.152}}: similarly in clauses with οἷά τε (πολλά), κῆτος ἐπισσεύῃ μέγα δαίμων ἐξ ἁλός, οἷά τε πολλὰ τρέφει.. Ἀμφιτρίτη {{Refs|5.422; οὐ γάρ σ᾽ οὐδέ.. δαήμονι φωτὶ ἐΐσκω ἄθλων, οἷά τε πολλὰ μετ᾽ ἀνθρώποισι πέλονται 8.160, compare 11.364, 14.63, 15.324, 379.}}
**B.6** in relative clauses indicating what is true of all persons or things denoted by the same word, οὐ γάρ τις νήσων ἱππήλατος οὐδ᾽ εὐλείμων αἵ θ᾽ ἁλὶ κεκλίαται no one of the islands which lie in the sea (as all islands do, i.e. no island at all), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.608; ἡμίονον.. ἥ τ᾽ ἀλγίστη δαμάσασθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.655; ἐσθλὸς ἐὼν γαμβρὸς ἢ πενθερός, οἵ τε μάλιστα κήδιστοι τελέθουσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.582; αἰετοῦ οἴματ᾽ ἔχων.. ὅς θ᾽ ἅμα κάρτιστός τε καὶ ὤκιστος πετεηνῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.252, compare 24.294; οὐδέ μιν εἰσοιχνεῦσι κυνηγέται, οἵ τε καθ᾽ ὕλην ἄλγεα πάσχουσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.120; δικασπόλοι, οἵ τε θέμιστας πρὸς Διὸς εἰρύαται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.238, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.67, 101, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.279, 19.31, 24.415; οἶνός σε τρώει.., ὅς τε καὶ ἄλλους βλάπτει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.293, compare 14.464; πάρφασις, ἥ τ᾽ ἔκλεψε νόον πύκα περ φρονεόντων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.217; οἰκωφελίη, ἥ τε τρέφει ἀγλαὰ τέκνα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.223.}}
**B.7** when the antecedent is a definite group of gods or men, the relative clause with τε indicates an essential characteristic of the antecedent, Ἐρινύες, αἵ θ᾽ ὑπὸ γαῖαν ἀνθρώπους τείνυνται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.259; Σειρῆνας.., αἵ ῥά τε πάντας ἀνθρώπους θέλγουσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.39; Φαίηκές μ᾽ ἄγαγον ναυσίκλυτοι, οἵ τε καὶ ἄλλους ἀνθρώπους πέμπουσιν 16.227, compare 20.187; νυμφάων αἵ τ᾽ ἄλσεα καλὰ νέμονται καὶ πηγὰς ποταμῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.8; Λωτοφάγων, οἵ τ᾽ ἄνθινον εἶδαρ ἔδουσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.84: similarly when the antecedent is an individual person (including god)}} or thing, the relative clause with τε indicates one of his or its general or essential characteristics or aspects, οὐ μὰ Ζῆν᾽ ὅς τίς τε θεῶν ὕπατος καὶ ἄριστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.43, compare 2.669, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.4; Ἑρμείαο ἕκητι διακτόρου, ὅς ῥά τε πάντων ἀνθρώπων ἔργοισι χάριν καὶ κῦδος ὀπάζει 15.319; Λάμπον καὶ Φαέθονθ᾽, οἵ τ᾽ Ἠῶ πῶλοι ἄγουσι 23.246; Τειρεσίαο μάντιος ἀλαοῦ, τοῦ τε φρένες ἔμπεδοί εἰσι 10.493; τεύχεα δύνεις ἀνδρὸς ἀριστῆος, τόν τε τρομέουσι καὶ ἄλλοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.203, compare 7.112}}; κεῖται ἀνὴρ ὅν τ᾽ (variant{ὃν}) ἶσον ἐτίομεν Ἕκτορι δίῳ, Αἰνείας {{Refs|5.467}}; the relative clause sometimes indicates what is customary, οὐδέ σε λήθω τιμῆς ἧς τέ μ᾽ ἔοικε τετιμῆσθαι μετ᾽ Ἀχαιοῖς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.649; ἔνθα δ᾽ ἀνὴρ ἐνίαυε πελώριος, ὅς ῥά τε μῆλα οἶος ποιμαίνεσκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.187; τῶν πάντων οὐ τόσσον ὀδύρομαι.. ὡς ἑνός, ὅς τέ μοι ὕπνον ἀπεχθαίρει καὶ ἐδωδὴν μνωομένῳ 4.105; σῆς ἀλόχου.. ἥ τέ τοι αὔτως ἧσται ἐνὶ μεγάροισιν 13.336; καὶ κήρυκα Μέδοντα σαώσομεν, ὅς τέ μευ αἰεὶ.. κηδέσκετο 22.357, compare 346.}}
**B.8** τε is used in descriptions of particular places or things when attention is called to their peculiar or characteristic features, or their position, e.g. Λιβύην, ἵνα τ᾽ ἄρνες ἄφαρ κεραοὶ τελέθουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.85; ἔνθα δέ τ᾽ ὄρνιθες τανυσίπτεροι εὐνάζοντο 5.65, compare 9.124, 13.99, 100, 107, 109, 244}}; ἓξ δέ τέ οἱ (i.e. Σκύλλῃ) δειραὶ περιμήκεες {{Refs|12.90, compare 93, 99, 105}}; ἐν δέ τε Γοργείη κεφαλή (in Athena's αἰγίς) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.741}}; χαλεπὸν δέ τ᾽ ὀρύσσειν ἀνδράσι γε θνητοῖσι (i.e. μῶλυ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.305; δοιαὶ γάρ τε πύλαι ἀμενηνῶν εἰσὶν ὀνείρων 19.562}}; sometimes τε draws attention to a well-known custom or permanent feature, ἀρξάμενοι τοῦ χώρου, ὅθεν τέ περ οἰνοχοεύει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.142; ἦ μένετε Τρῶας σχεδὸν ἐλθέμεν, ἔνθα τε νῆες εἰρύατ᾽ εὔπρυμνοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.247, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.266; ἐν ποταμῷ, ὅθι τ᾽ ἀρδμὸς ἔην πάντεσσι βοτοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.521, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.353.}}
**B.9** a part of the anatomy is defined by a clause (containing τε) which indicates a feature which universally belongs to it, κατ᾽ ἰσχίον, ἔνθα τε μηρὸς ἰσχίῳ ἐνστρέφεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.305, compare 8.83, 13.547, 16.481, 20.478}}; similarly a point of time is defined, ὥρῃ ἐν εἰαρινῇ, ὅτε τ᾽ ἤματα μακρὰ πέλονται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.367.}}
**B.10** τε is used in relative clauses which define a measurement of a particular thing or action by reference to the measurement (in general) of some thing or action well known in daily life, γεφύρωσεν δὲ κέλευθον μακρὴν ἠδ᾽ εὐρεῖαν, ὅσον τ᾽ ἐπὶ δουρὸς ἐρωὴ γίγνεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.358; τοῦ δ᾽ ἤτοι κλέος ἔσται ὅσον τ᾽ ἐπικίδναται ἠώς 7.451; ὅτε τόσσον ἀπῆν ὅσσον τε γέγωνε βοήσας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.473, compare 3.321, +others}}; more rarely the definition is by reference to the measurement of a particular thing or action, ἤσθιε.. ἕως ὅ τ᾽ ἀοιδὸς ἐνὶ μεγάροισιν ἄειδεν{{Refs|(assuming variant) 17.358; ἥ τις δὴ τέτληκε τόσα φρεσίν, ὅσσα τ᾽ ἐγώ περ 19.347.}}
**B.11** the frequently use of τε B in similes is to be explained under one or other of the {ταώς} heads, e.g. when reference is made to generally known kinds of things or natural phenomena, to human experience in daily life, or to well-known phenomena of the animal world, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.456, 459, 463, 468, 470, 471, 474, 481, 3.23 - 5, 33, 11.415 - 7, +others; or when universal characteristics of gods, men, animals, etc., are indicated by relative clauses introduced by ὅς τε, ὅς ῥά τε, etc., 3.61, 151, 198, +others; or by ὥς τε, ἠΰτε, ὥς τίς τε, etc., e.g. 5.136, 17.133, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.535, ὡς εἴ τε 9.314, 14.254, etc.}}
**B.II** in post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} Gr. this use of τε is more restricted; outside of _Epic dialect_ and other early dactylic verse [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 30, 214, 233, +others, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 13.3, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 148, 359, etc.) it is not found except with relatives, and with these it has scarcely any discernible sense, so that ὅς τε in Lyric poetry and Trag. is for the most part only ={ὅς}, e.g. (possibly generalizing) Μοῖρ᾽, ἅ τε πατρώϊον τῶνδ᾽ ἔχει τὸν εὔφρονα πότμον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.35, compare 14.2, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1024, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 445 (Lyric poetry), etc. (see. ὅστε)}}]; without generalizing force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.9, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 297, “Ch.” 615, etc.; 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has τά πέρ τε 1.74, ὅκως τε 2.108 codices, ὅσον τε}} (without a verb, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.325, +others) 1.126, 2.96, 3.5, +others, οἷά τε 1.93 codices (adverbially 2.175, 5.11): in _Attic dialect_ Prose and Comedy texts even these uses disappear and we find only a few phrases, as ἅτε, ὥστε, ἐφ᾽ ᾧτε, οἷός τε}}; in later Gr. we find exceptionally ἔνθεν τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17; ἀφ᾽ οὗ τε “UPZ” 62.8 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἀπ᾽ οὗ τε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 291.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; οἵ τε {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 215.23 (Erythrae, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἥ τ᾽ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PMag.Par.” 1.2962; ὅσον τε ὀκτὼ στάδια 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.26.1}}; καὶ ἔστιν ἔπη Μαντικὰ ὁπόσα τε (= **which**) ἐπελεξάμεθα καὶ ἡμεῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 9.31.5; οἷόν τε καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς κύων φωνῆς θεωροῦμεν 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.28.}}
**C** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} τε is also (but less frequently) used in conjunction with other particles in contexts (mainly particular statements) such as the following:
**C.1** in assurances, statements on oath, and threats, σχέτλιος, ἦ τ᾽ ἐκέλευον ἀπωσάμενον δήϊον πῦρ ἂψ ἐπὶ νῆας ἴμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.13; ἐξ αὖ νῦν ἔφυγες θάνατον, κύον· ἦ τέ τοι ἄγχι ἦλθε κακόν 11.362; ἦ τε is similarly used in 11.391, 17.171, 236, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.28, 311, +others}}; ἦ τ᾽ ἄν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.69, +others}}; γάρ τε (assuming variant) in οὐ γάρ τ᾽ οἶδα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.367, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.190; νύ τε in 1.60, 347}} (but τ᾽ more probably = τοι, see at {σύ}); δέ τε in ἀγορῇ δέ τ᾽ ἀμείνονές εἰσι καὶ ἄλλοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.106; σὲ δέ τ᾽ ἐνθάδε γῦπες ἔδονται 16.836}}; μέν τε in σφὼ μέν τε σαώσετε λαὸν Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs|13.47, compare 4.341}}; εἴ πέρ τε in οὔ τοι ἔτι δηρόν γε φίλης ἀπὸ πατρίδος αἴης ἔσσεται, οὐδ᾽ εἴ πέρ τε σιδήρεα δέσματ᾽ ἔχῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.204, compare 188, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.223, 245.}}
**C.2** also in commands, warnings, and admonitions, σίγα, μή τίς τ᾽ ἄλλος Ἀχαιῶν τοῦτον ἀκούσῃ μῦθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.90, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.486; ὣς ἄγαγ᾽ ὡς μήτ᾽ ἄρ τις ἴδῃ μήτ᾽ ἄρ τε νοήσῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.337}}; τούσδε τ᾽ (variant δ᾽) ἐᾶν {{Refs|16.96 (nisi to be read τούσδ᾽ ἔτ᾽); δὸς δέ τέ μ᾽ ἄνδρα ἑλεῖν 5.118; μηδέ τ᾽ ἐρώει (nisi to be read μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽) 2.179, 22.185.}}
**C.3** also in passionate utterances, in clauses which indicate the cause of the speaker's passion or a circumstance which might have caused others to behave more considerately towards him, ὤ μοι ἐγὼ δειλή.. ἥ τ᾽.. τὸν μὲν.. θρέψασα.. ἐπιπροέηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.55; σχέτλιοί ἐστε, θεοί, ζηλήμονες ἔξοχον ἄλλων, οἵ τε θεαῖς ἀγάασθε.. ἤν τίς τε.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.119, 120, compare 21.87, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.468, 17.174}}; ἡμεῖς δ᾽ αὖ μαχόμεσθ᾽, οἵ πέρ τ᾽ ἐπίκουροι ἔνειμεν and we, who (**mark you**) are only allies (not γαμβροί and κασίγνητοι), are fighting, {{Refs|5.477}}; τρεῖς γάρ τ᾽ ἐκ Κρόνου εἰμὲν ἀδελφεοί for we, **let me tell you**, are three brothers, sons of Cronos (and Zeus has no prior title to power),{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.187; ποῖόν δε ἔπος φύγεν ἕρκος ὀδόντων δεινόν τ᾽ ἀργαλέον τε· νεμεσσῶμαι δέ τ᾽ ἀκούων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.169; οὐ μήν οἱ τό γε κάλλιον οὐδέ τ᾽ ἄμεινον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52.}}
**C.4** in descriptions of particular events and things where there is no general reference, κνίση μὲν ἀνήνοθεν, ἐν δέ τε φόρμιγξ ἠπύει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.270}}; ὥς (= **so**) τέ μοι ὑβρίζοντες ὑπερφιάλως δοκέουσιν δαίνυσθαι κατὰ δῶμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.227; τοὺς μέν τ᾽ ἰητροὶ πολυφάρμακοι ἀμφιπένονται.. σὺ δ᾽ ἀμήχανος ἔπλευ, Ἀχιλλεῦ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.28}}; πόλιν πέρι δινηθήτην καρπαλίμοισι πόδεσσι, θεοὶ δέ τε πάντες ὁρῶντο uncertain {{Refs 8th c.BC+|l. in 22.166; εὗρε δ᾽ ἐνὶ σπῆϊ γλαφυρῷ Θέτιν, ἀμφὶ δέ τ᾽ ἄλλαι εἵαθ᾽ ὁμηγερέες ἅλιαι θεαί 24.83 (assuming variant); ἐν δέ τε φάρμακον ἧκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.317; νῶϊ δέ τ᾽ ἄψορροι κίομεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.456; πολλὰς γὰρ δὴ νύκτας.. ἄεσα καί τ᾽ ἀνέμεινα.. Ἠῶ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.342; δέελον δ᾽ ἐπὶ σῆμά τ᾽ ἔθηκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.466; ἐν δέ τε οἶνον κρητῆρσιν κερόωντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.252}}; so with οὐδέ τ᾽ (nisi to be read οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽), τὸν καὶ ὑπέδδεισαν μάκαρες θεοὶ οὐδέ τ᾽ ἔδησαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.406; οὐδέ τ᾽ ἔληγε μέγας θεός, ὦρτο δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν 21.248; οὐδέ τ᾽ ἄειρε 23.730; οὐδέ τ᾽ ἔασεν 11.437, 21.596, compare 15.709.}}
**C.5** ὅτε τε (**when**) frequently introduces a temporal clause defining a point of time in the past by means of a well-known event which occurred then, ἦ οὐ μέμνῃ ὅτε τ᾽ ἐκρέμω ὑψόθε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.18; ὅτε τε Κρόνον.. Ζεὺς γαίης νέρθε καθεῖσε 14.203; ἤματι τῷ ὅτε τ᾽ ἦλθον Ἀμαζόνες 3.189 (but ἤματι τῷ ὅτε τε is general in 13.335}}; so also ὅτε πέρ τε.. κέρωνται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|in 4.259); ὅτε τ᾽ ἤλυθε νόσφιν Ἀχαιῶν ἄγγελος ἐς Θήβας 5.803, compare 10.286, 22.102, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.323, 18.257.}}
**C.6** in ὅ τε (**that** or **because**) the τε has no observable meaning, χωόμενος ὅ τ᾽ ἄριστον Ἀχαιῶν οὐδὲν ἔτισας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.244, compare 412, 4.32, 6.126, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.357, +others}}
**C.7** ἐπεί τε = {ἐπεί} (**when**) is rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐπεί τ᾽ ἐνόησε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.393, compare ἐπείτε.}}
**C.8** where τ᾽ ἄρ occurs in questions, e.g. πῇ τ᾽ ἂρ μέμονας καταδῦναι ὅμιλο; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.307, compare 1.8, 18.188, +others}}, ταρ (which see) should probably be read, since ἄρ (α) usually precedes a τε which is not copulative; so perhaps ταρα should be read for τ᾽ ἄρα in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.346.}}
**C.9** in ἣ θέμις ἐστὶν.. ἤ τ᾽ ἀνδρῶν ἤ τε γυναικῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.276}}, it is not clear whether τε is copulative (τε A) or generalizing (τε B) or neither (τε C); ἤ is probably ={ἦ} (accented as in ἤτοι (; ἤ τ᾽ ἀλκῆς ἤ τε φόβοιο is uncertain reading in {{Refs|17.42}}; ἤ τ᾽ = **or** is found in {{Refs|19.148, =}}**than** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.216.}}
**C.10** Rarer and later uses;
**C.10.a** **also**, especially with ἄλλος, Ἑρμεία, σὺ γὰρ αὖτε τά τ᾽ ἄλλα περ ἄγγελός ἐσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.29, compare 17.273, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.483; ἐπεὶ τά τε ἄλλα πράττουσιν καλῶς, ἀναθεῖναι αὐτοὺς καὶ στήλην “IG” 22.1298.9, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 100 (assuming variant); ἐκομισάμην τὸ παρὰ σοῦ ἐπιστόλιον, ἐν ᾧ ὑπέγραψάς μοι τήν τε παρὰ Ζήνωνος πρὸς Ἰεδδοῦν γεγραμμένην “PCair.Zen.” 18.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἰ οὖν περὶ τούτων ἐπιστροφὴν μὴ ποιήσει, οἵ τε λοιποί μοι τὰς χεῖρας προσοίσουσιν (-σωσιν Papyrus) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῶν δὲ παρὰ ταῦτα ποιησόντων τά τε κτήνη ὑπὸ στέρεσιν ἀχθήσεσθαι πρὸς τὰ ἐκφόρια {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.74 (2nd c.BC)}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.3b.}}
**C.10.b** with ὅδε, adding a slight emphasis to the preceding word, εἰ δὴ τήνδε τε γαῖαν ἀνείρεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.238, compare 15.484.}}
**C.10.c** τε γάρ rarely ={καὶ γάρ} or γάρ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 75b41, “de An.” 405a4, “PA” 661b28, “Pol.” 1318b33, 1333a2}}; ἐάν τε γάρ for **even** if, {{NT|NT.2Cor.10.8}}; τήν τε γὰρ ἐπιθυμίαν οὐκ ᾔδειν for I had not known **even** lust. {{NT|NT.Rom.7.7.}}
**D** Position of τε:
**D.1** in signf. A, as an enclitic, it stands second word in the sentence, clause, or phrase, regardless of the meaning: ἐγγύθι τε Πριάμοιο καὶ Ἕκτορος near **both** Priam and Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.317; ἡμέτεραί τ᾽ ἄλοχοι καὶ νήπια τέκνα 2.136, compare 4.505, 7.295; αἰεί τε δὴ νηλὴς οὺ καὶ θράσους πλέως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 42 codices, compare 291 (_anapaest meter_); ἄνευ τε δόλου καὶ ἀπάτης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.69; ὑπέρ τε σοῦ καὶ τῆς ἀδελφῆς “PEnteux.” 6.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; τοῖς τε πόνοις καὶ μαθήμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 537a, compare “Ti.” 70b; hence in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 897 πόλεος must be taken with what precedes (Porson (at [prev. work]))}}: but article + noun, preposition + noun are frequently regarded as forming a unity indivisible by τε, τοῖς κτανοῦσί τε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 41 (Lyric poetry); πρὸς βίαν τε [prev. author] “Pr.” 210}}; also the order is frequently determined by the meaning, τε being placed immediately after the word (or first word of a phrase or clause) which it joins to what precedes or to what follows, πατὴρ ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.544; ἔξω δόμων τε καὶ πάτρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 665}}; the copulative or preparatory τε precedes many other particles, e.g. τε γάρ, τ᾽ ἄρα, τέ τις.
**D.2** τε is enclitic in signfs. B, C also, and stands early in its sentence, clause, or phrase (see. above), but many particles which follow τε in signf. A precede it in signfs. B, C, e.g. in signfs. B, C we have δέ τε, μέν τε, γάρ τε, ἀλλά τε, δ᾽ ἄρα τε, ὅς ῥά τε, οὔτ᾽ ἄρ τε, καὶ γάρ τίς τε, ὅς τίς τε, καί τε.
**E** Etymology: signf. A is found also in Sanskrit _ca_, Latin _-que_; for signfs. B and c cf. Sanskrit _ca_ in **yá[hudot] káś ca** 'whosoever (with following verb)', Latin _-que_ in **quisque, ubique, plerique, usque, neque, nec** (= **non** in **necopinans**, etc.), Gothic _ni-h_ 'not' (also 'and not'), Latin _namque_ (= **nam**).

5038:
** τεῖχος**, εος, τό,
**wall**, especially **city-wall**, Ἰλιόφι κλυτὰ τείχεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.295, compare 446, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.9, +others}}; of the **embankment** round the ships, τάφρος Δαναῶν καὶ τ. ὕπερθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.4, compare 25ff; τ. ἐς ἀμφίχυτον (of heapedup earth) 20.145}}; Κιμμέρια τ. **earthworks**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.12; but τ. λάϊνα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1087 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93, etc.; λίθοις οὓς ἔλιπον εἰς τὸ τ. ἀναλίσκοντες “IG” 12.81.9}}; ξύλινον τεῖχος exceptionally, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.141, compare 8.51, 9.65, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.4, Oracle texts cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1040 (5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} uses this phrase for **a funeral pile, ** “P.” 3.38); τ. χάλκεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.3}}; τ. σιδηροῦν, τείχη χαλκᾶ καὶ ἀδαμάντινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1046, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.84}}; τειχέων κιθῶνες coats **of walls**, i.e. walls one within the other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139}}; τεῖχος ἐλαύνειν, see at {ἐλαύνω} {{Refs 8th c.BC+|111.2; τ. ἔδειμαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.436; οἰκοδομέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1132, etc. (τ. οἰκοδομήσασθαι}} to build oneself **walls**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.11); τ. ἄξειν [prev. author] 6.99; τὰ τ. στῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.74; τ. περιβάλλειν ταῖς πόλεσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a3}}; τ. περιβαλέσθαι build **walls** round one's city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, etc. (also τ. περιβαλέσθαι τὴν πόλιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.163:}} —hence _passive_, τεῖχος περιβεβλημένος having a **wall** round it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174e}}; τείχη περιβεβλημένοι, of citizens, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a8)}}; but also νῆσον περιβάλλεσθαι τείχει surround one's island with **walls**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116a}}; τ. ῥήξασθαι breach the **wall**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.90, compare 257; τεῖχος ἀναρρήξας 7.461}}; so in Prose, τ. διαιρεῖν, περιαιρέειν, κατελεῖν κατασκάψαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.46, 48, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.109, etc.}}
**2** τὰ μακρὰ τ., at Athens, lines of wall connecting the city-wall (ὁ περίβολος) and the harbours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}; they were called respectively τὸ βόρειον or Peiraic, and τὸ νότιον or Phaleric wall {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439e, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.173, 174), compare σκέλος 11: an intermediate wall (τὸ διὰ μέσου τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 455e) ran parallel to the northern, which was therefore called also τὸ ἔξωθεν, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13: the quarter inside the walls is sometimes called τὸ μακρὸν τ}}. **Long Wall**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45.}}
**II** **fortification, castle, fort**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 213, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, 91, etc.}}
**III** **walled, fortified town** or **city**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.41, 115, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.13, etc.}}
**IV** **wall** of a temple, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 20.52 (3rd c.BC)}}; of a house, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 15.8 (3rd c.AD), “PStrassb.” 9.8 (4th c.AD)}}, etc. (Cf. Sanskrit _dehmi_ 'anoint, smear, plaster', Gothic _digan_ 'mould, create (= πλάσσω)', **daigs** 'dough', Latin _fingo_, **figura**, Osc. **feihúss** 'walls', etc.)

5039:
** τεκμήρι-ον**, τό,
(τεκμαίρομαι) ={τέκμαρ}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11 (compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1357b8, 9)}}, a **sure sign** or **token**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 9.100, etc.; τεκμηρίοισιν ἐξ οἰμωγμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1366}}; καὶ μὴν στίβοι γε, δεύτερον τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 205}}; θανόντος πίστ᾽ ἔχων τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 774; ἐμφανῆ τ. [prev. work] 1109}}; ἀσφαλὲς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 94; ταῦτα δὴ πάντα τ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 8}}: in Medicine texts, a **sure symptom**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Prog.” 25, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.33, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).306.}}
**2** simply **sign, symbol**, τοῦ φιλαποδήμου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hippocr.” 12.}}
**II** **proof** (properly of an argumentative kind, opposed to direct evidence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.12, 8.6), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 485, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158b, +others}}; opposed to τὰ εἰκότα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.10}}; but οὐκ εἰκότα τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.4.2; τ. δίκαιον [prev. author] 1.10}}; τ. τινός **proof** of a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 662, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 482, etc.}}; τ. δὲ τοῦδε τὸν Ὅμηρον λαβέ (i.e. the case of Homer) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 97.5; also τ. περὶ τῶν μελλόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 185b}}; τ. τινὸς δοῦναι, παρασχέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 826, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.1; λέξω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 447}}; δείξω, ἐπιδείξω, ἀποδεῖξαι,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 662, “Supp.” 53 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158b; τ. ἀποφαίνειν περὶ σοφίας [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 283a; ἔχειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 271.}}
**II.2** τεκμήριον δέ as an independent clause, **now the proof of it is this** (which follows), **take this as a proof**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.10, etc.}}; more fully, τ. δέ μοι τούτου τόδε· αἱ μὲν γὰρ φαίνονται κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.58}}; τ. δὲ τούτου καὶ τόδε· παρὰ μὲν Κύρου κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.29}}; χρῆσθαι τεκμηρίῳ ὅτι.. (ὅτι introducing the reason, not the fact) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.24, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.15.}}
**II.3** in the Logic of Aristotle, **demonstrative proof**, opposed to to the fallible σημεῖον and εἰκός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“APr.” 70b2, “Rh.” 1357b4, 1402b19, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.369 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

5043:
** τέκνον**, τό,
(τίκτω,--οὐκ ἔστι μήτηρ ἡ κεκλημένου τέκνου τοκεύς, τροφὸς δὲ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 658)}}, **child**, ἄλοχοι καὶ νήπια τέκνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.136, +others; πατρὸς σωφροσύνη μέγιστον τέκνοις παράγγελμα 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 208, compare 222; τέκνα καὶ γυναῖκες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.164, 2.30, “SIG” 569.10 (Cos, 3rd c.BC), +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.58.9, 9.39.3}}; γυναῖκες καὶ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.19, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.78.1 (compare 10.34.3), “SIG” 633.46 (Milet., 2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1811.5 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.: the _singular_ is used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _vocative_, as a form of address from elders to their youngers, **my son, my child**, sometimes with _masculine_ _adjective_, φίλε τέκνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.84, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.363, +others}}: the _relative_ _pronoun_ or Participle sometimes follows in _masculine_ or _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 171, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 12f, “Tr.” 740}}:—the word is used in Prose at Cyrene, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.160, and Epidaurus, “IG” 42(1).122.82 (4th c.BC), +others, but is rarer than παῖς}} in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.74, 11.10, 12.96, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.109; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, “Lac.” 1.8, +others, also 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “Pol.” 1253b7, +others, and later, “PPetr.” 3p.237 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 620.9 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Gen.3.16, +others, “PAmh.” 2.35.55 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus (see. above)}}; rare in Comedy texts except in paratragoedic passages, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 891, +others}}; in Trag. it is generally used with especially reference to the mother, ὦ τέκνον Νηρῇδος, ὦ παῖ Πηλέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 896 (troch.)}}; Ἀγαμέμνονος παῖ (sic codices) καὶ Κλυταιμήστρας τέκνον {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 238.}}
**2** of animals,**young**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.217, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.311, 12.170, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 292 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66, 3.102, 109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 753a8, etc.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, flowers are γαίας τέκνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 618; birds αἰθέρος τέκνα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 897}}; frogs λιμναῖα κρηνῶν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 211, etc.}} [The _penultimate_ is long in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; it is occasionally long in Trag.(e.g. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 249, 260, 875, 914)}}, but much more frequently short, as always in old Comedy texts, e.g.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 7, “Th.” 469, +others}}, except in mock Tragic passages, e.g.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1518 (Lyric poetry)}}; but sometimes long in later Comedy texts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 163.6.]}}

5045:
** τέκτων**, ονος, ὁ
(_feminine_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1406, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 409)}}, **worker in wood, carpenter, joiner**, τέκτονες ἄνδρες, οἵ οἱ ἐποίησαν θάλαμον καὶ δῶμα καὶ αὐλήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.315, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 91; τέκτονος υἱόν, Ἁρμονίδεω.. ὂς καὶ Ἀλεξάνδρῳ τεκτήνατο νῆας ἐΐσας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.59}}; νηῶν, δούρων τ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.126, 17.384, compare 19.56, 21.43; [πίτυν] οὔρεσι τέκτονες ἄνδρες ἐξέταμον πελέκεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.390; τ., ὅς ῥά τε πάσης εὖ εἰδῇ σοφίης 15.411; τ. γὰρ ὢν ἔπρασσες οὐ ξυλουργικά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 988, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 357, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 474, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.37}}: it is frequently opposed to to a smith (χαλκεύς), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319d, “R.” 370d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.17}}; to a mason (λιθολόγος), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1154: frequently in Inscrr., “IG” 12.373.245, etc., and Papyri, “PCair.Zen.” 27.3 (3rd c.BC), etc.:}} —but also,
**2** generally, any **craftsman** or **workman**, κεραοξόος τ. **a worker** in horn, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.110, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 768}}; rarely of **metal-workers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Venerem 12; τ. Δίου πυρὸς Κύκλωπας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 5}}; **sculptor, statuary**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 348.}}
**3** **master in any art**, as in gymnastics, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.49}}; of poets, τέκτονες σοφοὶ (i.e. ἐπέων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 3.113; τέκτονες εὐπαλάμων ὕμνων 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 70 (cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 530)}}; τέκτονες κώμων, i.e. the χορευταί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.4}}; τ. νωδυνίας, i.e. a physician, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 3.6}}; δεξιᾶς χερὸς ἔργον, δικαίας τέκτονος a true **workman**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1406.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, **maker, author**, νεικέων {{Refs|[prev. work] 152 (Lyric poetry)}}; κακῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 409}}; γένους **the author** of a race, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 594 (Lyric poetry), compare 283}}; ψευδῶν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28; ὁ γὰρ χρόνος μ᾽ ἔκαμψε, τ. μὲν σοφός 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 39}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _ták[snull]an_- 'carpenter', **ták[snull]ati, tā[snull][tnull]i** 'form by cutting, plane, chisel, chop', Lett. **test, tēst** 'hew, plane', etc.: compare τέχνη.)

5046:
** τέλειος**
and **τέλεος**, α, ον, in Trag., _Attic dialect_, and _Doric dialect_also ος, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 382 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 67a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1153b16, “SIG” 265 (Delph., 4th c.BC), etc.: the form τέλειος}} is alone used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, neither form in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}; τέλεος is alone used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, except in 9.110; in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ both forms occur; _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. up to the end of 3rd c.BC have only τέλεος, “IG” 12.76.39, +others, and τέλεος, τελέως, τελεῶ}} are recommended by {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Thomas Epigrammaticus Mag. p.358R.}}; τέλειος first in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 22.2314.51, +others (early 2nd c.BC)}}, frequently in Papyri {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(“PCair.Zen.” 429.13, +others (3rd c.BC), etc.), but the _neuter_ used as}} _adverb_ is sometimes τέλεον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(“BGU” 903.12 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc., τέλειον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 707.31 (2nd c.AD), etc.)}}: the form **τέλεως**, _accusative_ τέλεων, with _plural_ τέλεῳ, is found in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1025.61, 1026.14 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC), uncertain in “Schwyzer” 734 (from Zeleia) and 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.20}}: the form **τέληον** in {{Refs|“GDI” 4963 (from Crete)}}: (τέλος):—**perfect**, of victims, **entire, without spot** or **blemish**, ἀρνῶν αἰγῶν τε τελείων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.66, compare 24.34}}; βοτὸν τ. {{Refs|“Riv.Fil.” 56.265 (from Cyrene)}}; τὸνς ϝεξήκοντα τελέονς ὄϝινς (_accusative_ _plural_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.30 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; of sacrifices, ἱερὰ τ. **perfect, of full tale** or **number**, or **performed with all rites**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47, Legal cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.97, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.60; τελέους ἀεὶ τελετὰς τελούμενος τέλεος ὄντως.. γίγνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249c; in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.247, 24.315, αἰετὸς τελειότατος πετεηνῶν}} is probably **the surest bird of augury** (compare τελήεις).
**b** in Dialects, ={κύριος}, **fully constituted, valid**, ἐν ἀγορᾷ τελείῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 324.1 (Delph., 4th c.BC), “SIG” 265 ([prev. passage]), etc.; ἀλιαίᾳ ἔδοξε τελείᾳ [prev. work]594.3 (Mycenae, 2nd c.BC)}}; **authoritative, final**, ἁ δέ κα ϝράτρα ἁ δαμοσία τελεία εἴε ¯ δικάδο ¯ σα {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 412 (from Elis)}}; τὸ θέθμιον.. τέλεον εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.47 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; so in Trag., τελεία ψῆφος a **final** decision, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 739, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 632.}}
**2** of animals, **full-grown**, τέλεον νεαροῖς ἐπιθύσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1504 (_anapaest meter_, and so perhaps αἶγες τ. in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same places])}}; ἐπ᾽ οὗ θύεται τὰ τ. τῶν προβάτων, opposed to γαλαθηνά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.183, compare “SIG” 1015.31 (Halic.), 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 44, “PCair.Zen.” 429.13, +others (3rd c.BC), “Sammelb.” 5277.5 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; τ. ζῷον defined in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.677}}; as _substantive_, τέλειον καὶ δέκα ἄρνες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1024.35 (Myconus, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; τ. ἵππος, opposed to πῶλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834c}}; τ. ἅρμα a chariot **drawn by horses**, opposed to ἅρμα πωλικόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2758 111.D2 (from Aphrodisias), “SIG” 840 (Olympia, 2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 50; τελέᾳ συνωρίδι “IG” 5(2).549.2, +others (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC); τελέῳ τεθρίππῳ [prev. work] 5; κέλητι τελέῳ [prev. work] 550.29; κέλητι τελείῳ [prev. work] 7.1772.14, compare 16}}; of trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.7.5, “POxy.” 909.18 (3rd c.AD)}}; εἰκὼν τελεία **life-sized**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 4942b7 (Crete, 2nd c.BC)}}; of a torsionengine, **full-sized**, opposed to to the model of one, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 55.30}}: of human beings, **full-grown, adult**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.4, 12, 14, “BGU” 1100.10 (1st c.BC), “POxy.” 485.30 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.10, +others}}
**2.b** **married**, τέλειοι οἱ γεγαμηκότες {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 306}}; Ἥρα Τελεία is so explained at Stymphalus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.22.2, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Aristocles Historicus 5 (cited in Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.64); see below 11.}}
**3** of persons, **accomplished, perfect in his kind**, in relation to quality, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.32,242; ἱστοριῶν συγγραφέα τέλειον “Supp.epigram” 1.400 (Samos, 2nd c.AD)}}; τ. σοφιστής {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 403e; τ. εἴς τι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 269e (_superlative_)}}; κατὰ πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 30d; πρός τι [prev. author] “Lg.” 647d, 678b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.9, etc.; ἔν τινι [prev. author] “Ep.” 4.3 (_superlative_)}}; οἱ τ. δογματικοί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.60}}; but ἡ τελεία μαῖα the **trained** or **qualified** midwife, distinguished from ἡ ἀρίστη (the trained and experienced midwife), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4.}}
**3.b** of things, φάρμακον τελεώτατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 106b}}; τ. ἀρετή, φιλία, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129b30, 1156b34, +others}}; of a syllogism in the _1st pers._ figure, the other figures being ἀτελεῖς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “APr.” 27a1, etc.; τὸ τελεώτατον ἐκεῖνο γυμνάσιον, ὂ δὴ καὶ κατασκευὴν ὀνομάζουσι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.169, compare 208: even of evils, τ. νόσημα}} a **serious, dangerous** illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.30; τελειοτάτη κακία 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.500}}; ἀδικία τελέα, τελεωτάτη, **absolute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348b, 344a}}; συνθέσεις λευκὰς τελείας δέκα τρεῖς thirteen **complete** white suits, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 10.14 (2nd c.AD)}}; τ. ἀποζυγή **complete** divorce, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.76.19 (4th c.AD)}}; ὕνις τελεία, κράβακτος ξύλινος τ., etc., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 406.19, +others (3rd c.AD)}}; of land, **fully inundated**, opposed to ἀβροχικός, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 107.13, +others (6th c.AD)}}, probably in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 286.23 (6th c.AD).}}
**4** of prayers, vows, etc., **fulfilled, accomplished**, εὐχωλαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 122.15; τέλειον ἐπ᾽ εὐχᾷ ἐσλόν [prev. author] “P.” 9.89; τελεία γένεος Οἰδίπου τ᾽ ἀρά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 832 (Lyric poetry); μὰ τὴν τ. τῆς ἐμῆς παιδὸς Δίκην [prev. author] “Ag.” 1432; τέλεα εὔγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 353 (Lyric poetry)}}; of omens or predictions, ὄψις ὀνείρου οὐ τελέη a vision **which imported** nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.121; τ. σύμβολον “hymnus ad Mercurium” 526 (assuming variant); τ. τὸ ἐνύπνιον ἀποτετέλεσται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443b.}}
**5** of numbers, **full, complete**, τελέους ἑπτὰ μῆνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 104}}; τ. ἐνιαυτός the **great** year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39d.}}
**5.b** in Arithm., of **perfect**numbers, which are equal to the sum of their divisors, as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|6 = 3 + 2 + 1; 28 = 14 + 7 + 4 + 2 + 1, [prev. author] “R.” 546b, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 23, Theo Sm.p.45 H., 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.16:—but 9 is τ. ὅτι ἐκ τελείου τοῦ γ ¯ γίνεται, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 58}} (3 is τ. because it has ἀρχή, μέσον, τέλος, {{Refs|[prev. work] 14).}}
**6** τ. κρατήρ, i.e. the **third** bowl offered to Ζεὺς Σωτήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 526, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 148.}}
**II** of the gods, **having power to fulfil prayer, all-powerful** (as implied in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 973, Ζεῦ Ζεῦ τέλειε, τὰς ἐμὰς εὐχὰς τέλει), Ζεὺς τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.115, “P.” 1.67; τ. ὕψιστον Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 28; τελέων τελειότατον κράτος, Ζεῦ [prev. author] “Supp.” 526 (Lyric poetry)}}; of Hera ζυγία, the presiding goddess of marriage (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|above 1.2 b, τέλος 1.6), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.18, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 214, “fragment” 383, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 973 (Lyric poetry)}}; of Apollo, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.22 (_superlative_)}}; of the Eumenides, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 382 (Lyric poetry); Μοῖραι “Supp.epigram” 3.400.9 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}: generally, θεοὶ τέλειοι τέλειαί τε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 167 (Lyric poetry); πῦρ τέλεον ἄρρητον “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 36.14: also ἀνὴρ τ}}. **the head** or **lord** of the house, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 972.}}
**III** ={τελευταῖος}, **last**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 948 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** τέλειον, τό, **a royal banquet**, as a translated of the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus τυκτά, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.110.}}
**V** ἡ τελεία (i.e. στιγμή) the **full point**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 630.6; so τελείαν δεῖ στίξαι 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.84 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}
**V.2** ἡ τελεία (i.e. ἀντίδοτος) the **perfect** antidote, effective against all poisons, Scrib. Larg.177.
**VI** Τέλεος (i.e. μήν(, ὁ, name of a month at Epidaurus, {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).109 ii 114.}}
**VII** _adverb_ τελέως **finally, absolutely, with full authority**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 320, 953 (both _anapaest meter_).}}
**VII.2** **completely, absolutely, thoroughly**, τ. ἐς ἀσθενὲς ἔρχεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; τ. ἐκκλησιάσαιμεν **perfectly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 329 (Lyric poetry); τ. ἄφρων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 12.4; ἔρια τ. ῥυπαρά “PCair.Zen.” 287 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. μ᾽ ὑπῆλθε **completely** deceived me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 9}}; τ. ἑστιᾶν **perfectly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.2}}; τ. κινήσεται **absolutely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 182c}}; τ. γὰρ ἡμᾶς ἐνώχλει he was a **perfect** nuisance to us, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 637.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. γυμνάζειν put a person through the τέλειον γυμνάσιον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.286}}; μέσα τ. **completely** neutral, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(2).59, compare 79, +others--This is the only form of the}} _adverb_ allowed by Thom.Mag.p.358 R., but τελείως is found in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 18, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 411d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1021b26, “PPetr.” 3p.114 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Jdt.11.6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.639, etc.}}
**VII.3** the _neuter_ τέλεον is also used as _adverb_ in later Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.8, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.56, etc.}}
**VIII** _comparative_ and _superlative_: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only τελειότατος: in Prose τελεώτερος, -ώτατος}} prevail, though the other forms occur in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097a30, 1174b22}}. _comparative_ _adverb_ τελεώτερον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520b (τελειοτέρως Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.350, variant in 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 18); τελεώτατα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 351b.}}

5048:
** τελει-όω**
and **τελεόω** (the latter always in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, and the prevailing form in _Attic dialect_ Prose, see below, and compare τέλειος [near the start])}}:— **make perfect, complete**:
**I** of things, acts, works, time, **make perfect, complete, accomplish**, πάντα ἐτελέωσε ποιήσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; τελεώσαντες τὰς σπονδάς **having completed** the libations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.32; τελειοῖ τὴν ἐνέργειαν ἡ ἡδονή 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1174b23}}; τ. βίου ἐνιαυτούς **complete the tale of** years, {{Refs|“App.Anth.” 2.281 (from Philadelphia)}}; τὸ ἔργον {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.4.34; τὰς ἡμέρας NT.Luke.2.43; τὸν δρόμον NT.Act.20.24; τὸν περὶ παιδοτροφίας λόγον ἐνθάδε τελειοῦμεν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.126}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 29.158, “Protr.” 20}}:—_passive_, **to be accomplished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.160, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1257; ἐπειδὴ χρόνος ἐτελεώθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272d}}; τελεωθέντων ἀμφοτέροισι **when** both men **had their wishes accomplished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.11.}}
**I.b** **execute** a legal instrument, **make** it **valid by completing** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PCair.Preis.” 43.10 (1st c.AD), “PAmh.” 2.111.16 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 578.21 (2nd c.AD), 1657.6 (3rd c.AD).}}
**I.2** in Logic, τ. τὸ εἶδος **complete, make perfect** the form or species, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1174a16}}:—_passive_, of syllogisms, **to be made perfect** (by reduction to the _1st pers._ figure, the other figures being ἀτελεῖς), {{Refs|[prev. author] “APr.” 29a16, 30, +others}}
**I.3** _passive_, of prophecies, **to be fulfilled,** {{NT|NT.John.19.28.}}
**II** **bring to perfection** or **consummation**, ἐπιγενόμενα δὲ ταῦτα τῷ Δαρείῳ ἐτελέωσέ μιν **sealed** his **success**, in his claim to the monarchy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86}}; τελειῶσαι λόχον **make** the ambush **successful**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1089 (Lyric poetry)}}:— _passive_, **to be made perfect, attain perfection**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1510 (_anapaest meter_)}}; especially **by reaching maturity** in point of age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 192a, “R.” 466e, 487a, 498b, etc.}}; so of the embryo, plants, **come to maturity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 776a31, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.2.6, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.33, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.531; τελειωθέντος [μειρακίου] κατὰ τὸ μέγεθος [prev. work]162.}}
**II.2** in _passive_ also, τελειωθῆναι, ={γῆμαι}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 306, compare τέλειος 1.2b, τέλος 1.6.}}
**II.3** _passive_, **to be made perfect**, of true Christians, {{NT|NT.Heb.11.40, 12.23.}}
**II.4** _passive_, **die,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.628 (from Rhegium).}}
**III** _intransitive_, **bring fruit to maturity, come to maturity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 757b24.}}

5049:
Included with: ** τέλειος**
and **τέλεος**, α, ον, in Trag., _Attic dialect_, and _Doric dialect_also ος, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 382 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 67a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1153b16, “SIG” 265 (Delph., 4th c.BC), etc.: the form τέλειος}} is alone used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, neither form in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}; τέλεος is alone used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, except in 9.110; in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ both forms occur; _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. up to the end of 3rd c.BC have only τέλεος, “IG” 12.76.39, +others, and τέλεος, τελέως, τελεῶ}} are recommended by {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Thomas Epigrammaticus Mag. p.358R.}}; τέλειος first in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 22.2314.51, +others (early 2nd c.BC)}}, frequently in Papyri {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(“PCair.Zen.” 429.13, +others (3rd c.BC), etc.), but the _neuter_ used as}} _adverb_ is sometimes τέλεον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(“BGU” 903.12 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc., τέλειον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 707.31 (2nd c.AD), etc.)}}: the form **τέλεως**, _accusative_ τέλεων, with _plural_ τέλεῳ, is found in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1025.61, 1026.14 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC), uncertain in “Schwyzer” 734 (from Zeleia) and 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.20}}: the form **τέληον** in {{Refs|“GDI” 4963 (from Crete)}}: (τέλος):—**perfect**, of victims, **entire, without spot** or **blemish**, ἀρνῶν αἰγῶν τε τελείων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.66, compare 24.34}}; βοτὸν τ. {{Refs|“Riv.Fil.” 56.265 (from Cyrene)}}; τὸνς ϝεξήκοντα τελέονς ὄϝινς (_accusative_ _plural_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.30 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; of sacrifices, ἱερὰ τ. **perfect, of full tale** or **number**, or **performed with all rites**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47, Legal cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.97, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.60; τελέους ἀεὶ τελετὰς τελούμενος τέλεος ὄντως.. γίγνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249c; in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.247, 24.315, αἰετὸς τελειότατος πετεηνῶν}} is probably **the surest bird of augury** (compare τελήεις).
**b** in Dialects, ={κύριος}, **fully constituted, valid**, ἐν ἀγορᾷ τελείῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 324.1 (Delph., 4th c.BC), “SIG” 265 ([prev. passage]), etc.; ἀλιαίᾳ ἔδοξε τελείᾳ [prev. work]594.3 (Mycenae, 2nd c.BC)}}; **authoritative, final**, ἁ δέ κα ϝράτρα ἁ δαμοσία τελεία εἴε ¯ δικάδο ¯ σα {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 412 (from Elis)}}; τὸ θέθμιον.. τέλεον εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.47 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; so in Trag., τελεία ψῆφος a **final** decision, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 739, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 632.}}
**2** of animals, **full-grown**, τέλεον νεαροῖς ἐπιθύσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1504 (_anapaest meter_, and so perhaps αἶγες τ. in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same places])}}; ἐπ᾽ οὗ θύεται τὰ τ. τῶν προβάτων, opposed to γαλαθηνά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.183, compare “SIG” 1015.31 (Halic.), 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 44, “PCair.Zen.” 429.13, +others (3rd c.BC), “Sammelb.” 5277.5 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; τ. ζῷον defined in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.677}}; as _substantive_, τέλειον καὶ δέκα ἄρνες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1024.35 (Myconus, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; τ. ἵππος, opposed to πῶλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834c}}; τ. ἅρμα a chariot **drawn by horses**, opposed to ἅρμα πωλικόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2758 111.D2 (from Aphrodisias), “SIG” 840 (Olympia, 2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 50; τελέᾳ συνωρίδι “IG” 5(2).549.2, +others (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC); τελέῳ τεθρίππῳ [prev. work] 5; κέλητι τελέῳ [prev. work] 550.29; κέλητι τελείῳ [prev. work] 7.1772.14, compare 16}}; of trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.7.5, “POxy.” 909.18 (3rd c.AD)}}; εἰκὼν τελεία **life-sized**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 4942b7 (Crete, 2nd c.BC)}}; of a torsionengine, **full-sized**, opposed to to the model of one, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 55.30}}: of human beings, **full-grown, adult**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.4, 12, 14, “BGU” 1100.10 (1st c.BC), “POxy.” 485.30 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.10, +others}}
**2.b** **married**, τέλειοι οἱ γεγαμηκότες {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 306}}; Ἥρα Τελεία is so explained at Stymphalus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.22.2, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Aristocles Historicus 5 (cited in Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.64); see below 11.}}
**3** of persons, **accomplished, perfect in his kind**, in relation to quality, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.32,242; ἱστοριῶν συγγραφέα τέλειον “Supp.epigram” 1.400 (Samos, 2nd c.AD)}}; τ. σοφιστής {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 403e; τ. εἴς τι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 269e (_superlative_)}}; κατὰ πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 30d; πρός τι [prev. author] “Lg.” 647d, 678b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.9, etc.; ἔν τινι [prev. author] “Ep.” 4.3 (_superlative_)}}; οἱ τ. δογματικοί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.60}}; but ἡ τελεία μαῖα the **trained** or **qualified** midwife, distinguished from ἡ ἀρίστη (the trained and experienced midwife), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4.}}
**3.b** of things, φάρμακον τελεώτατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 106b}}; τ. ἀρετή, φιλία, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129b30, 1156b34, +others}}; of a syllogism in the _1st pers._ figure, the other figures being ἀτελεῖς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “APr.” 27a1, etc.; τὸ τελεώτατον ἐκεῖνο γυμνάσιον, ὂ δὴ καὶ κατασκευὴν ὀνομάζουσι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.169, compare 208: even of evils, τ. νόσημα}} a **serious, dangerous** illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.30; τελειοτάτη κακία 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.500}}; ἀδικία τελέα, τελεωτάτη, **absolute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348b, 344a}}; συνθέσεις λευκὰς τελείας δέκα τρεῖς thirteen **complete** white suits, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 10.14 (2nd c.AD)}}; τ. ἀποζυγή **complete** divorce, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.76.19 (4th c.AD)}}; ὕνις τελεία, κράβακτος ξύλινος τ., etc., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 406.19, +others (3rd c.AD)}}; of land, **fully inundated**, opposed to ἀβροχικός, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 107.13, +others (6th c.AD)}}, probably in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 286.23 (6th c.AD).}}
**4** of prayers, vows, etc., **fulfilled, accomplished**, εὐχωλαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 122.15; τέλειον ἐπ᾽ εὐχᾷ ἐσλόν [prev. author] “P.” 9.89; τελεία γένεος Οἰδίπου τ᾽ ἀρά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 832 (Lyric poetry); μὰ τὴν τ. τῆς ἐμῆς παιδὸς Δίκην [prev. author] “Ag.” 1432; τέλεα εὔγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 353 (Lyric poetry)}}; of omens or predictions, ὄψις ὀνείρου οὐ τελέη a vision **which imported** nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.121; τ. σύμβολον “hymnus ad Mercurium” 526 (assuming variant); τ. τὸ ἐνύπνιον ἀποτετέλεσται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443b.}}
**5** of numbers, **full, complete**, τελέους ἑπτὰ μῆνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 104}}; τ. ἐνιαυτός the **great** year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39d.}}
**5.b** in Arithm., of **perfect**numbers, which are equal to the sum of their divisors, as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|6 = 3 + 2 + 1; 28 = 14 + 7 + 4 + 2 + 1, [prev. author] “R.” 546b, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 23, Theo Sm.p.45 H., 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.16:—but 9 is τ. ὅτι ἐκ τελείου τοῦ γ ¯ γίνεται, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 58}} (3 is τ. because it has ἀρχή, μέσον, τέλος, {{Refs|[prev. work] 14).}}
**6** τ. κρατήρ, i.e. the **third** bowl offered to Ζεὺς Σωτήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 526, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 148.}}
**II** of the gods, **having power to fulfil prayer, all-powerful** (as implied in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 973, Ζεῦ Ζεῦ τέλειε, τὰς ἐμὰς εὐχὰς τέλει), Ζεὺς τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.115, “P.” 1.67; τ. ὕψιστον Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 28; τελέων τελειότατον κράτος, Ζεῦ [prev. author] “Supp.” 526 (Lyric poetry)}}; of Hera ζυγία, the presiding goddess of marriage (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|above 1.2 b, τέλος 1.6), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.18, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 214, “fragment” 383, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 973 (Lyric poetry)}}; of Apollo, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.22 (_superlative_)}}; of the Eumenides, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 382 (Lyric poetry); Μοῖραι “Supp.epigram” 3.400.9 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}: generally, θεοὶ τέλειοι τέλειαί τε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 167 (Lyric poetry); πῦρ τέλεον ἄρρητον “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 36.14: also ἀνὴρ τ}}. **the head** or **lord** of the house, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 972.}}
**III** ={τελευταῖος}, **last**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 948 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** τέλειον, τό, **a royal banquet**, as a translated of the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus τυκτά, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.110.}}
**V** ἡ τελεία (i.e. στιγμή) the **full point**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 630.6; so τελείαν δεῖ στίξαι 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.84 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}
**V.2** ἡ τελεία (i.e. ἀντίδοτος) the **perfect** antidote, effective against all poisons, Scrib. Larg.177.
**VI** Τέλεος (i.e. μήν(, ὁ, name of a month at Epidaurus, {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).109 ii 114.}}
**VII** _adverb_ τελέως **finally, absolutely, with full authority**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 320, 953 (both _anapaest meter_).}}
**VII.2** **completely, absolutely, thoroughly**, τ. ἐς ἀσθενὲς ἔρχεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; τ. ἐκκλησιάσαιμεν **perfectly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 329 (Lyric poetry); τ. ἄφρων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 12.4; ἔρια τ. ῥυπαρά “PCair.Zen.” 287 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. μ᾽ ὑπῆλθε **completely** deceived me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 9}}; τ. ἑστιᾶν **perfectly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.2}}; τ. κινήσεται **absolutely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 182c}}; τ. γὰρ ἡμᾶς ἐνώχλει he was a **perfect** nuisance to us, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 637.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. γυμνάζειν put a person through the τέλειον γυμνάσιον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.286}}; μέσα τ. **completely** neutral, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(2).59, compare 79, +others--This is the only form of the}} _adverb_ allowed by Thom.Mag.p.358 R., but τελείως is found in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 18, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 411d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1021b26, “PPetr.” 3p.114 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Jdt.11.6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.639, etc.}}
**VII.3** the _neuter_ τέλεον is also used as _adverb_ in later Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.8, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.56, etc.}}
**VIII** _comparative_ and _superlative_: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only τελειότατος: in Prose τελεώτερος, -ώτατος}} prevail, though the other forms occur in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097a30, 1174b22}}. _comparative_ _adverb_ τελεώτερον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520b (τελειοτέρως Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.350, variant in 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 18); τελεώτατα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 351b.}}

5050:
** τελεί-ωσις**
or **τελέωσις**, εως, ἡ, **development, completion**, of physical growth, λαμβάνει τελέωσιν τὰ ᾠά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 543a19, compare 561a5, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑπταμήνου” 1, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.18, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.26; τὴν τ. τῶν μορίων ἀπολαμβάνειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 583b24, etc.}}; ἡ τῶν καρπῶν τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.4.3}}; ἕως τελειώσεως to **saturation**-point, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.38U.}}; of a building, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 246a26}}; of a statue, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.48; in moral sense, αἱ ἀρεταὶ τελειώσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 247a2, compare 246a13, “Metaphorical” 1021b20; εἰς τὴν τ. ἄγεσθαι τῆς φύσεως [prev. author] “EN” 1153a12.}}
**b** **execution** of a legal instrument **by completing** it, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1168.3 (1st c.BC), “PFlor.” 56.7, +others (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** in Logic, ἡ τ. τῶν συλλογισμῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 42a35; compare τελειόω 1.2.}}
**II** **attainment of manhood**, {{Refs|“AJA” 18.324 (Sardis, Epist. Augusti).}}
**II.b** **marriage**, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.2.2; compare τέλειος 1.2b, 11.}}
**II** of events, **accomplishment, fulfilment,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Luke.1.45; λόγων LXX.Jdt.10.9.}}

5052:
** τελεσφορ-έω**,
**bring fruit to perfection**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.7.6, NT.Luke.8.14}}:—_passive_, τελεσφορουμένων καρπῶν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.36.}}
**2** of young women, **bear perfect offspring**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.16}}; τ. καὶ μὴ ἀποβάλλειν τὸ ἔμβρυον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 2.97}}:—_passive_, of the offspring, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 34, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.41, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.220c.}}
**3** generally, **bring to a head**, ἔαρ ἐς κορυφὴν τ. [νοῦσον] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.16, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.31}} O.:—_passive_, **to be brought to perfection**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 14.3.}}
**II** **pay toll** or **custom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.5.}}
**III** **to be a** τελεσφόφος{{Refs|(111.2), “GDI” 4837 (from Cyrene), in form -φορέντες,compare “Africa Italiana” 2.130, 153 ([prev. passage]).}}

5053:
** τελευτ-άω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1029, etc.}}: _perfect_ τετελεύτηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 75e, +others:}} —_passive_, _future_ _middle_ τελευτήσομαι always in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.100, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.510, 9.511, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 370 (Lyric poetry)}}: _aorist_ ἐτελευτήθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.74}}:—**bring to pass, accomplish**, ὄφρα.. τελευτήσω τάδε ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.9; τ. ἃ μενοινᾷς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.275; ἐπὴν ταῦτα τελευτήσῃς τε καὶ ἔρξῃς 1.293, compare 2.306; γάμον τ. 24.126}}; **fulfil** an oath or promise, wish or hope, τ. ἐέλδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.200; τ. ὅσ᾽ ὑπέστης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.375; οὐ Ζεὺς ἄνδρεσσι νοήματα πάντα τελευτᾷ 18.328, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.56, 62; ὅρκια 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.29}}; τελευτᾶν τινι κακὸν ἦμαρ **bring about** an evil day for one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.524; τ. πόνους Δαναοῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.54, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1581 (Lyric poetry); οἷ τ. λόγον [prev. author] “Tr.” 1029}}; τὸ δ᾽ ἔνθεν ποῖ τελευτῆσαί με χρ; **to** what **end** must I **bring** it? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 476; Ζεὺς ὅ τι νεύσῃ, τοῦτο τελευτᾷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 979 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.:—_passive_, **to be fulfilled, come to pass, happen**, [same places] entry [near the start]; πρίν γε τὸ Πηλεΐδαο τελευτηθῆναι ἐέλδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.74; πρὶν τελευτηθῇ φόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1218.}}
**2** **finish**, σχεδίην.. ἐπηγκενίδεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.253}}; ἐπεί ῥ᾽ ὄμοσέν τε, τελεύτησέν τε τὸν ὅρκον had sworn and **completed** (made binding) the oath, {{Refs|2.378}}, etc.; ἡσύχιμον ἁμέραν τ. **close** a peaceful day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.33}}; ἄρξομαι ἐκ βολβοῖο τελευτήσω δ᾽ ἐπὶ θύννον (i.e. τὸ δεῖπνον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 173.6 (hexameters).}}
**3** especially τ. τὸν αἰῶνα **finish** life, i.e. **die**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 9.17, etc.; τ. βίον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 929, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 646 codices (sed to be read δρόμον), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 419, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 351b}}; ὑπ᾽ ἄλλου τ. τὸν βίον, i.e. to be killed, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 870e: also (after the analogy of παύομαι) with _genitive_, τελευτᾶν τοῦ ἀνθρωπίνου βίου}} **make an end of** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.17}}; so λόγου τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59; ἐπαίνου τ. ἐς τάδε ἔπη [prev. work] 104.}}
**3.b** frequently _absolutely_, **end life, die**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, 3.38, 40, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614b, +others}}; πρὶν τελευτήσαντ᾽ ἴδῃς before you see him **dead**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 662; τ. μάχῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 617; νούσῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.161; γήραϊ [prev. author] 6.24}}; τ. ὑπό τινος **die** by another's hand or means,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 92; δόλῳ ὑπό τινος [prev. author] 4.78; ὑπὸ αἰχμῆς σιδηρέης [prev. author] 1.39; ὑπ᾽ ἀλλαλοφόνοις χερσίν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 930 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ τῆς πληγῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877b}}; of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 667b11, “PMich.Zen.” 67.25 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _intransitive_ (as always in Prose, except in _signification_ {{Refs|1.3a):}}
**II.1** **to be accomplished**, λόγων κορυφαί [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.68 (as variant for{τελεύταθεν}); ἐλπίδες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 908 (Lyric poetry).}}]
**II.2** **come to an end**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 635, etc.: especially of Time, τελευτῶντος τοῦ μηνός, τοῦ θέρους, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4, 32, etc.: of actions, events, etc., τ. ἡ ναυμαχία ἐς νύκτα [prev. author] 1.51, etc.}}
**II.2.b** with words indicating the kind of end or outcome, ἢν ὁ πόλεμος κατὰ νόον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.45, compare 7.47}}; εὖ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 211}}; πτωχοὶ τ. **end by being** beggars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 552c}}; οὕτως τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.110, 138}}; τ. ἔς τι **come to a** certain **end, issue in**, αἱ εὐτυχίαι ἐς τοῦτο ἐτελεύτησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.125}}; τ. ἐς τὠυτὸ γράμμα **end in** the same letter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.139, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, 4.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618a}}; εἰς ἄνδρας ἐκ μειρακίων τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 173b}}; ποῖ (ἐς τί) τελευτᾶν (φασι); **came to** what **end?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 735 (troch.), compare “Ch.” 528, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 630b; also τ. ἐπί τι [prev. author] “R.” 510d, “Smp.” 211c.}}
**II.3** **die**, see above {{Refs|1.3b.}}
**II.4** the _participle_ τελευτῶν, ῶσα, ῶν, is used with Verbs like an _adverb_, **to finish with, at the end, at last**, as τελευτῶν ἔλεγε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.75}}; κἂν ἐγίγνετο πληγὴ τελευτῶσα there would have been a fray **to finish with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 261; τελευτῶν.. ἐξεβλήθη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 524 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τὰς ὀλοφύρσεις τελευτῶντες ἐξέκαμνον **at last** they got tired of mourning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, compare 47; ἢν δέῃ τελευτῶντα τὴν στρωμνὴν ἐξαργυρῶσαι [prev. author] 8.81}}; sometimes with another _participle_, τὴν τυραννίδα χαλεπὴν τελευτῶσαν γενομένην having **at last** become.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228b; πόλεις ἐπάγοντες καὶ τελευτῶντες Λακεδαιμονίους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.60.}}
**II.5** of local limits and the like, μέχρι Σολόεντος ἄκρης, ἣ τελευτᾷ τῆς Λιβύης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32; τελευτῶντος τοῦ Λαβυρίνθου ἔχεται πυραμίς [prev. work] 148}}; τῇ ἡ Κνιδίη χώρη ἐς τὴν ἤπειρον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.174, compare 2.33, 4.39, “IG” 12.900, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 75e.}}

5054:
** τελευτ-ή**, ἡ,
(τελέω) **completion, accomplishment**, τελευτὴν ποιῆσαι [γάμου] accomplish, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.249, 16.126; κραίνειν τελευτὰν γάμου 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.66}}; τ. νόστου{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.35; οὐ γάρ μοι δοκέει μύθοιο τ. τῇδέ γ᾽ ὁδῷ κρανέεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.625.}}
**2** **event, issue**, δεῖξεν πᾶσαν τ. πράγματος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.75, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1075}}; γάμου πικραὶ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 745 (Lyric poetry); τ. πρευμενεῖς κτίσαι [prev. author] “Supp.” 138 (Lyric poetry); θεσφάτων [prev. author] “Pers.” 740 (troch.); κακοῦ θυμοῦ τ... κακὴ προσγίγνεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1198.}}
**3** **termination, end**, οὐδέ τις ἦν ἔριδος λύσις οὐδὲ τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 637; μή μ᾽ ἀποσβεσθὲν λάθῃ πρὸς τῇ τ. τῆς ὁδοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 294 (Lyric poetry); ἡ τ. τοῦ πολέμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13; καλλίστην τελευτὴν ἐπιτιθέντες κινδύνοις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.47; τελευτὴν ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 782a.}}
**4** especially βιότοιο τ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.104, 16.787; βίου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.24; τ. τοῦ βίου τελεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 79; ἐπὶ τελευτῇ τοῦ βίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516a.
**4.b** frequently without βίου, **the end of life, death**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118, etc.; τ. ὑστάτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1256}}; τελευτῆς λαχεῖν, τυχεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.6; τ. δοῦναι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.7.3}}; _periphrastic_, θανάτοιο τ. the **end** that is death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 357, compare τέλος 1.4; τῆς γηραιοῦ τ. προαποθνῄσκειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2.}}
**5** with Preps., in _adverb_ sense, ἐς τελευτήν **at the end, at last,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Hymni Homerici 7.29, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 333, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 201, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1223 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ τελευτῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267d, etc.; ἐν τελευτᾷ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.26, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 936 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **end, extremity** of anything, as of limbs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 654b24, compare 685a1, “GA” 720b18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 33b, “Men.” 75e.}}
**II.2** **end, close** of a sentence, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1420b2, etc.}}; of a play, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Po.” 1450b29, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 257}}; of a word, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.6.1.}}

5055:
** τελέω**,
_Epic dialect_ also **τελείω**, both in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} (τελέοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.262, compare 4.776, +others, τελείει 6.234, 23.161)}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ τέλεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.373, 768; ἐτέλειον 9.456, 15.593}}; _Ionic dialect_ τέλεσκον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 123, “fragment” 434; τελέεσκον 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 8.213}}: _future_ τελέσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.3, (δια-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425e codices, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.66 codices (-τελῶ Cobet in both places), “PAvrom.” 2 A 9 (1st c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ also τελέω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.415, 12.59, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.256, etc.}}; _Attic dialect_ τελῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1435, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311b}}: _aorist_ (ἐ) τέλεσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.390}}; _Epic dialect_ τέλεσσα and ἐτέλεσσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 246, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.222, 23.543, 559, +others}} (_infinitive_ τελέσσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.9)}}; _Attic dialect_ ἐτέλεσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.78, etc.}}: _perfect_ τετέλεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20a, (δια-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.203}}:—_middle_, _future_ (see. below): _aorist_ ἐτελεσάμην {{Refs|[prev. author] 38.18, etc.}}; _perfect_ τετέλεσμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 11.34 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἐτελείετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.5}}: _future_ τελεσθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16.12}}; _future_ _middle_ in this sense, τελεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 929, “Ag.” 68 (_anapaest meter_), etc., τελέεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.36, τελεῖσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.284}}; _participle_ τελεόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206, τελεύμενος [prev. author] 3.134}}: _aorist_ ἐτελέσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.663, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ τελέσθην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.4}}: _perfect_ τετέλεσμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.74, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ τετέλεστο {{Refs|19.242}}: Cretan dialect _perfect_ _participle_ τετελημένος {{Refs|“GDI” 4963}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _perfect_ τετέληνται uncertain in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1024.22 (Myconus, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}: (τέλος):—**fulfil, accomplish, execute, perform**, frequently in Poets from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards, less frequently in Prose (except in significations 11 and 111); τελέσαι ἔργον τε ἔπος τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.272, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.108, 523, etc.; τ. φιλοτήσια ἔργα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.246}}; μ᾽ ἔφαντο ἄξειν εἰς Ἰθάκην, οὐδ᾽ ἐτέλεσσαν but **did it** not,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.212; τ. ἀέθλους 3.262; πόνον 23.250; πύματον δρόμον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.373; ὁδόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.256, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 11; sometimes without ὁδόν, ἄτερ καμάτοιο τέλεσσαν ἤματι τῷ αὐτῷ καὶ ἀπήνυσαν οἴκαδ᾽ ὀπίσσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.325; ὁδῷ δὲ τὰ ξυντομώτατα ἐξ Ἀβδήρων ἐς Ἴστρον ἀνὴρ εὔζωνος ἑνδεκαταῖος τελεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97}}; ταύτῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ ἐς Φάρσαλον ἐτέλεσε {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.78}}; κίνδυνον τελέσσαι **perform** a dangerous feat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99; ἔργον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1399; δίδυμα κακά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 782 (Lyric poetry); προστάγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 926a}}, compare d:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206}}; καὶ εἰ τετελεσμένον ἐστί, ={τελεῖσθαι δύναται}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.90, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.196; τετέλεστο δὲ ἔργον 7.465}}; αὐτίκ᾽ ἔπειθ᾽ ἅμα μῦθος ἔην, τετέλεστο δὲ ἔργον 'no sooner said than done',{{Refs 5th c.BC+|19.242; ἐάνπερ ἐπὶ λόγῳ ἔργα τελῆται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 389d, compare “Plt.” 288c; γραφὴ τῶν τετελεσμένων ἔργων “PPetr.” 3p.340 (3rd c.BC)}}; τετέλεσται {{NT|NT.John.19.30 (compare 28).}}
**2** **fulfil** one's word, τ. ἔπος, μῦθον, ὑπόσχεσιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.44, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.776, 10.483; τελέω τὰ πάροιθεν ὑπέστην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.20}}; τελέσαι κότον, χόλον, **glut** one's fury, wrath, {{Refs|1.82, 4.178: also}}**grant** one **the fulfilment** or **accomplishment of** anything, τ. νόον τινί **fulfil** his wish,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.149, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.51; τ. ἐέλδωρ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 36; λιτάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 627 (Lyric poetry); κατάρας [prev. work] 724 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _infinitive_, οὐδ᾽ ἐτέλεσσε φέρων δόμεναι **he succeeded** not in.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.222 (compare ἀνύω 1.6)}}:—_passive_, **to be fulfilled**, {{Refs|2.36, 330, +others: especially}}_perfect_ _participle_, [μῦθος] τετελεσμένος ἐστί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.388, compare “hymnus ad Venerem” 26; elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only _neuter_, τὸ δὲ καὶ τετελεσμένον ἔσται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.212, compare 8.286, +others:}} —_middle_, τελέσασθαι δίκην **bring** a suit **to issue**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.18, compare 39.18 (_passive_).}}
**3** **grant in full, work out**, ἀγαθόν τινι, ὅ τι φρεσὶν ᾗσι μενοινᾷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.34; νόστον 15.112; μόγις δ᾽ ἐτέλεσσε Κρονίων 3.119; τ. λυγρά 18.134; γῆρας ἄρειον 23.286; κακὰ κήδεα τ. τινί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.8, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.699, 18.389, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; θεῶν τελεσάντων (i.e. αὐτό) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.49; εὖ τελεῖ θεός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 35.}}
**4** ὅρκια τελεῖν **make** an oath **effective**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.69}}: later, **execute** a legal document, δημόσιος χρηματισμὸς τετελεσμένος δι᾽ ἐπιτηρητῶν ἀγορανομίας Mitteis {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Chr.” 200.10 (3rd c.AD),compare “POxy.” 290.22 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**5** **bring to fulfilment** or **perfection**, ἀρετὰν.. πεπρωμέναν τελέσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.43}}; τ. τινά **bless** him **with perfect happiness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).46 (uncertain); so τετελεσμένον ἐσλόν [prev. author] “N.” 9.6; τελεσθεὶς ὄλβος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 751 (Lyric poetry)}}: also, **bring** a child **to maturity, bring** it **to the birth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 100 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5.b** with an _adjective_ added, ἅπαντας ἡ παίδευσις ἡμέρους τελεῖ **makes perfectly** gentle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 41.}}
**6** **bring to an end, finish, end**, ἐξ ἀγαθοῦ γὰρ ἀρξάμενος τελέω τὸν λόγον εἰς ἀγαθόν {{Refs|“Lyric poetry Alex. Adesp.” 21.2.}}
**7** of Time, ὅτε δὴ τρίτον ἦμαρ.. τέλεσ᾽ Ἠώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.390}}; βίον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 36, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1114}}; πολλοὺς τρόχους ἡλίου{{Refs|[prev. work] 1065; τελευτὴν τοῦ βίου [prev. author] “Tr.” 79}}; also τ. νοῦσον **come to the end of** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 799}}:—_passive_, περὶ δ᾽ ἤματα μακρὰ τελέσθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.470, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 59; τετελεσμένον εἰς ἐνιαυτόν [prev. work] 795}}; ἐν τοῖς ἔτεσι τοῖς δὶς ἑπτὰ τετελ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 581a14, compare “Metaphorical” 994a26}}; of men, **come to one's end**, οἴμοι.. δεσπότου τελουμένου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 875 (assuming variant).}}
**8** sometimes _intransitive_, like the _passive_, **come to an end, be fulfilled, turn out**, οὐ γὰρ οἶδ᾽ ὅπῃ τελεῖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1021, compare “Pers.” 225 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1417 (Lyric poetry)}}: later ={τελέθω}, **to be**, φύσει τελῶν μνησίκακος {{Refs|12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 2.83, +others}}
**II** **pay** what one owes, what is due, λιπαρὰς τελέουσι θέμιστας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.156, 298}} (unless this means '**will administer** good laws'); νῆας.. αἵ κεν τελέοιεν ἕκαστα ἄστε᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀνθρώπων ἱκνεύμεναι **bring supplies of** everything, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.127}}: generally, **pay, present**, δῶρα, δωτίνην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.598, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.352; μισθόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.457, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 4; ἀργύριον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 208b; ἀργύριον.. μισθόν [prev. author] “Prt.” 311d; δύο δραχμὰς μισθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 173: _metaphorically_, τ. ὕμνον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.79, 2.13}}; τ. ψυχὰν Ἀΐδᾳ, i.e. die, {{Refs|[prev. author] “I.” 1.68.}}
**II.b** especially **pay** tax, duty, toll, φόρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.123a; τὰ τέλη 4th c.BC: Cratinus Junior Comicus 9.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 55.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 847b}}; τ. μετοίκιον **pay** the tax of a μέτοικος,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 850b; ἱππάδα 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.39; θητικόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.4, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.54; ξενικά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34; συντάξεις 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.91; frequently in Papyri, οἱ τελοῦντες τὰ καθήκοντα εἰς τὸ βασιλικόν “PTeb.” 5.174 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; τ. σῖτον **pay one's contribution of** corn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.21}}: _absolutely_, **pay tax,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.1.2,3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109}}:—_passive_, of money, etc., **to be paid**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.93}}; of persons, **to be in receipt of rent**, χώραν ἀτέλεστον ἔχουσιν αὐτοὶ τετελεσμένοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 55.}}
**II.2** **lay out, spend**, χρήματα μεγάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.13}}:—_passive_, **to be spent** or **expended**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.125}}; ἐς τὸ δεῖπνον τετρακόσια τάλαντα τετελεσμένα **laid out** upon the dinner, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.118}}; ἕνδεκα μυριάδας μεδίμνων τελεομένας ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρῃ ἑκάστῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 187, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 955e.}}
**II.2.b** **consume, eat** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἀναλίσκω 1.3), [σιτία] μέτρια τελεύμενα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 47, compare 26, 43, 44.}}
**II.3** since, in many Greek cities, the citizens were distributed into classes _accusative_ to their taxable property, τ. εἴς τινας meant **to belong to** a class, **to be reckoned among**, τ. ἐς Ἕλληνας, ἐς Βοιωτούς, **belong to** the Greeks, the Boeotians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 6.108}}; εἰς ἀστοὺς τ. **become** a citizen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 222}}; εἰς ἄνδρας τ. **come to** man's estate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923e}}; εἰς γυναῖκας ἐξ ἀνδρὸς τ. **become** a woman instead of a man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 822}}; ἕκαστος ἡμῶν ὑπό τινα τελεῖ δαίμονα ὃς πάσης ἡμῶν τῆς ζωῆς ἐπάρχει **belongs.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.93 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}
**II.4** from the last sense perhaps may be explained the phrase, κοῖός τις δοκέοι ἀνὴρ εἶναι πρὸς τὸν πατέρα τελέσαι **to compare** with his father, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34 (τελέσαι omitting codex E, secl. Hude).}}
**III** **initiate** in the mysteries, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 277d; τῇ μητρὶ τελούσῃ τὰς βίβλους ἀναγιγνώσκειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.259}}; τυμπανίζειν καὶ τ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.60a; τ. τῷ Διονύσῳ “Milet.” 6.23}}:—_passive_, **to have oneself initiated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 258; τετελεσμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.169 (from Cyrene), etc.}}; ἐτέλεις, ἐγὼ δ᾽ ἐτελούμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.265}}; Διονύσῳ τελεσθῆναι **to be consecrated** to Dionysus, **initiated in** his **mysteries**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79; ὀργίοισι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.10: with _accusative_, Βακχεῖ᾽ ἐτελέσθη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 357 (_anapaest meter_); τελέους τελετὰς τελούμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249c, compare 250b; also τ. μεγάλοισι τέλεσι [prev. author] “R.” 560e.}}
**III.b** in Magic, **endow** a thing **with potency, consecrate** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMag.Par.” 1.1744, “PMag.Lond.” 46.242, 121.590, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 884.}}
**III.c** **enchant**, Παλλάδιον τετελεσμένον Eust.ad {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 620.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, τελεσθῆναι στρατηγός **to be formally appointed** general, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.19}}; τετελεσμένος σωφροσύνῃ **a votary of** temperance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.12.}}
**III.3** also of sacred rites, **perform**, ἱερά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 485, compare “IT” 464 (_anapaest meter_); θυσίαν τοῖς θεοῖς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.34, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 16; ὄργια “IG” 14.1183 (from Rome), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.14.1}}; γάμον, γάμους, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 14, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1387}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 775a.}}
**III.4** _passive_, of women, **to be married**, {{Refs|“GDI” 3721.5,9 (from Cos).}}

5056:
** τέλος**, εος, τό,
(τέλλομαι, τέλλω) **coming to pass, performance, consummation**, εἰ γὰρ ἐπ᾽ ἀρῇσιν τ. ἡμετέρῃσι γένοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.496; ἐν [θεοῖς] τ. ἐστὶν ὁμῶς ἀγαθῶν τε κακῶν τε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 669}}; δίκη δ᾽ ὑπὲρ ὕβριος ἴσχει ἐς τ. ἐξελθοῦσα issuing in **fulfilment, execution**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 218; καθάπερ ἐκ δίκης κατὰ νόμον τ. ἐχούσης “PEleph.” 1.12 (4th c.BC),compare “IG” 12(7).67.48 (Arcesine, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; καθήκει νῦν [τὰν γνώμαν] ἐπὶ τέλος ἀχθῆμεν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 793.7 (Halasarna, 1st c.AD)}}; ἔλπομαι μέν, ἐν θεῷ γε μὰν τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.105, compare “N.” 8.45, 10.29, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.193; ἢν θεὸς ἀγαθὸν τ. διδῷ αὐτῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.29; ἐν πείρᾳ τ. διαφαίνεται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.70; ψευστήσεις, οὐδ᾽ αὖτε τ. μύθῳ ἐπιθήσεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.107, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.71, 6.77}}; **result**, τ. δ᾽ οὔ πώ τι πέφανται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.122; εἵως κε τ. πολέμοιο κιχείω 3.291; ἐν γὰρ χερσὶ τ. πολέμου 16.630; ἶσον τείνειεν πολέμου τ. 20. 101, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 638 (but ἢ πολέμοιο ἢ λοιμοῖο τ. ποτιδέγμενοι}} the **coming to pass (outbreak**) of.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1282)}}; τί μὰν ἀφήσει τ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1468 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί ἔσται τὸ τ. τῶν γιγνομένων τούτων ἐμο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.50}}; ἀποίητον.. θέμεν ἔργων τ. undo things done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.17}}; ὁδοῦ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1400}}; φόνου τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.834; τοῦ δ᾽ ὔμμι τέλος κρηῆναι ἔοικεν [prev. author] 3.172}}; τῷ τ. πίστιν φέρων the **outcome**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 735}}; Ζεὺς πάντων ἐφορᾷ τ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.17; ἀκόλουθον τὸ τ. ἐξέβη τοῦ κινδύνου ταῖς ἐπιβολαῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.11.9; ἀμφίδοξα τὰ τ. τῶν κινδύνων αὐτοῖς ἀπέβαινε [prev. author] 18.28.11, compare 18.32.12, 3.5.7; τ. τοιόνδε ἐγένετο τῆς μάχης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.22, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.61.2}}; μάχης.. κεκύρωται τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 874}}; διὰ μάχης ἥξω τέλους, ={διὰ μάχης ἥξω}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 475}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα μαχᾶν τάμνειν τ. to seek to determine the **issue** of the battles in both directions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.57}}; more generally, **event**, οὐ γὰρ ἔγωγέ τί φημι τ. χαριέστερον εἶναι ἢ ὅτε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.5}}: in concrete sense, **result, product**, τ. εὐπεψίας αἱματικῆς πιμελὴ καὶ στέαρ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 672a4, compare “GA” 725b8.}}
**2** in contexts like {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 669, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.630 (see. above)}}, τ. can be understood as **power of deciding, supreme power**, and so we have τ. μὲν Ζεὺς ἔχει.. πάντων ὅσ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.1; ἐν δ᾽ ἐμοὶ τ. αὐτοῖν γένοιτο τῆσδε τῆς μάχης πέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 423; [Ἄπολλον].. ὅθεν πολεμόκραντον ἁγνὸν, τ. ἐν μάχᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 162 (Lyric poetry); τελέων τελειότατον κράτος, ὄλβιε Ζεῦ [prev. author] “Supp.” 525 (Lyric poetry); τ. ἔχει δαίμων βροτοῖς, τ. ὅπᾳ θέλει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1545 (Lyric poetry); τ. δ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἡμῖν, εἴτε.. εἴτε.. [prev. author] “Hel.” 887}}; καὶ τοῖσ᾽ (i.e. ἰητροῖς) οὐδὲν ἔπεστι τ. they have no **power** or **efficacy**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.58: and in the civil sphere, τ. ἔχειν}}, of persons, to have **the power to ratify,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.57.25, Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322b13}}; ὅ τι ἂν δόξῃ τοῖς πλείοσι τοῦτ᾽ εἶναι τ. the decision of the majority must be **final**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1317b6}}; κύριος ἔστω ἐπιβάλλειν κατὰ τὸ τ. shall have authority to inflict a fine up to the limit of his **powers**, Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.75; κατὰ τὸ τ. ζημιοῦσθαι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.11}}; τοῖς κατ᾽ ἐμπορίαν παραγιγνομένοις μηδὲν ἔστω τ. πλὴν ἐπὶ κήρυκι ἢ γραμματεῖ Foed. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.22.8}}; τ. ἔχειν, of things, to have **decisive** or **final authority**, σφῷν μὲν ἐντολὴ Διὸς ἔχει τ. δή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 13}}; ἡ.. τούτου αἰτίασις οὐκ ἔχει τ. has no **validity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.89}}; πρὶν τ. τι αὐτῶν ἔχειν before any of the terms had **validity**, i.e. had been ratified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.41, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.27}}; τοῦ ζῆν καὶ μὴ ζῆν τὸ τ. ἐστὶν ἐν τῷ ἀναπνεῖν the **decisive difference** between.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 480b19.}}
**3** **magistracy, office**, τ. δωδεκάμηνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.9 (δυω- codices)}}; οἱ ἐν τ. men in **office, magistrates**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1352, “Ph.” 385, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; ἔξω τῶν βασιλέων καὶ τῶν μάλιστα ἐν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.10, compare 6.88; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, 9.106; poetry, οἱ ἐν τέλει βεβῶτες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 67}}; οἱ τὰ τ. ἔχοντες Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47; ὂρ μέγιστον τ. ἔχοι “Schwyzer” 409.3 (Elis, 5th c.BC); τοὺς.. τὸ ὁροφυλακικὸν τ. ἔχοντας “SIG” 633.94 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ τ. the **government**, τοιαῦτ᾽ ἔδοξε τῷδε Καδμείων τέλει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1030}}; τὰ τ. the **magistrates**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus (with a _masculine_}}_participle_ and _plural_ (variant) verb) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.58, 4.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.4.}}
**4** **decision, doom**, Ζεὺς.. οἶδε, ὁπποτέρῳ θανάτοιο τ. πεπρωμένον ἐστί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.309; Κῆρες δὲ παρεστήκασι.., ἡ μὲν ἔχουσα τ. γήραος ἀργαλέου, ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρη θανάτοιο 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 2.6}}; μήτηρ.. μέ φησι διχθαδίας Κῆρας φερέμεν θανάτοιο τέλος δέ (or τέλοσδε) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.411, compare 13.602}}; ἐξέφυγον θανάτου τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 6.3; τ. θανάτου ἀλεείνων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.326; τ. θανάτοιο κάλυψεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.553; οὐδέ κέ μ᾽ ὦκα τ. θανάτοιο κιχείη 9.416, compare 11.451; ἡμετέρου θανάτοιο κακὸν τ., οἷον ἐτύχθη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.124, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 906 (Lyric poetry)}}:—judicial **decision**, ἀμμενῶ τ. δίκης {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 243; κύριον μένει τ. [prev. work] 544 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐκ ἔχουσα τῆς δίκης τ. not having **authority to decide** the case, {{Refs|[prev. work] 729}}; ἦ κἀπ᾽ ἐμοὶ τρέποιτ᾽ ἂν αἰτίας τ.; will you submit the **decision** of this case to me? {{Refs|[prev. work] 434}}; τὸ τ. κρίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 768b; τ. ἐπιθέτω τῇ δίκῃ [prev. work] 767a, compare 761e, 957b}}; **decision** of an assembly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 603, 624}}; of a king, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 934}}; ἐξαιτράπης ἐὼν Ἰωνίης, τ. ἐποίησε τὴν γῆν εἶναι Μιλησίων probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 134b30 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ὥς τοι ἐγὼ μύθου τ. ἐν φρεσὶ θείω the **summing up** or **crux** of the matter, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.83.}}
**5** **something done** or **ordered to be done, task, service, duty**, γνῶ.. ὅ οἱ οὔ τι τ. κατὰ καίριον ἦλθεν on no fatal **errand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.439 (nisi to be read κατακαίριον); οὐδὲ μακύνων τ. οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.286}}; ὅσοις τοῦτ᾽ ἐπέσταλται τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 743, compare “Ag.” 908}}; μ᾽ Ἀπόλλων τῷδ᾽ ἐπέστησεν τέλει{{Refs|[prev. work] 1202, compare “Ch.” 760}}; ἄυπνα ὀμμάτων τέλη the wakeful **duties** (or **services**) of the eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1137 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀμφοτερᾶν τοι χαρίτων.. ζεύξω τ. the **rendering** of both services, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.6}}; αἰτουμένῳ μοι κοῦφον εἰ δοίης τ. a small **service** or **favour**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 260; ἡξῶ ναὶ τὸν Πᾶνα κακὸν τ. αὐτίκα δωσῶν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.47}}; **obligation to render a service** or **payment**, ὅτε δὴ μισθοῖο τ. πολυγηθέες ὧραι ἐξέφερον the **Payment(-day**) of the wage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.450; οἱ δ᾽ ἐλάττω τῶν ἱκανῶν κεκτημένοι, τὴν ἀναγκαίαν ἀτέλειαν ἔχοντες, ἔξω τοῦ τ. εἰσὶ τούτου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.19, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.156}}; ἐν τέλει μαθεῖν to be taught for a **fee**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.46.}}
**6** _plural_, **services** or **offerings due** to the gods, δαίμοσιν θῦσαι θέλουσα πελανόν, ὧν τέλη τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 204; ἔνθ᾽ ὁρίζεται βωμοὺς τ. τ᾽ ἔγκαρπα Κηναίῳ Διί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 238}}; ἔλιπον Ζηνὶ τροπαίῳ πάγχαλκα τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 143 (_anapaest meter_); γῇ δὲ τῇδε Σισύφου σεμνὴν ἑορτὴν καὶ τέλη προσάψομεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1382; θεοῖσι μικρὰ θύοντες τέλη [prev. author] “fragment” 327.6}}; of the Eleusinian mysteries, οὗ πότνιαι σεμνὰ τιθηνοῦνται τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1050 (Lyric poetry), compare “fragment” 837; σεμνῶν ἐς ὄψιν καὶ τ. μυστηρίων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 25}}; called μεγάλα τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560e}}; rarely in _singular_, τοῦδε μυστικοῦ τέλους this mystic **rite**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 387}}; of the marriage **rite**, τ. γάμοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.74, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1202, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.276 (Antip.)}}; γαμήλιον τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 835}}; τὰ νυμφικὰ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1241; τ. ὁ γάμος ἐκαλεῖτο 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.38, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 306, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 982, 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.7.3a.}}
**7** **service rendered** by a citizen in the Solonian constitution to the state, also his **rating according to this service**, θητικοῦ ἀντὶ τέλους ἱππάδ᾽ ἀμειψάμενος epigram cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.4}}; τιμήματι διεῖλεν εἰς τέτταρα τ. four **ratings** or **classes**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.3}}; later, τὸ τῶν ἱππέων τ., Latin _ordo_ equester, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.45, +others}}
**8** **dues** exacted by the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 658 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425d (_plural_)}}; ἀγορᾶς τ. a market-**toll**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 896}}; πορνικὸν τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.119}}; τ. πρίασθαι, πωλεῖν, farm a **tax** or let **it**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.144, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator [same place]}}; ἐκλέγειν. πράττειν, levy **it**, D.[same place], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 263.3, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.113}}; τελεῖν pay a **tax** or **duty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 847b}}; εἰ τὰ τ. τελεῖ, ποῖον τ. τελεῖ, questions put to candidates at Athens, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.4; τέλη κατατίθησιν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.77; καταβαλεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.93; frequently in Inscrr., “IG” 12.46.12, +others, “SIG” 135.14 (Olynthus, 4th c.BC), +others, and Papyri, τὸ ὡρισμένον τῆς αἰτήσεως τ., etc., “POxy.” 1473.30 (3rd c.AD),compare “PCair.Zen.” 240.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.: _metaphorically_, τέλη λύειν, see at {λύω} see 2.}}]
**9** **financial means, expenditure**, usually in _dative_ _plural_, ὃς ἂν τοῖς ἰδίοις τ. μὴ ἑαυτὸν μόνον, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὴν πόλιν ὠφελῇ by the use of his own **means**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16}}; κακῶς ἡμᾶς αὐτοὺς ποιούντων τέλεσι τοῖς οἰκείοις if we harm ourselves at our own **expense**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.60; ἀναγραψάτω.. τέλεσι τοῖς Λεωνίδου “IG” 12.56.22, compare 94.14, +others; Χερρόνησον τοῖς αὑτοῦ τ. διορύξει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.30; δημοσίοις τέλεσι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 38: in _nominative_ _sing._, μάτην γὰρ οἴκῳ σὸν τόδ᾽ ἐκβαίη τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 639.}}
**10** a **military station** or **post with defined duties** (compare _signification_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5), ἐλθεῖν εἰς φυλάκων ἱερὸν τ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.56; αἶψα δ᾽ ἐπὶ Θρῃκῶν ἀνδρῶν τ. ἷξον ἰόντες [prev. work] 470}}; δόρπον ἔπειθ᾽ ἑλόμεσθα κατὰ στρατὸν ἐν τελέεσσιν at our **posts**, in the **ranks**, {{Refs|11.730, compare 18.298}}; later, **military unit, division, squadron**, τέλει ἑνὶ τῶν ἱππέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.22, compare 4.96; πελταστῶν τέλη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 311; κατὰ τέλεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103, 7.87, +others; κατὰ τέλη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.42, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.11.6, compare 11.15.2, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 2.1.17}}; in the Roman army, **legion**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.16.2, “BJ” 1.17.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 18.56, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.87, +others}}
**10.b** **a force of** {{Refs|2048}}**infantry**, ={μεραρχία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.10, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 10.5, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 9.7.}}
**10.c** **a force of** {{Refs|2048}}**cavalry**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 7.11, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 18.4, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 20.2.}}
**II** δίρρυμά τε καὶ τρίρρυμα τέλη **troops** or **columns** of.. chariots, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 47 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of ships, τρία τ. ποιήσαντες τῶν νεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.48: also ὀρνίθων τέλεα}} **flocks** of birds, variant for{γένεα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.64; τ. ἀθανάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 151 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.12** a territorial division, Στρατικὸν τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 421.44 (Acarnania, 3rd c.BC)}}; Κορωνείων τὸ τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.354 (Thebes, 3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ Λοκρικὸν τ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 2070 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **degree of completion** or **attainment**, τόσσον μὲν ἔχον τ., οὔατα δ᾽ οὔ πω.. προσέκειτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.378}}; **degree of maturity, age**, ἐπὴν δὴ τοῦτο τ. παραμείψεται ὥρης {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 2.9; ἥβης πρὶν τ. ἄκρον ἰδεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 123; ἥβης τ. μολόντας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 920}}; εἰς ἀνδρὸς τ. ἰέναι man's **estate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 249a; εἰς πρεσβύτου τ. ἀφικομένοις [prev. author] “Epin.” 992d; τὸ τῶν παίδων τ. ἄδηλον οἷ τελευτᾷ κακίας καὶ ἀρετῆς ψυχῆς τε πέρι καὶ σώματος [prev. author] “Smp.” 181e; οὐδὲ γήρως ἔβας τ. σὺν τᾷδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 413 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.b** a **length of time** (or **space), term, course**, ἀρετάς, αἷσι Κλεωνυμίδαι θάλλοντες αἰεὶ σὺν θεῷ θνατὸν διέρχονται βιότου τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).5(23); so perhaps in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 87 (see. below 3), and in διὰ τέλους (see. below 2 c).}}
**II.2** **state of completion** or **maturity**, τ. λαβεῖν, ἔχειν, of plants or animals, to attain **maturity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276b, “Lg.” 834c, compare 899e}}: hence, **completion, end, finish**, τ. ἐπιθεῖναι τῷ λόγῳ **complete** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 186a, compare “Prt.” 348a}}; ὃ πᾶσι τοῖς προτέροις ἐπ έθηκε τ. as a **finish** to all his former acts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.140; τὸ τ. τῆς σκηνῆς ἐποιήσαντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.24; ταύτης.. τῆς ἡμέρας τοῦτο τὸ τ. ἐγένετο [prev. author] “An.” 1.10.18}}; τ. λαβεῖν to be **completed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.5; τ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 772c}}; οὐ τ. ἵκεο μύθων didst not reach the **end** of thy speech, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.56; ἐπὶ τέλους τοῦ δρόμου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613d; μέχρι τοῦ τὸ προκείμενον ἐπὶ τέλος ἀχθῆναι “PTeb.” 14.8 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἑκάστων πρὸς τέλος ἀχθέντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 108.29 (2nd c.BC),compare “BGU” 1816.11 (1st c.BC)}}; ἡ εἰκοστὴ τοῦ νοσήματος ἡμέρα τ. μὲν τριῶν ἑβδομάδων, ἓξ δὲ τετράδων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).234:}} —frequently in Adverbial phrases:
**II.2.a** τέλος **at last**, ὥστε τ. ἡσυχίαν ἦγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.100, compare 5.46}}; but most frequently at the beginning of the clause, μάχης δὲ καρτερῆς γενομένης, τέλος οὐδέτεροι νικήσαντες διέστησαν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, compare 4.131, +others; τέλος δέ [prev. author] 1.36, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1294, etc.}}; ἀλλὰ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.137; τ. μέντοι [prev. author] 5.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.30; τ. γε μέντοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 233}}; καὶ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109}}; τό γε τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740e.}}
**II.2.b** <ἐς τ. in the **end**, in the **long run**, πάντως ἐς τ. ἐξεφάνη {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.28, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37}}; εἰς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 409; θνητῶν δ᾽ εἰς τ. ὄλβιος οὐδείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 161 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40}}; ὁρῶντες τὴν Λιβύην εἰς τ. ἀβλαβῆ διαμένουσαν **altogether, completely**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.7, compare “PTeb.” 38.11 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 90.12 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), “PSI” 10.1120.5 1st c.BC-1st c.AD}}; ἐς τ. ἄνυε μοίρας uncertain reading in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.93.}}
**II.2.c** διὰ τέλους (originally perhaps from _signification_ {{Refs|1.1 or 5, or 11.1b, through the (whole)}} **performance** or **time), through to the end, completely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 275, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 685, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 270, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.24, 8.17, 19.4}}; **throughout, all the time, always**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.42, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 8.5, 3rd c.BC: Hegesippus Comicus 2.3; so διὰ τέλεος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 46 (= διὰ παντὸς καὶ ἀεί _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.618); διὰ τέλους ἀεί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 36e}}; **permanently, for good**, τοῦ ἀφεθῆναί σε διὰ τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.45 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2.d** ἐπὶ τέλος at the **end**, opposed to ἐν ἀρχῇ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.183.}}
**II.2.e** τέλει perhaps **in the end**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 198 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** especially τ. ἔχειν βίου to have reached the **end** of life, to be dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 801e; ἐμοὶ μὲν τοῦ βίου τὸ τ. ἤδη πάρεστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.6; πᾶσίν ἐστιν ἀνθρώποις τ. τοῦ βίου θάνατος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.27; εἰς τ. τοῦ ζῆν ἀφικνεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1530}}: less frequently _absolutely_, **death**, ἐλπίς ἐστι νύκτερον τ. μολεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 367 (Lyric poetry); οἱ νεηνίαι οὐκέτι ἀνέστησαν ἀλλ᾽ ἐν τέλεϊ τούτῳ ἔσχοντο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31}}; ἔχει τὸ κάλλιστον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.11}}; ἔχει τ., ={τετελεύτηκε}, Laconian phrase _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; τῶν ἤδη τ. ἐχόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717e, compare 772c, “BGU” 1857.7 (1st c.BC)}}; reversely, τ. ἔχει τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740c; οἷόν σε βίου τ. εἷλε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 735 (_anapaest meter_):}} —but ὀλβίως ἔλυσεν τὸ τ. βίου has paid life's **toll** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare(above 1.8), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1720 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ τ. ὁ χρόνος ἀπαιτεῖ poetical in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Mus.Script.” p.452 von Jan; also τ. δὲ κάμψαιμ᾽ ὥσπερ ἠρξάμην βίου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 87 (compare above 11.1b)}}; πρὶν ἂν πέλας (variant{τέλος}) γραμμῆς ἵκηται καὶ τ. κάμψῃ βίου {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 955 - 6.}}
**II.4** **end, cessation**, ὡς δὲ πρὸς τ. γόων ἀφίκοντ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1621}}; πῶς τροχηλάτου μανίας ἂν ἔλθοιμ᾽ ἐς τ. πόνων τ᾽ ἐμῶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 83}}; ὅταν δὴ πημάτων λάβῃ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 534; τ. δέχει δὴ τῶν ἐμῶν προσφθεγμά των [prev. author] “Hec.” 413; ἡ μὲν οὖν ἐπανάστασις.. τοῦτο τὸ τ. ἔσχεν “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 10.3; ἐπειδὴ οὐχ οἷόν τε εἰς ἄπειρον, τ. ἔσται πάσης φορᾶς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphorical” 1074a30.}}
**II.5** **end** of a word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 12.25, +others}}; of a sentence, ἐπὶ τέλει πρόσκειται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.43, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.20}}; of a chapter or book, ἐπὶ τέλει ἀναλυθήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.10; πρὸς τῷ τ. ῥηθήσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 957b; πρὸς τῷ τ. τοῦ ἐντέρου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 675a16}}; ἀπὸ τέλους τοῦ σταδίου, opposed to ἀπὸ μέσου, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 239b34 (compare below 111.2).}}
**III** **achievement, attainment**, τηλοῦ ἐμοὶ νόστοιο τ. γλυκεροῖο γενέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.323, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.25; τ. δὲ τῆς ἀπαλλαγῆς τοῦ Αἰθίοπος ὧδε ἔλεγον γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.139}}; πῶς ἂν καὶ τοῦτο τοῦ τ. τυγχάνοι, i.e. might be achieved, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.36.}}
**III.2** **winning-post, goal** in a race, πρὸς τ. ὀρνύμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.45}}; in a contest, ἔστιν δ᾽ ἀφάνεια τύχας καὶ μαρναμένων, πρὶν τ. ἄκρον ἱκέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).32(50)}}; εἰς τ. ἐλθεῖν, of runners in a race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613c.}}
**III.2.b** **prize**, ἔφερε πυγμᾶς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).67}}; οὐ γὰρ ἦν πενταέθλιον ἀλλ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἑκάστῳ ἔργματι κεῖτο τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “I.” 1.27; ποτὶ γραμμᾷ μὲν αὐτὰν στᾶσεκοσμήσαις, τ. ἔμμεν ἄκρον [prev. author] “P.” 9.118}} (perhaps 'to be the **winning post and prize**'); κρίνεις τ. ἀρετᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.6: _metaphorically_, οὐκ ἔχω εἰπεῖν τίνι τοῦτο Μοῖρα τ. ἔμπεδον ὤρεξε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.57.}}
**III.3** Philos., **full realization, highest point. ideal**, ἅπτεσθαι τοῦ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211b}}; πρὸς τ. ἰὼν τῶν ἐρωτικῶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 210e; πρὸς τ. ἀρετῆς ἐλθόντα [prev. author] “Clit.” 410e, compare “R.” 613c.}}
**III.3.b** the **end** or **purpose** of action, τ. εἶναι ἁπασῶν τῶν πράξεων τὸ ἀγαθόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 499e; frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “EN” 1094a18, +others}}: hence, the **final cause**, ={τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 994b9, 996a26, +others}}; hence simply ={τὸ ἀγαθόν}, the **chief good**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “EN” 1097a21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.45, etc.}}

5058:
** τελών-ιον**, τό,
**custom-house**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 13, NT.Matt.9.9, NT.Mark.2.14; τὸ τ. τῆς ἰχθυϊκῆς “OGI” 496.9 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **customs-duty**, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.27, 17.1.16, Harp. see at {δεκατευτάς}.}}]

5059:
** τέρας**, τό:
_genitive_ _Epic dialect_ αος (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus)}}, _Ionic dialect_ εος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37}}: _plural_, _nominative_ _Epic dialect_ τέραα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.394}}, _Ionic dialect_ τέρεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], τεράᾰτᾰ 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 604, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.43; τέρᾱ 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1410, but τέρᾰ 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 186; τέρα (quantity not stated) _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to Moer. p.369 P.,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1343}}; _genitive_ _Epic dialect_ τεράων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.229, τερέων 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 155; _Attic dialect_ τερῶν _accusative_ to Moer.[same place], Thom.Mag. p.348 R.}}; _dative_ _Epic dialect_ τεράεσσι {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.398, +others; later τέρασι LXX.Deu.26.8, +others: the forms τέρατ-ος, -ι, -α, -ων are Hellenistic, Moer. pp.366,369 P., Thom.Mag. p.348 R. (τέρατα LXX.Exo.4.21, +others, τεράτωνLXX.Psa.104(105).27)}}; _genitive_ _singular_ τέρως variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.26.3: lengthened for the sake of meter τείρεα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.485 (= “IG” 42(1).129.9), 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 692, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1362; τείρεσιν “hymnus ad Martem” 7; later τείρεσσι “IG” 14.2461.11 (from Massilia)}}:— **sign, wonder, marvel, portent**, ἡμῖν μὲν τόδ᾽ ἔφηνε τ. Ζεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.324}}; ᾐτέομεν δὲ θεὸν φῆναι τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.173; τοῖσιν.. θεοὶ τέραα προὔφαινον 12.394; τέρας ἧκε Κρόνου πάϊς 21.415}}; Ζεὺς δ᾽ Ἔριδα προΐαλλε.., πολέμοιοτ. μετὰ χερσὶν ἔχουσαν **a sign** of coming battle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.4}}; especially of **signs** in heaven, ἀστέρα ἧκε Κρόνου πάϊς.., ναύτῃσι τ{{Refs|. 4.76; ἴρισσιν ἐοικότες, ἅς τε Κρονίων ἐν νέφεϊ στήριξε τ. μερόπων ἀνθρώπων 11.28, compare 17.548}}; and with _passive_ Verbs, τ. φανήτω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.101}}:—so always when the first syllable is _lengthened_, {{NT+8th c.BC+|see above:—also in Prose, ἢν δὲ χειμῶνος βροντὴ γένηται, ὡς τέρας θωμάζεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28, compare 6.98; τ. πέμπειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.15; ἐφάνη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.57; ἐπιγίνεταί σφι τέρεα [prev. author] 8.37, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 744, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.73, etc.; frequently in “NT”, σημεῖα καὶ τέρατα NT.Mark.13.22, +others}}
**II** in concrete sense, **monster**, Διὸς τ. αἰγιόχοιο, of the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Gorgon 's head, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.742}}; of a serpent, {{Refs|12.209, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 302}}; δάϊον τ., of Typhoeus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 354}}; ἀπρόσμαχον τ., of Cerberus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1098}}; οὔρειον τ., of the Sphinx, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 806 (Lyric poetry)}}; ταῦρον, ἄγριον τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1214, compare 1247; ὅλον τ. ὀπτήσας.. βασιλεῖ παρέθηκε κάμηλον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 172.7 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 3.13}}; used by Cicero of Caesar, {{Refs|“Attic” 8.9.4.}}
**II.2** **monstrous birth, monstrosity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 393b, 394a, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.111, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 769b30, 773a3, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 341.13}}; ὡς ἔθρεψεν ἔκπαγλον τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 548.}}
**III** in colloquial language, τέρας λέγεις καὶ θαυμαστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 283c, compare “Tht.” 163d; τέρας λέγεις, εἰ.. [prev. author] “Men.” 91d}}; 'a marvel' of a cup, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56}}: _plural_, of incredible statements, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.74 K.}}

5062:
** τεσσᾰράκοντα** [ρᾰ],
_Attic dialect_ **τεττᾰράκοντα** {{Refs|“IG” 22.334.23}}; _Ionic dialect_ **τεσσεράκοντα** (which see); Sicilian Ionic **τετράϟοντα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 4.64 (6th c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ **τετρώκοντα** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.20, +others, “SIG” 241.67 (Delph., 4th c.BC), “IG” 5(2).357.16 (Stymphalus, 3rd c.BC), 9(1).880.15 (Corc.), compare τετρωκοντάλιτρος}} and see at {τεσσαρακοστός}; once _Doric dialect_ **τεταράκοντα** {{Refs|“IG” 4.823.63 (from Troezen)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **πετταράκοντα** (which see): οἱ, αἱ, τά, _indeclinable_:—**forty**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.524, etc.}}
**II** οἱ τ. **the Forty**, a body of justices who went round the Attic demes to hear all causes up to ten drachmae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.237}}; also cases of assault, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.33}}: changed from Thirty to “Forty” after the expulsion of the Thirty Tyrants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.1.}}

5066:
** τεταρτ-αῖος**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **τετόρταιος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 30.2}}, α, ον, **on the fourth day**, τ. γενέσθαι to be **four days** dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89; ἀφικνεῖσθαι τεταρταίους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616b.}}
**2** τ. πυρετός **quartan** fever, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 86a; ῥῖγος “POxy.” 1151.37 (5th c.AD)}}; so without πυρετός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.25, “POxy.” 1088.38 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; πυρετῷ καὶ τεταρταίῳ {{Refs|“IG” 3.1424}}; τ. πονεῖσθαι to have fits **every four days**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ κρισίων” 36; ἑπτὰ τεταρταίῳ μῆνας ἔκαμνε πυρί 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.17; λύτρα τεταρταίοιο δυσαλγέος οὕνεκα παῦσαν “Rev.Bibl.” 14.295 (from Lycia).}}
**II** τεταρταίη, ={ἡ τετάρτη}, **the fourth day**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 806.}}
**II.2** **quartan fever**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 635.}}

5067:
** τέταρτος**, η, ον,
_Epic dialect_ also **τέτρᾰτος** (which see), _Boeotian dialect_ **πέτρᾰτος** (which see), **fourth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.301, etc.}}
**II** τὸ τέταρτον as _adverb_, **the fourth time**, {{Refs|5.438, etc.: without _Article_}}**fourthly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 266e: regular}} _adverb_ -τως, **fourfold**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 86a.}}
**II.2** (i.e. μέρος) **a quarter**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.50, “POxy.” 1293.25 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** ἡ τετάρτη:
**III.1** (i.e. ἡμέρα) **the fourth day**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 800, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.21.}}
**III.2** (i.e. μοῖρα) **a liquid measure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57}}:—also, **a measure of weight**, λαβὼν χρυσοῦ τετάρτας <β> {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.V.” 6.24, compare 6.22.}}
**III.2.b** **a fourth part**, ἐπὶ τετάρταις ἐργάζονται τῶν καρπῶν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.40.}}
**III.3** **tax of**{{Refs 6th c.BC+|25%, τ. ἐπὶ τοῖς καρποῖς 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 83, compare “SIG” 4.8 (Cyzicus, 6th c.BC)}}; ὧν τετάρτη goods which pay a **tax of** 25%, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 12.59, 70, +others (3rd c.BC); τ. σιτοποιῶν [prev. work] 206.34 (3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** Τέταρτος, ὁ, a month in Locris, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 1901.2, 2097.5 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}. (Sanskrit _caturthás_, Lithuanian _ketvi[rtilde]tas_, Latin _quartus_, etc.: l.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **q[uglide]eturto-** and **q[uglide]et[uglide]ṛto-.**)

5068:
** τετράγων-ος** [ᾰ], ον,
**with four angles**, but usually **square**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.178, 181, 2.124, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 13}}; δοκοὶ τ. **squared**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.112}}; ξύλα τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.313.101, 42(1).108.162, +others (Epid., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; τ. ἐργασία, of the Hermes-statues, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.27; πρόσωπον -ότερον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 809b16}}; κύκλος τετράγωνος ταῖς ἐπιφανείαις a ring **with four surfaces**, the breadth of the outer and inner equal to the depth of the two sides, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 5.1 (with the commentary of 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 6.3).}}
**2** τὸ τ. **a square**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 510d}}, and frequently in Geometry texts, but sometimes of any **quadrilateral**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1054b2, compare 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Deff.” 100, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.166 F.}}
**2.b** in Tactics, **a body of men drawn up in square**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 12.1; τ. τάξις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125.}}
**3** τ. ἀριθμός a **square** number, i.e. a number **made up of two equal factors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147e, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 1, 15.}}
**4** **in quartile aspect**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 2.16, 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 446, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 1.16.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **square**, i.e. **perfect as a square**, χερσί τε καὶ ποσὶ καὶ νόῳ τετράγωνος τετυγμένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.2; τὸν ἀγαθὸν ἄνδρα φάναι τετράγωνον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411b27, compare “EN” 1100b21.}}
**III** ἱμάτιον τ., of the χλαῖνα **which hung square**, while the χλαμύς took a circular form, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 500}}; contrasted with the ἡμικύκλιον formed by the Roman toga, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.11.}}
**III.2** οἱ ἔμποροι καὶ οἱ τὴν τ. ἐργαζόμενοι perhaps **those who trade in the** ἀγορὰ τετράγωνος, Durrbach **Choix d'Inscrr. de Délos** {{Refs|138.}}
**IV** _adverb_ **-νως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.42.}}

5069:
** τετρᾰδ-ιον**, τό,
a **guard** of soldiers (normally consisting of four men), {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.533, NT.Act.12.4.}}
**II** **quaternion** of parchment, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 2156.10 (4th-5th c.AD), 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 9.290}}; compare τετράδειον.

5071:
** τετρᾰκόσι-οι**, αι, α,
_Doric dialect_ **τετρα-κάτιοι** [κᾰ] {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.52, etc.}}; _poetry_ (once) **tetrhkoϟsioi** {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.67 (Myrinus Epigrammaticus)}}:— **four hundred**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.178, etc.: in _sing._, τ. ἀσπίς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.10.}}
**II** οἱ τ., at Athens,
**II.1** the oligarchy established in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.69, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.7, Decrees cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.78, etc.}}
**II.2** a more ancient Council, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.13.}}

5074:
** τετρά-πους**, ὁ, ἡ, πουν, τό,
_genitive_ ποδος, _plural_ ποδα, **four-footed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 92a}}; λεία τ. a booty **of cattle**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.75.7}}; τετράποδα ζῴδια, viz. Aries, Taurus, Leo, Sagittarius, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.166: compare τετράποδος.}}
**2** τετράπουν, τό, **quadruped, beast**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 697b23, etc.: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.106, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 659, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.50, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 490a29, etc.; πάντα τὰ τ. καὶ ἑρπετὰ τῆς γῆς NT.Act.10.12.}}
**II** of things, δίφρος τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 58, “POxy.” 646 (2nd c.AD).compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 149.}}
**II.2** **of four feet** in length or area, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.372.10, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 83b; πάχος ποήσει τὸ στρῶμα τετράπουν “IG” 22.1668.14.}}

5076:
** τετράρχ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**tetrarch**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.5.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 56, “OGI” 416 (Cos, i. 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), 543.3 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; of rulers under the protection of Rome of lower grade than kings, e.g. in Palestine, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.14.1, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.12.5, +others; generally, Sall. “Cat.” 20.7, Hor. “Sat.” 1.3.12, etc.}}: also **τέτραρχος**, Θεσσαλῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 274 (Delph., 4th c.BC):}} _genitive_ **-χου** {{Refs|“OGI” 606.4 (Syria, 1st}} c.AD), but **-χα** {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.1683 (Pergam.): compare τετραρχία.}}
**II** **a leader of four** λόχοι, or 64 men, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Rev.Arch.” 3 (1934).40 (Amphipolis, 3rd-2nd c.BC), 6(1935).31 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.8, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 10.1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 9.2.}}

5078:
** τέχν-η**, ἡ,
(τέκτων) **art, skill, cunning of hand**, especially in metalworking, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.433, 6.234, 11.614}}; also of a shipwright, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.61}}; of a soothsayer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 249 (_plural_, Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 17, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 389, etc.; τέχναι ἑτέρων ἕτεραι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.25; ὤπασε τ. πᾶσαν [prev. author] “O.” 7.50.}}
**2** **craft, cunning**, in bad sense, δολίη τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.455, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 160}}: _plural_, **arts, wiles**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.327.332, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 496, 929; δολίαις τέχναισι χρησάμενος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.58}}; τέχναις τινός by his **arts** (or simply by his **agency**), {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 9.52, “P.” 3.11}}; τέχνην κακὴν ἔχει he has a bad **trick**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 770, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).35(53), S “Ph.” 88, etc.}}
**3** **way, manner**, or **means whereby a thing is gained**, without any definite sense of art or craft, μηδεμιῇ τ. in no **wise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112}}; ἰθέῃ τ. straight**way**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.57}}; πάσῃ τ. by all **means**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1323, “Th.” 65, “Ec.” 366}}; παντοίᾳ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 752, etc.; οὐκ ἀποστήσομαι.. οὔτε τ. οὔτε μηχανῇ οὐδεμιᾷ “IG” 12.39.22; πάσῃ τ. καὶ μηχανῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.16; μήτε τ. μήτε μηχανῇ μηδεμιᾷ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.95.}}
**II** **an art, craft**, πᾶσαι τέχναι βροτοῖσιν ἐκ Προμηθέως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 506, compare “IG” 12.678}}; τὴν τ. ἐπίστασθαι to know the **craft**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130; φλαύρως ἔχειν τὴν τ. [prev. passage]; τῆς τ. ἔμπειρος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 811}}; ταύτην τέχνην ἔχει he makes this his **trade**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.16, compare 6.7}}; ἐν τῇ τ. εἶναι practise **it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317c}}; ἐπὶ τέχνῃ μαθεῖν τι to learn a thing **professionally**, opposed to ἐπὶ παιδείᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 312b, compare 315a; τέχναι καὶ ἐργασίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.1}}; τέχνην τὸ πρᾶγμα πεποιημένοι having made **a trade** of it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.53}}; τέχνας ἀσκεῖν, μελετᾶν, ἐργάζεσθαι, to practise **them**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.26, 41 (_passive_), “Oec.” 4.3; πατρῴαν τέχναν ἐργάζεσθαι ἁλιεύεσθαι Πρακτικὰ Ἀρχ. Ἑτ. 1932.52 (Dodona, 4th c.BC)}}; ἰατρὸς τὴν τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 40.5 (2nd c.AD)}}; τεθεραπευκὼς ἀνεγκλήτως τῇ τ., of a barber, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 47.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; παραμενῶ πρὸς ὑπηρεσίαν τῆς τ. (_i.e. _ weaving) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7358.20 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἀπὸ τεχνῶν τρέφεσθαι live by **them**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 7.1.}}
**III** **an art** or **craft**, i.e. **a set of rules, system** or **method of making** or **doing**, whether of the useful arts, or of the fine arts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 171.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a11, “EN” 1140a8; ἡ ἐμπειρία τέχνην ἐποίησεν, ἡ δ᾽ ἀπειρία τύχην Polus cited in [same work] “Metaphorical” 981a4}}; ἡ περὶ τοὺς λόγους τ. the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Art” of Rhetoric, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 90b}}; οἱ τὰς τ. τῶν λόγων συντιθέντες **systems** of rhetoric, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271c, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.50 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others}}; hence title of various treatises on Rhetoric (see. VI; but rather **tricks** of Rhetoric, in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.117)}}; τέχνῃ **by rules of art**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 282d; ἢ φύσει ἢ τέχνῃ [prev. author] “R.” 381b; τέχνῃ καὶ ἐπιστήμῃ [prev. author] “Ion” 532c}}; ἄνευ τέχνης, μετὰ τέχνης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 89e}}: τ. defined as ἕξις ὁδοποιητική, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.20, cf. Cleanth. [prev. work]1.110.}}
**IV** ={τέχνημα}, **work of art, handiwork**, κρατῆρες.., ἀνδρὸς εὔχειρος τέχνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 472; ὅπλοις.., Ἡφαίστου τέχνῃ [prev. author] “fragment” 156, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.14, “PLond.” 3.854.4 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.25.1, +others}}
**V** ={συντεχνία, ἡ τ. τῶν λιθουργῶν, τῶν σακκοφόρων}, Dumont-Homolle “Mélanges d' archéol. et d' épigr.” {{Refs|p.378 No. 65, 66 (from Perinthus); τ. βυρσέων, συροποιῶν, “IGRom.” 1.717,1482 (both Philippopolis)}}; τοὺς καταλειπομένους ἀπὸ τῇς τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1572.12 (2nd c.AD)}}; ὁ χαλκεὺς ἀπὸ τῆς τ. {{Refs|“SIG” 1140 (from Amphipolis).}}
**VI** **treatise** on Grammar, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus at Title Page, or on Rhetoric, Anaximenes Lampsacenus at Title Page}}

5079:
** τεχνῑτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**artificer, craftsman**, opposed to γεωργός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262b26, +others}}; opposed to ῥήτωρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus cited in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 11}}; of a potter, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 500.2, 3 (3rd c.BC)}}; τεχνῖται οἱ χρήσιμόν τι ποιεῖν ἐπιστάμενοι, opposed to οἱ ἐλευθερίως πεπαιδευμένοι, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.4, 5, cf. NT.Act.19.24: _metaphorically_, πόλις ἧς τ. καὶ δημιουργὸς ὁ θεός NT.Heb.11.10, cf. LXX.Wis.13.1.}}
**II** **one who does** or **handles** a thing **by the rules of art, skilled workman**, opposed to ἄτεχνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 219a, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1397b23, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.155, 18(2).245}}; opposed to ἰδιώτης, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.204}}; opposed to ὁ ἔμπειρος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 981b31}}; with _genitive_ _of things_, τ. τῶν πολεμικῶν **skilled** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 13.5}}; also οἱ περὶ τοὺς θεοὺς τ. **persons versed** in religious practices, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.3.11}}; ἄνθρωπος τ. λόγων, as a sneer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.170}}; οἱ Διονυσιακοὶ τ. or οἱ περὶ τὸν Διόνυσον τ., theatrical **artists**, musicians as well as actors, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.192}} (where τ. alone), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1405a24, “Pr.” 956b11, “SIG” 399.12 (Amphict. Delph., 3rd c.BC), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2619, +others (from Cyprus), “OGI” 50 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.21.8, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.}}; so perhaps in οἷος τ. παραπόλλυμαι, = Latin _qualis artifex pereo_ (Nero's last words), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 63.29.}}
**III** **trickster, intriguer**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 13.5.}}

5080:
** τήκω**,
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 300.5, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ **τάκω** [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 123 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.28}}: _future_ τήξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.277 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), (συν-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 398 (troch.)}}; _Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ταξεῖς (κατα-) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 6.1}}: _aorist_ ἔτηξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.96, (κατ-) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.206, etc.:}}_perfect_ τέτηκα, in _intransitive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.176, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ τέτᾱκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1141 (Lyric poetry), (προσ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 836 (Lyric poetry)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐτετήκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.15:}} — _middle_, _future_ τήξομαι (but in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 12}}: _aorist_ ἐτηξάμην {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 63, 164, 350}}:—_passive_, _future_ τᾰκήσομαι {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Lev.26.39, +others, “Anacreontea” 10.16, (συν-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.752e:}}_aorist_ ἐτάκην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251b, “Ti.” 83a}}; frequently in compounds ἐξ-, ἐν-, συν-; rarely ἐτήχθην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 61b}}, once in Trag., συντηχθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1029 (Lyric poetry)}}: _perfect_ τέτηγμαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.106d, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.272 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; but in early Gr. the _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_ are supplied by the _intransitive_ _active_ _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ τέτηκα, ἐτετήκειν (see. above).
**I** _active_, **melt, melt down** (_transitive_), of metals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.96, etc.}}; τ. πετραίαν χιόνα A.[same place]; **bring** clouds **down in rain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25}}; **dissolve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60e, 84d, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.523, etc.}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, **dissolve, cause to waste** or **pine away**, μὴ θυμὸν τῆκε **let** it not **melt** or **pine away**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.264}}; τίν᾽ αεὶ τάκεις ὧδ᾽ ἀκόρετον οἰμωγὰν τὸν Ἀγαμέμνον; (i.e. τί ὧδε τήκει οἰμώζουσα τὸν Ἀγ.;) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 123 (Lyric poetry); τ. βιοτάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 141 (_anapaest meter_); σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 609c}}; τ. καὶ λείβει [τὸ θυμοειδές{{Refs|] [prev. work] 411b; τ. ἧπαρ Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2079.8}}; διαφορεῖν καὶ τ. [σάρκα] carry off and **reduce** superfluous flesh, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.96, compare Vict. _Attic dialect_ 1; ἡ ταχεῖα κίνησις τὴν θερμασίαν ἐπὶ πλέον αὐξάνουσα τήκει τὸ σῶμα [prev. author] 15.191; ἔρωτες τήξουσιν κραδίην “Anthologia Graeca” 5.277 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** _passive_, with _intransitive_ _perfect_ _active_ τέτηκα, **melt, be dissolved, melt away**, of snow, **thaw**, χιὼν τηκομένη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.207; ῥέειν ἀπὸ τηκομένης χιόνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22; λευκῆς τακείσης χιόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 3; ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν τακῇ χιών [prev. author] “fragment” 228.4; τὴν χιόνα τετηκέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.15}}; of metals, ἐτήκετο κασσίτερος ὥς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 862; σίδηρος.. πυρὶ κηλέῳ τήκεται [prev. work] 866}}; also τετηκότα (i.e. κρέα) **sodden** flesh, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 246}}; ἄλφιτα πυρὶ τ. **is consumed**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.18}}; τήκεται κοιλίη, merely, **is relaxed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}}“Aër.” 7; of putrefying flesh, **fall away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 82e}}; of a corpse, κατθανὼν ἐτήκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 906}}; κηκὶς μηρίων ἐτήκετο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1008; πυρὸς τετακότας σποδῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1141 (Lyric poetry)}}; εἰς τοῦτο τετηκυῖα **resolved** into.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85d; στοιχεῖα καυσούμενα τήκεται NT.2Pet.3.12}}; of fat, τακείσης πιμελῆς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.192, compare 18(2)}}.140; of food in the digestive organs, τήκεται μὲν ἡ πρότερον ῥηθεῖσα [πτισάνη], ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα δύστηκτός ἐστι {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.784.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **melt** or **waste away, pine**, κλαίουσα τέτηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.176; τήκετο χρώς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.204}}; τήκετο καλὰ παρήϊα δάκρυ χεούσης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 208; ἐν νούσῳ.. δηρὸν τηκόμενος 5.396; τ. νούσῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.99, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.66, 82, etc.}}; Ὀδυσσεὺς τήκετο **was moved to tears**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.522; κλαίω, τέτηκα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 283; μὴ λίαν τάκου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 159 (Lyric poetry); ψυχὴν ἐτήκου [prev. author] “Heracl.” 645, compare “El.” 208 (Lyric poetry); ἐτάκευ βασκαίνων 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.12; τὸ κάλλος ἐτάκετο [prev. author] 2.83}}; **come to naught**, δόξαι.. τακόμεναι κατὰ γᾶν μινύθουσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 374 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐπί τινι τακείς **consumed for love** of.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.31 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 12.1}}; βλέμμα τηκόμενον a **languishing** look, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 53}}. (Cf. Latin τᾱβες, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **pawian** 'thaw', Slavonic _tajati_ 'melt'.)

5081:
Included with: ** τηλαυγ-ής**, ές,
(τῆλε, αὐγή) **far-shining, far-beaming**, πρόσωπον, of the sun, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 31.13; εἵματα, of the moon, [prev. work] 32.8}}; φάος, φέγγος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.75 (_comparative_), “N.” 3.64}}; ἀκτίς, ἀκτίνων σέλας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1092 (Lyric poetry), 1711; στέφανοι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.6}}; πρόσωπον θέμεν τ. to make it **beam from afar**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 6.4: _metaphorically_, τ. νοῦς}} **luminous** meaning, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 30; σαφεῖς καὶ τ. αἰτίαι 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.174d; λέξεις ἐπὶ τὸ -έστερον ἀχθεῖσαι 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “Prooem.”}}
**II** of distant objects, **far-seen, conspicuous**, σκοπιή {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 550; κορυφά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 7.12; φᾶρος 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.5; ὄχθος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 524 (Lyric poetry)}}; of leprosy, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.13.4, +others}}
**III** **far-seeing**, αἴσθησις, ψυχή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 22 (_comparative_).}} _adverb_ -γῶς, τηλαυγέστερον ὁρᾶν to see **more clearly**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.50, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.30, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.540, NT.Mark.8.25.--poetical word, used in late Prose: δηλαυγῶς seems to be a different word.}}

5082:
** τηλῐκόσδε**, ήδε, όνδε,
and **τηλῐκοῦτος**, αύτη, οῦτον (also τηλικοῦτος as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 751, “El.” 614}}; and -οῦτο in _neuter_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 244)}}, strengthened forms of τηλίκος (as ὅδε, οὗτος of ὁ, τημοῦτος of τῆμος,
**A** see at {οὗτος} A); the latter being more common in Prose:
**A.I** of persons, **of such an age**, usually meaning **so old**, with a _participle_, τηλικόσδ᾽ ὤν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 643, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34e, etc.; γεγῶσα τηλικήδ᾽ ὅμως 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 533; τηλικοῦτος ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 881, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 261, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489b, etc.: without}}_participle_, τηλικόσδε, τηλικοῦτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 735, “El.” 614}}; νοῦς τηλικοῦτος the mind **of one so old as he is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 767; τηλικῷδε ἀνθρώπῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37d: pleonastic, τηλικοίδε γέροντες ἄνδρες [prev. author] “Cri.” 49a (assuming variant): with _Article_, διδάσκεσθαι βαρὺ τῷ τηλικούτῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1620, see below 3.}}
**A.I.2** of degrees of youth, **so young**, τηλικάσδ᾽ ὁρῶν πάντων ἐρήμους girls **of so tender age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1508, compare “OC” 1116}}; ἀεί σε κηδεύουσα.. τηλικοῦτος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 751; ὃν εἰ τηλικοῦτον ὄντα ἀπεκτείνατε.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378d, “Prt.” 361e.}}
**A.I.3** repeated in opposed to senses, οἱ τηλικοίδε καὶ διδαξόμεσθα δὴ φρονεῖν ὑπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς τηλικοῦδε τὴν φύσι; shall we **old as we are** take lessons forsooth from **one so young?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 726}}; σὺ ἐμοῦ σοφώτερος εἶ τηλικούτου ὄντος τηλικόσδε ὤν you **though so young** are wiser than I **though so old**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25d.}}
**A.II** **so great, so large**, ={τόσος, τοσόσδε, ἐμὲ τηλικόνδε ὄντα} **the size I am**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 155b; τ. κακά 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 819, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.380d}}; τὰ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370c: mostly in the stronger form, ἡ τηλικαύτη [πόλις] [prev. author] “R.” 423b}}; ἀνὴρ τ. ὤν being **so great**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.31}}; ἡ τ. ἀρχή, τ. ἔχθρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755b, 928e}}; τ. κακά, τ. ἀγαθόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.5, 4.4.8}}; τ. [ἀδικήματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13; τ. τιμωρίαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.173; πεπραγμένα τ. τὸ μέγεθος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.151, compare 98; τηλικαύτην βλάβην “PCair.Zen.” 378.11 (3rd c.BC):}} —τηλικοῦτος is frequently conjoined with τοιοῦτος and τοσοῦτος, νησύδρια τοιαῦτα καὶ τ. **so small**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.70; τ. καὶ τοιοῦτον σύστημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 686b; τ. καὶ τοσοῦτος θεός [prev. author] “Smp.” 177a; τοσοῦτοι καὶ τ. θόρυβοι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.174; τ. καὶ τοσαῦτ᾽ ἀγαθά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.24; οἱ τ. καὶ τοιοῦτοι τῷ γένει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 120.}}

5083:
** τηρ-έω**
(τη- also in _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 23.77, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.88, compare διατηρέω)}}, _perfect_ τετήρηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 24, etc.}}:—**watch over, take care of, guard**, δώματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 142; πόλιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place], compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 210; τὰς κύνας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.1; τὴν ἀσφάλειαν τῆς ἐπιβουλῆς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.8}}; rarely of persons, δαιμόνων.., αἵτινες τηροῦμεν ὑμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 579 (troch.)}}; τ. τὴν ἀρχήν **maintain** it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.32.2; τὸ τῆς πόλεως ἀξίωμα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.15:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἔξωθεν [τεῖχος] ἐτηρεῖτο **was constantly guarded**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13:}}_future_ _middle_ τηρήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.30.}}
**2** τ. ὅπως.. ἔσται **take care that.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309b16; ὅπως μηθὲν παρανομῶσι [prev. work] 1307b31}}; τ. μὴ.. **cavere ne.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 580, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169c; τ. ἐμέ, ὅπως μὴ ἐξαπατήσω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.276}}: also in _middle_, τηρώμεσθ᾽, ὅπως μὴ.. αἰσθήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 372; τηροῦ μὴ λάβῃς ὑπώπια [prev. work] 1386.}}
**3** τηρεῖν ἀπὸ τοῦ πυρός **protect** them from the fire, i.e. cook them slowly, Bilabel Ὀψαρτ.{{Refs|P. 10.}}
**II** **give heed to, watch narrowly, observe**, τηρῶ αὐτοὺς οὐδὲ δοκῶν ὁρᾶν κλέπτοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1145, compare “V.” 364; τὰς ἁμαρτίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.60; ἐκεῖνο τ. μὴ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 146, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 442a; τ. ὅ τι καὶ δράσει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 946.}}
**II.2** **watch for** a person or thing, with a _participle_, παραστείχοντα τηρήσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 808}}; ἔνδον ὄντα τηρήσαντες αὐτόν **having watched** for his being within, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134}}; τ. τὸν πορθμὸν κατιόντος τοῦ ἀνέμου **watching** for a crossing with the wind blowing down, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.2}}; τ. τινὰ ἀνιόντα **watch for** one's coming up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.17: with _accusative_ only, ἄνεμον τηρῆσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.65; τ. νύκτα χειμέριον [prev. author] 3.22, compare 4.27}}; νύκτα καὶ ὕδωρ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.103; τ. τοὺς ἀστέρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 292a8}}; τὴν θήραν τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 623a13; τ. καιρόν [prev. author] “Rh.” 1382b10:}} —_passive_, ὁ καιρὸς ἐτηρήθη **was watched for**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.71.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **watch, keep watch**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167b13}}: with _infinitive_, **watch** or **look out**, so as to.., ἐτήρουν ἀνέμῳ καταφέρεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26.}}
**II.4** **observe, notice**, [μετακόσμησιν σωμάτων] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.41; τὸν χαρακτῆρα τῆς φράσεως [prev. author] “Vit. Hippocr.” 13; τὸ πολὺ μὲν οὕτως ἀποβαίνειν τετήρηται 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).13.}}
**II.5** **test by observation** or **trial**, τετηρημένον βοήθημα an **approved** method of treatment, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.22.3; τετήρηνται χρησιμεύοντές τισι [prev. author][prev. work] 21.9}}; as Empiric term, τετηρημένης ἐπ᾽ αὐτοῖς τῆς θεραπείας, οὐκ ἐνδεικτικῶς εὑρισκομένης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.361; Μηνόδοτος ὁ ἐμπειρικός, ἐπὶ μόνῃ τῇ πληθωρικῇ καλουμένῃ συνδρομῇ φάσκων τετηρῆσθαι φλεβοτομίαν [prev. author] 15.766.}}
**III** **observe** or **keep** an engagement, ὅρκους {{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 239; παρακαταθήκας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.22; ἀπόρρητα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.31; εἰρήνην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.89; τὸ πρέπον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.35; τὴν πίστιν NT.2Tim.4.7.}}
**III.2** **preserve, retain**, τὰς αἰσθήσεις uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U., compare 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1055.9,10; ἰδιότητας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.154 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τὴν ποιότητα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.51; τὴν τροφὸν ἐπ᾽ ὀλιγοποσίας.. τ. [prev. work] 118, compare 46, +others:—in 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.125 there is a double use.}}

5084:
** τήρ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**watching, safe-keeping, guarding**, ἀφύλακτος ἡ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 162; τῆς πολιτείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308a30, compare “PA” 692a7; τῆς πόλεως “Supp.epigram” 6.724 (Perga, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}; τῆς οἰκίας {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1070.51 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἀξιώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 413e; τῆς ἡλικίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 80}}; [πλούτου] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.44J.}}; **preservation**, e.g. of health, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.646, Papyrus in “Stud.Ital.” 12(1935).94 (3rd c.AD)}}; **observance**, νόμων, ἐντολῶν, {{LXX+NT+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Wis.6.18(19), NT.1Cor.7.19; λεξάντων πρὸς τὴν τήρησιν τοῦ ὕδατος “SIG” 683.60 (Olympia, 2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **vigilance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.11A. 10.}}
**3** **means of keeping** or **guarding**, τὰς λιθοτομίας.., ἀσφαλεστάτην τ. the quarries.., the most secure **place of custody**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86, cf. NT.Act.4.3, “BGU” 388 iii 7 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **observing, observation**, τῶν καθόλου συμβαινόντων (as Empiric term) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.830, 16.550, 18(2).307, “Sect.Intr.” 4, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.23, 2.246, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 37.14, etc.}}

5087:
** τίθημι** [τῐ],
_2nd pers._ _singular_ τιθεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.11, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 992 codex B (τιθείς LA recentia), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 545}} codices L**p** (-θείς P, τίθης **l), Alc.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|890 codices pler., corrupted to τιθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 376e 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.76, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 301e (ἐπι-), 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 46.28}} (προσ-); ἐν-τιθεῖς (variant{-εὶς}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 717; περι-τιθεῖς “BGU” 1141.19 (1st c.BC)}}; but τίθης is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” [prev. cited] codices A,D, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [prev. cited] codex A, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 27.11}} (προσ-), etc., and is taught by {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.328 H.}}; _Epic dialect_ τίθησθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.404, 24.476}}, and so in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.27 (τίθεισθα 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ τίθησι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.83, +others}}, and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τίθητι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 331.13 (Megara, 4th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.48}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τιθέασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.96, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 128}}; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ τιθεῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.262, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 597, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91 (also 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 466 (Lyric poetry))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ τίθεισι (προ-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 631 A 2 (2nd c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ τίθεντι {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).103.10 (from Nisvrus)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ τιθεῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.732, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 1.6, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.113, also 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, “SIG” 559.16 (Megalopolis, 3rd c.BC) (τιθῶ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 43, 81, διατιθῶ cited by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 290.6)}}: _imperfect_ ἐτίθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500b; ἐτίθεις [prev. author] “R.” 528d, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 59}} (ἐν-), etc.; ἐτίθει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.541, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 532, “Nu.” 63}} (προσ-), etc., _Epic dialect_ τίθει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.441, +others}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τίθεσαν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.456; τίθεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.65; πρό-τιθεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.112 (3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus); late ἐτίθουν NT.Act.4.35}}; _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ τίθεσκον {{Refs|Hes “fragment” 112}}; ἐτίθεα (ὑπερ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155: _imperative_ τίθει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.509, etc.}}; _infinitive_ τιθέναι, not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}; _Epic dialect_ τιθήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.83; τιθέμεν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 744, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.40; τιθεῖν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 286, “IG” 12(9).189.5 (from Eretria)}}; written τιθῖν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Byzantion” 8.50 (Phrygia, 4th c.AD)}}; _participle_ τιθείς, but _Ionic dialect_ _plural_ τιθεῦντες variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91:}}_future_ θήσω, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ θησέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.35, θησέμεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.58:}}_aorist 1_ ἔθηκα, only used in _indicative_, and mostly in _singular_, for though _3rd.pers._ _plural_ is common, the {{Refs|1}} and _2nd pers._ _plural_ are rare, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.15, (ἀν-) 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 9}}; even ἔθηκαν is very rare in early Attic, ἀνέθηκαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 2.1620d, 22.2971 (both 4th c.BC)}}, but is found in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.4.4, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ θῆκαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.795, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἔθην, not used in _indicative_ _singular_, whereas _plural_ is very common, ἔθεμεν, ἔθετε, ἔθεσαν, _Epic dialect_ θέσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.29, etc.; _imperative_ θές 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 185, etc.; _Laconian dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _sing._ σέτω [prev. work] 1081}}; _subjunctive_ θῶ, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ θέω {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 12, (προσ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108}}, _Epic dialect_ θείω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.83, +others (for *θή-ω); _Epic dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ θήῃς, θήῃ, 6.432, 16.96, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.301, 341 (sometimes with the _optative_ forms θείης, θείη as variant); _Epic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ θέωμεν (_disyllable_) 24.485, θείομεν (for *θήο-μεν, short-vowel subjunctive) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.244, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.364}}; _optative_ θείην, _1st pers._ _plural_ θεῖμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.347, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343e (θείημεν codices BT), προσ-θεῖμεν [prev. author] “R.” 370d, and κατα-θεῖτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.27}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ θεῖεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 865}}; _infinitive_ θεῖναι, _Epic dialect_ θέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.285, θέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.3, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 61, 67}}; _Doric dialect_ θέμειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677.13 (Rhodes, 4th c.BC)}}; _participle_ θείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.254, etc.:}}_perfect_ τέθηκα _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.2490.7 (4th c.BC), (ἀνα-) [prev. work]839.38, 1299.44, 1534.76, also at Delos, [prev. work]11(2).161 A 6 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc., and in Papyri, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“POxy.” 1087.42 (1st c.BC)}}; τέθεικα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 324 (3rd c.BC), (ὑπο-) “PPetr.” 3p.53 (3rd c.BC), (ἐκ-) “UPZ” 62.4 (2nd c.BC), (ἀνα-) “IG” 22.1011.71,80 (2nd c.BC), (προσ-) 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.23}}; hence some editors restore τέθηκα for τέθεικα in Attic authors, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.55, 22.16, 27.36, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 15.13}}; Phocian _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἀνα-τεθέκαντι {{Refs|“BCH” 59.202 (from Daulis):}} —_middle_ τίθεμαι, _2nd pers._ _singular_ τίθεσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202c}}; τίθη or τίθῃ uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 768.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; as _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.300 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus); _imperative_ τίθεσο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1039, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 237b, τίθου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 226, _Doric dialect_ τίθευσο conjecture in “Anthologia Graeca” 9.564 (2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus, τιθεύσω codex, τίθεσσο Plan., compare ἀφίκευσο)}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ τιθήμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.34}}: _future_ θήσομαι {{Refs|24.402, etc.:}}_aorist 1_ ἒθηκάμην, only in _indicative_ and _participle_, and never in _Attic dialect_; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἐθήκαο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.18}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ θήκατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.31, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 128}}; _participle_ θηκάμενος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1150, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.29}}: _aorist 2_ ἐθέμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.750, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry _3rd.pers._ _singular_ θέτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.149, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.89; _imperative_ θέο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.333, θοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 466}}; _subjunctive_ θῶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 486, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ θῆαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.403; _optative_ θείμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 188, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ θεῖτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.225, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 527 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 195c, etc. (πρόσ-θοιτο, -θοισθε, ἔν-θοιτο}} are found in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.6, 21.188, 34.17, but προσ-θεῖτο [prev. author] 6.12 codices}}; ἐπιθοίμεθα, -θοιντο, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, 11; compare τιθοῖτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.10)}}: _perfect_ (see. below):—_passive_ τίθεμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.25 (Milet., 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705e, 744a}}: _future_ τεθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1268, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 730b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.17}}: _aorist_ ἐτέθην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1245, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.28, etc. (ἐθέθην “IG” 14.862 (Cumae, 6th c.BC)):}}_perfect_ τέθειμαι, rare in early Gr., {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.9.24, NT.Mark.15.47, (προσ-) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 853a35}}; _infinitive_ τεθεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 327 codices (but _falsa lectio_)}}; _participle_ τεθειμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 12, (προ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.11, (δια-) 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 591}}; also used in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.49, “SIG” 705.17 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1735.11 (1st c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 9, (ἐν-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.16, (προ-) “Supp.epigram” 7.62.6 (Seleucia Pieria, 2nd c.BC), (συν-) “OGI” 229.62 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὑπεκ-τεθημένος (sic) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“BCH” 54.269 (Rhamnus, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἀνα-τέθηται (_passive_ sense) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.81 K.}}; Phocian _perfect_ _participle_ (_middle_ sense) ἀνα-τεθεμένος {{Refs|“BCH” 59.202 (from Daulis)}}:— the _passive_ never occurs in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, and is generally rare, κεῖμαι being used instead.}}
**A** in local sense, **set, put, place**, λίθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.405, compare “IG” 12.373.10, +others; θεμείλια 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.29}}; τέρματα τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.193}}; κλισίην, θρόνον τ. τινί, **set** a stool or chair for him, {{Refs|4.123, 8.65 (so in _middle_}}**set for oneself**, δίφρον {{Refs|20.387); ἐκελήσατο θέμεν τὰν κλίναν, ἐφ᾽ ἇς τὰν Σωστράταν ἔφερον}}**lay down,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).122.31 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; πόδα τ. **plant** the foot, i.e. walk, run, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 294, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 32: so in}}_middle_, τετράποδος βάσιν θηρὸς τιθέμενος, i.e. going on all fours, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 1059 (Lyric poetry): the mode is expressed by _adverbs_ or Preps.}}
**A.a** with _adverbs_, τ. τι πυρὸς ἐγγύς, ἀπάνευθε πυρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.518, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.412; προπάροιθε ποδῶν 20.324; χαμαὶ τ. τὸν πόδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 906}}; τὰ ἄνω κάτω and τὰ κάτω ἄνω τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.3, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 651, etc.: with _adverbs_ implying motion, ἄλλοσε θῆκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.184, 204; ἔχεις.. ὅποι θήσεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 479c}}:—_middle_, ὅποι.. τιθοῖτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.10.}}
**A.b** with Preps. of local sense, θεῖσα στέφανον ἀμφὶ βοστρύχοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1160}} (_middle_, ἀμφ᾽ ὤμοισι τιθήμενον ἔντεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.34)}}; ἀνά τινι or τι, as ἂμ βωμοῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.441; ἀνὰ μυρίκην 10.466}}; ἐπί τινος, τινι, or τι, as εἵματα ἐπ᾽ ἀπήνης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.252, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.223, etc.; ἐπὶ κρατὶ κυνέην 15.480; πέπλον Ἀθηναίης ἐπὶ γούνασι 6.92 (see. below 111.2)}}; ἐπὶ [θρόνον τὰ ἱμάτια] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 483, etc.}}; τὴν ἀρχὴν (i.e. τοῦ ἐπιδέσμου) κατὰ μεσοφρύου, ἐπὶ ἰνίον, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 1, 2, +others}}; ὑπό τινι or τι, as δέμνι᾽ ὑπ᾽ αἰθούσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.644; ἀμβροσίην ὑπὸ ῥῖνά τινι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.445: most frequently with the Preps. ἐν or εἰς}}, **put in** or **put into.. **, as θῆκεν ἐν ἀκμοθέτῳ ἄκμονα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.476; τόξα ἐν πυρί 5.215; ἐν κίστῃ ἐδωδήν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.76}}; ἐν λεχέεσσι θ. [τινά] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.352 (so in _middle_, ἐς δίφρον ἄρνας θέτο}} **put** into the car,{{NT|3.310; ὁ θεὸς ἔθετο τὰ μέλη ἐν τῷ σώματι NT.1Cor.12.18)}}; ἐς λάρνακα, ἐς κάπετον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.795, 797; ἐς ταφάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1110}} (_middle_, ἐν τάφοισι θέσθε {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1410), compare “Ant.” 504, “Tr.” 1254.}}
**A.c** in Poets also with _dative_ only, χρήματα μυχῷ ἄντρου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.364}} (so in _middle_, κολεῷ ἄορ θέο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10.333), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 691, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1064.--The same constructions will be found under many of the following heads.}}
**A.II** Special phrases:
**A.II.1** θεῖναί τινί τι ἐν χερσίν, ἐν χειρί, **put** it in his hands, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.441, 585, etc.}}; ἐν χερσί or χείρεσσί τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.482, 23.597; οἶνον Ὀδυσσῆϊ ἐν χείρεσσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.448}}; ἐς χεῖρά τινος **into** his hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 751.}}
**A.II.2** of women, θέσθαι παῖδα, υἱὸν ὑπὸ ζώνῃ, **to have** a child **put** under her girdle, i.e. to conceive, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Venerem” 255, 282.}}
**A.II.3** ἐν ὄμμασι θέσθαι **set** before one's eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.43.}}
**A.II.4** **set** a plant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.7, 9.}}
**A.II.4.b** **lay** a mosaic, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 665.10, 15 (3rd c.BC).}}
**A.II.5** θέσθαι τὴν ψῆφον **lay one's** voting-pebble on the altar, **put** it into the urn, ἐς τεῦχος οὐ διχορρόπως ψήφους ἔθεντο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 816}}: hence simply, **give one's** vote, ἐπὶ φόνῳ for death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 756 (troch.)}}; ἑωυτῷ in one's own favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.123; σὺν τῷ νόμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.17}}; εὔφρονα, δικαίαν τὴν ψῆφον τ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 640 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 128, etc.}}; and in _passive_, ἔστω δὴ φανερὰ ἡ ψῆφος τιθεμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855d: also γνώμην θέσθαι}}, with _infinitive_, **give one's** opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.82; περὶ ἡμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.21: τίθεσθαι}} _absolutely_, **vote**, γνώμῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1448 codices (_anapaest meter_, γνώμην Lambinus), 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.29; μετά τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 644 (Lyric poetry); ἐναντία τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 58b}}; τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.37 codices, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 1.65.}}
**A.II.6** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, θεῖναί τινί τι ἐν στήθεσσι, ἐν φρεσί}}, etc., **put** or **plant** it in his heart, ἐν στήθεσσι τιθεῖ νόον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.732}}; βουλὴν ἐν στήθεσσι τ{{Refs|. 17.470; ἔπος ἐν φρεσί 19.121, +others; also μένος δέ οἱ ἐν φρεσὶ θῆκε 21.145:}} —_middle_, ἄγριον ἐν στήθεσσι θέτο θυμόν **laid up** wrath in **his** heart, **treasured** it there,{{Refs|9.629; ἐν φρεσὶ θέσθε αἰδῶ καὶ νέμεσιν 13.121}}; τοῖσιν κότον αἰνὸν ἔθεσθε **harboured** enmity against them,{{Refs 6th c.BC+|8.449; καθαρὸν θέμενος νόον 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 89; θέμενος ἄγναμπτον νόον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 164 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐνὶ φρεσὶ θέσθαι, with _infinitive_, **bear** in mind, **think** of doing a thing, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.729; θ. [τι] ἐν καρδίᾳ NT.Luke.1.66.}}
**A.II.7** **deposit**, as in a bank, τὰ πρυτανεῖα πρὸς τοὺς ἄρχοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.22.33; θεὶς ἐπὶ τὴν τράπεζαν τὰς τετταράκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 5; ἐνέχυρον τιθέναι τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 451, compare “Ec.” 755, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.11, “PEnteux.” 32.7 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.:—_middle_, τὰ ἡμίσεα τῆς οὐσίης θέσθαι παρά τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86.ά, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.207; τὴν τιμὴν θήσονται ἐπὶ τὴν τράπεζαν, ἕως.. “PCair.Zen.” 723.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐγγύην θέσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 898; συνθήκας παρά τινι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 23}}:—_passive_, τὰ ληφθέντα καὶ τὰ τεθεντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.5 (but}}_active_ and _middle_ are sometimes distinguished, ὁ θείς **the mortgagor**, ὁ θέμενος **the mortgagee**, τοὺς θέντας ἡμᾶς ἢ καὶ τοὺς θεμένους ὑμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 820e, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 169, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.10}}; τίθεσθαι seems to have the same meaning as ὑποτίθεσθαι in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.43.41, 2758.4, 12(7).55.12 (Arcesine, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}, but the two are distinguished in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.760 (Euboea, 4th c.BC)): _metaphorically_, χάριν or χάριτα θέσθαι τινί}} **deposit a claim for** favour with one, **lay** an obligation on one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, 107, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 783, etc.}}
**A.II.8** **pay down, pay**, τόκον, εἰσφοράν, μετοίκιον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.9, 22.43, 29.3; τὸ γιγνόμενον [prev. author] 18.104; τὸν πριάμενον ἑκατοστὴν τιθέναι τῆς τιμῆς 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 97.1; τὴν τιμήν “PRev.Laws” 18.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ μέρη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 218.33 (3rd c.BC); [τὰς δραχμὰς] εἰς ἀνήλωμα τοῦ πλοίου [prev. work] 753.64 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, θέμενος ἀρραβῶνα {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 303.3 (6th c.AD).}}
**A.II.9** **put down in writing**, θοῦ δ᾽ ἐν φρενῶν δέλτοισι τοὺς ἐμοὺς λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 597 (conjecture Nauck)}}:—_passive_, τὰ ἐν γράμμασι τεθέντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 793b.}}
**A.II.9.b** **place to account, reckon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.34,36, 28.13; θήσω εἰς δύο παῖδας χιλίας δραχμὰς ἑκάστου ἐνιαυτοῦ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.28, compare [prev. work] 21}}:—_metaphorically_ in _middle_, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἀκριβῶς αὐτὸ θήσομαι λίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 532}}; τἀγαθὰ ἐς ἀμφίβολον ἀσφαλῶς ἔθεντο **reckoned** as doubtful, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18.}}
**A.II.10** in military language, τίθεσθαι or θέσθαι τὰ ὅπλα has four senses,
**A.II.10.a** **rest arms**, i.e. halt, with arms in an easy position but ready for action, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.44, 93, 7.3}}; θέμενοι ἐς τὴν ἀγορὰν τὰ ὅπλα advancing to the market-place and **resting arms** there, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.2, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.14, 17, 1.6.4, etc.; εἰς τάξιν τὰ ὅπλα τ. [prev. work] 2.2.21, 5.4.11; so ἐν τάξει [prev. work] 2.2.8}}; ἀντία τισί over against them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.74 (in 1.62 ἀντία ἔθεντο τὰ ὅπλα over against it (the temple))}}; _poetry_, πάτρας ἕνεκα εἰς δῆριν ἔθεντο ὅπλα Inscription cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.289.}}
**A.II.10.b** **bear arms, fight**, τὸ θυμοειδὲς.. ἐν τῇ τῆς ψυχῆς στάσει τίθεσθαι τὰ ὅπλα πρὸς τὸ λογιστικόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440e; τοῦ δήμου.. παρακαλοῦντος τοὺς στρατιώτας τίθεσθαι πρὸς τὴν πόλιν “IG” 22.666.10; ὃς ἂν μὴ θῆται τὰ ὅπλα μηδὲ μεθ᾽ ἑτέρων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 8.5, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.14, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.145}}; so ὁπόσοιπερ ἂν ὅπλα ἱππικὰ ἢ πεζικὰ τιθῶνται who **serve** on horseback or on foot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 753b, compare 756a; ἐν ταῖς ναυσὶ τὰ ὅπλα θέσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 5.}}
**A.II.10.c** **lay down one's arms, surrender**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.31, 45}}; so, without the idea of surrender, θέσθαι τὰς ἀσπίδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.12}} (but _active_, τὰ ὅπλα θείς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.759a).}}
**A.II.10.d** τὰ ὅπλα εὖ τίθεσθε **keep** your arms **in good order**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.3; εὖ ἀσπίδα θέσθω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.382.}}
**A.II** **lay in the grave, bury**, ἐμὰ σῶν ἀπάνευθε τιθήμεναι ὀστέα {{Refs|23.83 (frequently with words added, ἐν τάφοισι, ἐς ταφάς, etc., see above 1 b)}}; ποῦ σφε θήσομεν χθονό; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1006 (Lyric poetry)}}:— _passive_, τὰ δὲ ὀστᾶ φασι.. τεθῆναι.. ἐν τῇ Ἀττικῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 242c, “Lg.” 947e; ἄλλῳ δὲ μηδενὶ ἐξεῖναι ἐν τῷ πυργίσκῳ τεθῆναι μετὰ τὸ ἐνταφῆναι αὐτήν· ἐπεὶ ὁ θείς τινα ἀσεβὴς ἔστω θεοῖς καταχθονίοις “TAM” 2(1).51 (from Telmessus), compare 55, +others, “AJP” 48.30 (from Apamea), “Supp.epigram” 6.221 (from Phrygia), etc.}}
**A.II.12** τιθέναι τὰ γόνατα **kneel down,** {{NT|NT.Mark.15.19, NT.Luke.22.41, +others}}
**A.III** **set up**, of the prizes in games, ἄεθλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.263, etc.}}; ἀέθλιον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 748; νικητήρια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 537 (so in _passive_, τὰ τιθέμενα}} **the prizes**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.25)}}; also with the object offered as the prize, τ. δέπας, βοῦν, σόλον, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.656, 750, 826, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.144, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 573:—this is more fully expressed by ἐς μέσσον τ., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.704: after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} more generally, **lay before** people as common property, βούλομαι ὑμῖν εἰς τὸ μέσον αὐτὸ θεῖναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 719a; ἐς μέσον ἀρχὴν τιθεὶς ἰσονομίην ὑμῖν προαγορεύω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142; so also τ. τι εἰς τὸ κοινὸν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.1}}; reading and sense are doubtful in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 145.}}
**A.III.2** **set up** in a temple, **dedicate**, ἀγάλματα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.347; τάσδε.. θεοῖς ἀσπίδας ἔθηκε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 576; so perhaps 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.92 (see. above 1b).}}
**A.IV** **assign, award**, τιμήν τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.57; ὄνομά τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 244d: especially in}}_middle_, ὄνομα (or οὔνομα) θέσθαι τινί **give** a child a name **at one's own discretion**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.5, 19.406 (in 19.403 with variant{θείης}), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107, 113, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 13: elliptically, without ὄνομα, ᾧ δὴ ἀθροίς ματι ἄνθρωπόν τε τίθενται καὶ λίθον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157b, compare “Cra.” 402b: pleonastic, Ἴωνα δ᾽ αὐτὸν ὄνομα κεκλῆσθαι θήσεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 75.}}]
**A.V** τιθέναι νόμον **down** or **give** a law, of a legislator, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 580, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 57, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 532, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 339c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.99, etc.}}:—so in _middle_, of Solon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29}}; of a people, state, or legislature, **give oneself** a law, **make** a law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289a14}} (_passive_, τίθεται νόμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1425, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705e, 744a; τιμωρίαι.. ἐτέθησαν [prev. work] 943d)}}; also θήσω θεσμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 484; κήρυγμα θεῖναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 8}}; σκῆψιν τιθέναι **allege** an excuse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 584}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **order matters so that.. **, [ὁ Λυκοῦργος] ἔθηκε θύειν βασιλέα πρὸ τῆς πόλεως τὰ δημόσια ἅπαντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 15.2, compare 1.5, 2.11}}; without _infinitive_, καλῶς ἔθεντο ταῦτα πατέρες οἱ πάλαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 512}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, γυναιξὶ σωφρονεῖν.. θήσει {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1057.}}
**A.V.2** _middle_, **agree upon**, ἡμέραν θέσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.1,13}}; so θ. συγγραφήν, ὁμολογίαν, σύμβολόν τινι, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 2.16 (3rd c.BC), “PGoodsp.Cair.” 6ii 2 (2nd c.BC), “PRein.” 11.9 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**A.V.3** **execute** a document. τ. διαθήκην **make** a will, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 1.6.3 (5th c.AD)}}: so in _middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PSI” 10.1119.16 (2nd c.AD)}}; θέσθαι τινὸς ἀπαρχήν **make out** a person's birth-certificate, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 9.1067.15 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**A.VI** **establish, institute**, ἀγῶνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 845, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.10}}; ἐν τοῖς ἀγώνοις οἷς ἁ πόλις τίφητι (sic) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Delph.” 3(3).120.17 (2nd c.BC)}}; πενταετηρίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.21.}}
**A.VII** **dispose, order, ordain, bring to pass**, of gods, οὕτω νῦν Ζεὺς θείη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.465, 15.180; ὣς ἄο᾽ ἔμελλον θησέμεναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.35; [Ζεὺς] τίθησ᾽ ὅπῃ θέλει 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.2}}; τὰ δ᾽ ἄλλα πάντ᾽ ἄνω τε καὶ κάτω στρέφων τίθησιν (i.e. Ζεύς) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 651}}; πάντα παγκάκως θεοὶ θέσαν conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 283 (Lyric poetry); τέλος δ᾽ ἔθηκε Ζεὺς.. καλῶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 26}}; κόσμῳ θέντες, as _etymology_ of θεοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.52}}; of human beings, **administer, manage**, [τι] κακῶς θέμεν, εὖ θέμεν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 845, 846; τὰ δ᾽ ἄλλα φροντὶς.. θήσει δικαίως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 913; ἐγὼ καὶ σὺ θήσομεν κρατοῦντε τῶνδε δωμάτων καλῶς [prev. work] 1673 (troch.)}}; ταῦτ᾽ ἐγὼ θήσω καλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 521, compare “Andr.” 737; τὰ παρ᾽ ὑμῶν εὖ τίθει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 243; τ. τὰ τῶν φίλων ἀσφαλῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.12; τὰ πράγματ᾽ ὀρθῶς ἂν τιθῇ πράξει καλῶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 287}}:—_middle_, **administer for oneself**, οἶκον εὖ θέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 23; ἄνδρας σοφοὺς χρὴ τὸ παρὸν πρᾶγμα καλῶς εἰς δύναμιν τίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 172 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.134, Anon.ap Suid.see entry τίθεσθαι}}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see τὸ παρὸν εὖ τίθεσ; ἐν ἀπόρῳ εἴχοντο θέσθαι τὸ παρόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25}}; τὸ παρὸν εὖ θέσθαι **make the best of** one's resources or situation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 21, M. Ant. 6.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.35 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; εὐτυχίαν τὴν παροῦσαν ἔξεστι καλῶς θέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17; τὰ παρόντα θέσθαι καλῶς 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.11; τὰ σεωυτοῦ τιθέμενος εὖ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236; τὰ οἰκεῖα εὖ θέμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443d; τὰ ἴδια ἕκαστοι εὖ βουλόμενοι δὴ θέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59; τὰ πάντα ὅπως ἂν αὐτῇ ἡδὺ ᾖ οὕτως τίθεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.17; εἰ μὴ θήσομαι τἄμ᾽ ὡς ἄριστα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 378; τὸ σαυτοῦ θέμενος εὖ [prev. author] “IT” 1003, compare “Ba.” 49, “HF” 605, 938, “Hipp.” 709, 5th c.BC: Dionysius Chalcus Elegiacus 1.5; τὰ πρὶν εὖ θέμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1434}}; συνετῶν ἀνδρῶν (i.e. εἶναι), πρὶν γενέσθαι τὰ δυσχερῆ, προνοῆσαι ὅπως μὴ γένηται· ἀνδρείων δέ, γενόμενα εὖ θέσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pittacus Lyricus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.78}}; τὸ κοινῶς φοβερὸν ἅπαντας εὖ θέσθαι that all should **face** the common danger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.61}}; of wars, quarrels, etc., **bring** them **to a successful issue**, but sometimes **put a good face on** them, **patch** them **up**, ἕως ἂν τὸν πόλεμον εὖ θῶνται {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.84; θήσονται τὸν πόλεμον ᾗ βούλονται [prev. author] 1.31}}; πόλεμον ἀραμένους οὐ ῥᾴδιον εὐπρεπῶς θέσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 82; ὅτῳ τρόπῳ.. τὸ σφέτερον ἀπρεπὲς εὖ θήσονται [prev. author] 6.11; μεθ᾽ ἧς τὸ νῦν παρεστὸς νεῖκος εὖ θέσθαι χρεών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 633; τὸν τρὸς τοὺς Ἐλευσῖνι πόλεμον ὡς μετρίως ἔθεντο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 243e}}; ἄμεινον ἢ τότε ἐθέμεθα τὸν πόλεμον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 245e; τὰς γενομένας συμφορὰς πρὸς ἀλλήλους θέσθαι καλῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.140: _absolutely_, θέσθαι καλῶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 350:—_passive_, εἰ τεθήσεται κατὰ νοῦν τὰ πράγματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.120.}}
**A.VII.2** in the game of πεττεία, κυβεία, Latin _tesserae_ (compare {{Refs|Ter. “Adelph.” 739)}}, **to place as skilfully as possible** the pieces which have been assigned to one by the luck of the dice, πεττείᾳ τινὶ ἔοικεν ὁ βίος, καὶ δεῖ ὥσπερ ψῆφόν τινα τίθεσθαι τὸ συμβαῖνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.56.39; ὥσπερ ἐν πτώσει κύβων πρὸς τὰ πεπτωκότα τίθεσθαι τὰ αὑτοῦ πράγματα ὅπῃ ὁ λόγος αἱρεῖ βέλτιστ᾽ ἂν ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604c, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 26; στέργειν δὲ τἀκπεσόντα καὶ θέσθαι πρέπει σοφὸν κυβευτήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 947}}; τὰ δεσποτῶν γὰρ εὖ πεσόντα θήσομαι I **will take advantage of** my master's good luck, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 32}}: many of the passages cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus v11. I may be _metaphorically_ applications of this sense.}}
**B** **put in a certain state** or **condition**, much the same as ποιεῖν, ποιεῖσθαι, and so often to be rendered by our **make**:
**B.I** followed by an attributive _substantive_, **make** one something, with the predicate in apposition, θεῖναί τινα αἰχμητήν, ἱέρειαν, μάντιν, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290, 6.300, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.253, etc.; θ. τινὰ ἀρχέπολιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.54}}; θεῖναί τινα ἄλοχόν τινος **make** her another's wife, of a third person who negotiates a marriage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.298 (for _middle_, see below 3)}}; ἥτε με τοῖον ἔθηκεν ὅπως ἐθέλει who **has made** me such as she will, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.208}}; σῦς ἔθηκας ἑταίρους **thou hast made** my comrades swine,{{LXX+8th c.BC+|10.338; so [νῆα] λᾶαν ἔθηκε 13.163, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.319, etc.; ἕως ἂν θῶ τοὺς ἐχθρούς σου ὑποπόδιον LXX.Psa.109(110).1}}; but γέλων ἔθηκε συνδείπνοις **caused** them laughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1172}}; λόγους εἰς μέτρα τ. **put** them into verse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 669d.}}
**B.I.2** with an _adjective_ for the attributive, θεῖναί τινα ἀθάνατον καὶ ἀγήρων **make** him undying and undecaying, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.136}}; πηρόν, τυφλόν, ἀφνειὸν τ. τινά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.599, 6.139, 9.483; τὸν μὲν.. θῆκε μείζονά τ᾽ εἰσιδέειν καὶ πάσσονα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.229, compare 18.195, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 344d.}}
**B.I.2.b** of things, ἅλιον πόνον, πόνον οὐκ ἀτέλεστον, πάντα μεταμώνια, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.26, 57, 363; ὄλεθρον ἀπευθέα θῆκε Κρονίων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.88, compare 11.274; ἀποίητον θέμεν ἔργων τέλος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.17; ἀρὰν τ. ἀλαθῆ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 944 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀναστάτους οἴκους τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 674; τ. λεῖον τὸν τραχὺν ἐχῖνον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1086}}; τὸ πραχθὲν ἀγένητον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324b.}}
**B.I.3** frequently in _middle_, γυναῖκα or ἄκοιτιν θέσθαι τινά **make** her **one's** wife, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.72, 316, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.169}}; παῖδα τὸν αὑτᾶς πόσιν θ. **take** her own son as husband, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 929 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.I.3.b** υἱὸν θέσθαι τινά, like{ποιεῖσθαι}, **make one's** son, **adopt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929c, etc.: _absolutely_, θέσθαι τινά}} **adopt**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 5.}}
**B.I.3.c** generally, προσφιλῆ θέσθαι τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 532}}; but φίλον ἐμαυτῷ θ. **deem** my friend, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 188}}; γέλωτα θέσθαι τινά **make** him **one's** butt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.29, 7.209.}}
**B.I.4** with _infinitive_, **make** one do so and so, τιθέναι τινὰ νικᾶσαι **make** him conquer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.48 (uncertain); μετατραπεῖν [prev. author] “fragment” 177; τὸν πάθει μάθος θέντα κυρίως ἔχειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 178 (Lyric poetry), compare 1036, 1174 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 718, “Heracl.” 990, etc.}}
**B.II** in reference to mental action, when _middle_ is more frequently than _active_, **lay down. assume, hold, reckon** or **regard as.. **, τί δ᾽ ἐλέγχεα ταῦτα τίθεσθ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.333; δαιμόνιον αὐτὸ τίθημ᾽ ἐγώ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1270 (Lyric poetry); τοιοῦτον θέντες τὸν δίκαιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361b, compare 430b (_middle_)}}; θὲς δή μοι.. now **suppose** so and so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 191c; εὐεργέτημά τι θεῖναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.10; with ὡς, θέντες ὡς ὑπάρχον εἶναι ὃ βούλονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458a, compare “Phd.” 100a; μὴ τοῦτο ὡς ἀδίκημα θῇς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.193.}}
**B.II.2** followed by _adverbs_, ποῦ χρὴ τίθεσθαι ταῦτ; in what light must we **regard** these things? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 451}}; οὐδαμοῦ τιθέναι τι **hold** of no account, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 210}}; πρόσθεν or ἐπίπροσθέν τινος τιθέναι τι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 129 (_anapaest meter_), “Supp.” 515}}; πόρρω τίθεσθαί τί τινων **set** far below.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.299.}}
**B.II.3** followed by Preps., τ. τινὰ ἐν φιλοσόφοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475d; ἐν τοῖς φίλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4}}; also εἰς ὁποτέραν (of two classes) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264c}}; εἰς τὸν δῆμον, εἰς τοὺς πλουσίους, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.39; also οὐκ ἐν λόγῳ τίθεσθαί τινα 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.1; ἐν τιμῇ τίθεσθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.3; ἐν αἰτίῃ τιθέναι τινά [prev. author] 8.99}}; ἐν οἰωνῷ τινι τοῦ μέλλοντος, ἐν ἐπαίνῳ, ἐν γέλωτι τίθεσθαι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 31, “Cat.Ma.” 20, “TG” 17}}; θέσθαι παρ᾽ οὐδέν **set** at naught, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 230 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 732, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252a (but ἐν οὐδενί “BGU” 1816.23 (1st c.BC), “Supp.epigram” 7.1.6 (Susa, 1st c.AD, Epist.Artabani))}}; ἐν παρέργῳ θοῦ με {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 473}}; πάντα ταῦτ᾽ ἐν εὐχερεῖ ἔθου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 876; ταῦτ᾽ ἐν αἰσχρῷ θέμενος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 806; ἐν ἀδικήματι θέσθαι τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35; ἐν ἀδικήματος μέρει τιθέναι τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.148}}; θέσθαι τὰ δίκαια ἔκ τινος **estimate** them by.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.8.}}
**B.II.4** with _partitive_ _genitive_, ἐμὲ θὲς τῶν πεπεισμένων **put** me **down as one of** the convinced, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 424c, compare 376e, 437b}}; τῆς ἡμετέρας ἀμελείας ἄν τις θείη **might reckon** it as due to our carelessness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.10.}}
**B.II.5** with _infinitive_, οὐ τίθημ᾽ ἐγὼ ζῆν τοῦτον I **hold** not that he lives, **count** him not as living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1166}}: so in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 93c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.43,44: rarely with}}_participle_, θήσω ἀδικοῦντα [αὐτόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 23.76, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343e, “Ap.” 27c.}}
**B.II.6** elliptically, **lay down, assume**, θῶμεν δύο εἴδη (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 79a, etc.}}; θήσω οὕτω (i.e. εἶναί τι) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.85, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1290a30.}}
**B.II.7** **affirm**, opposed to αἴρω (deny), τὸ ἐπέκεινα ὄντος οὐ τόδε λέγει- οὐ γὰρ τίθησιν--the phrase 'beyond being' does not denote a 'this' (for it **is** not **an affirmation**), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.6.}}
**C** without any attributive word following, **make, work, execute**, of an artist, ἐν δ᾽ ἐτίθει νειόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.541, compare 550, 561, 607}}; [δόρπον] θησέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.394.}}
**C.2** **make, cause, bring to pass**, ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.321; τ. κέλαδον καὶ ἀϋτήν 9.547; ὀρυμαγδόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.235; ἔριν μετ᾽ ἀμφοτέροισιν 3.136}}; φιλότητα, ὅρκια μετ᾽ ἀμφ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.83, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.546: with _dative_ _person_, σῆμα τιθεὶς Τρώεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.171; Ἀχαιοῖς ἄλγε᾽ ἔθηκεν 1.2, etc.; πᾶσι δ᾽ ἔθηκε πόνον 21.524, compare 15.721, 16.262; φόως ἑτάροισιν 6 6, etc.; χάρματ᾽ ἄλλοις ἔθηκεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.99; πόλει κατασκαφὰς θέντες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 47; εἰρήνην φίλοις [prev. author] “Pers.” 769; αἷμα θήσεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 837 (assuming variant).}}
**C.3** frequently in _middle_, **make** or **prepare for oneself**, θέσθαι κέλευθον **make oneself** a road, **open** a way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.418; θέτο δῶμα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.241}}; τίθεντο δὲ δαῖτα, δόρπα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.475, 9.88 (but δαῖτα τίθενται}} **are holding** a feast, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.269)}}; μεγάλην ἐπιγουνίδα θέσθαι **to make oneself, get** a large thigh, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.225}}; θέσθαι μάχην **engage in.. **, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.402; δυσμενέεσσι πόνον καὶ δῆριν ἔθεντο 17.158}}; ἱδρῶτα τίθεσθαι **have an access of** perspiration, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 2}}; μαρτύρια θέσθαι **produce as** testimony, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55}}; ἀνδρὸς αἰδοίου πρόσοψιν θηκάμενος **putting on** the aspect of a reverend man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.29, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see at {θήκατ; πόνον πλέω τίθου} **work thyself** the more annoy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 226; εὐκλεᾶ θέσθαι βίον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1422, etc.}}
**C.4** _periphrastic_ for a single Verb. μνηστήρων σκέδασιν θεῖναι **make** a scattering, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.116}}; θέμεν κρυφόν, νέμεσιν, αἶνον, for κρύπτειν, νεμεσῦν, αἰνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.97, 8.86, “N.” 1.5; μὴ σχολὴν τίθει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1059}}; ὑμῖν ἔθηκε σὺν θεοῖς σωτηρίαν (variant{προμηθίαν}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 915:}} — also in _middle_, θέσθαι μάχην, for μάχεσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.402}}; θέσθαι θυσίαν, γάμον, for θύειν, γαμεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.42, 13.53}}; σπουδήν, πρόνοιαν θέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 13, 536, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.276; θ. ἐπιστροφὴν πρό τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 134; περὶ τούτων οἰκονομίας “PEnteux.” 22.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; and with _genitive_, θ. λησμοσύναν, συγγνωμοσύνην τινῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 151 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 1265 (_anapaest meter_)}}. (Cf. Lithuanian _dēti_ 'lay (eggs, etc.)', Sanskrit _dáti_ 'lay down, place', Latin -**do** in **con-do**, etc., Engl. **do, doom.**)

5088:
** τίκ-τω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.86, etc.}}: _future_ τέξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.249, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 493, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.β, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 851, 869, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 747, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1037 (Oracle texts), “Th.” 509; also τέξομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.99, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 469, 898, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 101, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 768, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 744, etc.}}; _poetry_ _infinitive_ also τεκεῖσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Venerem” 127; _plural_ τεξείεσθε 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 124}}: _aorist_ ἔτεκον, _Epic dialect_ τέκον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.352, 5.875}}, etc.: _aorist 1_ ἔτεξα only late, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 41.8 codices (for ἐντήξῃ}} is probable reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 553)}}: _perfect_ τέτοκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 591, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 757, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 64.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.13, compare ἐντίκτω}}:—_middle_, in same sense as _active_, only in Poets, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 127, “fragment” 44}}: _future_ (see. above): _aorist_ ἐτεκόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1193 (Lyric poetry)}}, _Epic dialect_ τεκόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.59, +others}}; _subjunctive_ τέκηαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.905}}:—_passive_, _present_ _indicative_ τίκτεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 437}}; _infinitive_ τίκτεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.53}}_participle_ τικτόμενος{{Refs|[prev. work] 54}}: _future_ τεχθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.9.2, “Geoponica” 17.6.1, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐτέχθην {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 18, Ps.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1132.44, LXX.Num.26.60 (variant), +others, “Geoponica” 17.6.2, etc.:}}_perfect_ τέτεγμαι, _infinitive_ τετέχθαι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.12, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.7.7, etc.}}--These _passive_ tenses seem not to have been used in correct _Attic dialect_:—**bring into the world, engender**; of the father, **beget**, of the mother, **bring forth.**
**I** _imperfect_ _active_ τίκτε, ἔτικτε, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually of the father, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.628, 6.155, 206, 11.224, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 44 (of the mother, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.180, 22.428, 24.497, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.325); in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus (“Frr.” 17,142)}}, Lyric poetry, and Trag. the _present_ and _imperfect_ are also used of the mother, ἃ Θήβαν ἔτικτεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.85, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 18.50; μᾶτερ, ἅ μ᾽ ἔτικτες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 321 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ag.” 763 (Lyric poetry, of Υβρις), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 533}}; δεινὸν τὸ τίκτειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 770, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151a, etc.; τ. καὶ γεννᾷ [prev. author] “Smp.” 206d}}; of both parents. Στάσις δὲ καὶ Κρόνος.. τίκτετον τύραννον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 240.}}
**I.2** _aorist_ _active_ τέκε, ἔτεκε, mostly of the mother, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.36, 352, 2.513, etc.}} (also _future_ _middle_ τέξεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|19.99); τεκεῖν τινά τινι 2.658, 6.22, etc.; ὑπό τινι 2.714, 728, etc.; τ. ἔκ τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 20; παρά τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 42; παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 62: but τέκεν}} of the father, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.450, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.489, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 208, “fragment” 99.2: _metaphorically_, τῷ τεκόντι ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 212a.}}
**I.3** the _aorist_ _middle_ τέκετο is commonly used of the father, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.741, 6.154, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 19: but τέκετο}} of the mother, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.742, 15.187, 22.48, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 46}}; so τῶν τεκομένων of the mother, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 419 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.4** the two are conjoined, ὃν τέκετο θάνατος, ἔτεκε δ᾽ αἰόλος δράκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 834 (codices, Lyric poetry).}}
**I.5** _aorist_ _active_ is used in _plural_ of both parents, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.55, 8.554 (οὓς Ἑκάβη ἠδὲ Πρίαμος τέκε παῖδας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.234)}}; _aorist_ _middle_ τεκόμεσθα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.61, 24.293.}}
**I.5.b** οἱ τεκόντες the **parents**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 49, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 999, etc.}}; the _Article_ is rarely omitted, πατέρων τε καὶ τεκόντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 329 (Lyric poetry): with _genitive_, κιόντων τοῖς τεκοῦσι [prev. author] “Pers.” 245 (troch.): ὁ τεκών}} **the father**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 690, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1108}}; ἁ τεκοῦσα **the mother**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 926 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ch.” 133, etc. (rarely ἡ τίκτουσα, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1247, “El.” 342); in Prose, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.8}}; ἡ τ. αὐτόν his **mother**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; ὅ τ᾽ ἐκεῖνον τεκών 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 335.}}
**I.6** frequently in in Medicine texts and other Prose, of women, τίκτουσι ῥηϊδίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 5,compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.54, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.670; κόρον ἔτεκε “IG” 42(1).121.5, compare 21 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}
**II** of female animals, **bear young, breed**, of mares, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.150, 20.225}}; of cows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 591}}; of sheep, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.86, etc.; τὰς τετοκυίας τοκάδας “PCair.Zen.” 292.305, compare 710.4 (3rd c.BC); ἐὰν τέκῃ ἵππος [prev. work] 635.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; of the hare, τὰ μὲν τέτοκε, τὰ δὲ τίκτει, τὰ δὲ κύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.13}}; of birds, **hatch**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.313}}; ᾠὰ τ. **lay** eggs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 185, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 718b23, etc.}}; of fish, **spawn**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 568a16, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.718, etc.}}
**III** of the earth, **bear, produce**, ἔμπεδα μῆλα (sheep) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.113; ἡ γῆ.. τίκτουσα ποίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 333}}:—_middle_, γαῖαν.., ἣ τὰ πάντα τίκτεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 127, compare “fragment” 44.4}}:—_passive_, τίκτεσθαι δὲ φόρους γᾶς.. εὐχόμεθ᾽ ἀεί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 674 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **generate, engender, produce**, λέγω τὴν χώρην λιμὸν τέξεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49; ἐπειχθῆναι πρῆγμα τίκτει σφάλματα [prev. work] 10.ζ}}; of impiety, τὸ γὰρ δυσσεβὲς ἔργον μετὰ μὲν πλείονα τίκτει {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 759 (Lyric poetry), compare 763 (Lyric poetry, compare above 1.1), “Ch.” 805 (Lyric poetry); ἡ ἐπιθυμία τ. ἁμαρτίαν NT.Jam.1.15; μὴ θράσος τέκῃ φόβον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 498}}; of Night as the mother of Day, τῆς.. τεκούσης φῶς τόδ᾽ εὐφρόνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 279; ὃν αἰόλα νὺξ.. τίκτει.., Ἅλιον αἰτῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 95 (Lyric poetry): generally, τ. [νόμους] [prev. author] “OT” 870 (Lyric poetry); χάρις χάριν γάρ ἐστιν ἡ τίκτουσ᾽ ἀεί [prev. author] “Aj.” 522; τ. ἀοιδάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 767 (Lyric poetry); ὕδωρ δὲ πίνων οὐδὲν ἂν τέκοι σοφόν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 199; τ. ῥήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1059 (_anapaest meter_)}}; also in Prose, νουσήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ χυμῶν” 12; ἃ ἀεὶ τίκτει πόλεμον καὶ ἔχθραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 547a; πολλοὺς καὶ καλοὺς λόγους [prev. author] “Smp.” 210d; [δὰς] πῦρ τέξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.23, etc.:}} —_passive_, τῷδε κέρδει (sic codices) κέρδος ἄλλο τίκτεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 437. (τίκτω from τί-τκ-ω reduplicate from τεκ (τέκνον, etc.).)}}

5089:
** τίλλω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.78, etc.:}}_future_ τῐλῶ (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 123, (παρα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 373}}: _aorist_ ἔτῑλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.21, (ἀπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 578. “fragment” 686}}: _perfect_ τέτιλκα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 782 (b.). 121 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ τιλλέσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.711:}}_future_ τῐλοῦμαι (παρα-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 363.5}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐτίλθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1083: 2}}_aorist_ ἐτίλην [ῐ{{LXX|] LXX.Dan.7.4}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ τιλήτωι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFay.” 131.18 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}; _participle_ τειλείς (i.e. τιλ-) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 322.36 (3rd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ τέτιλμαι {{LXX+6th c.BC+|LXX.Isa.18.7, (ἐκ-) 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 21.10, (ἀπο-) 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.20, (παρα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 516}}:—**pluck** or **pull out**hair, etc., πολιὰς δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἀνὰ τρίχας ἕλκετο χερσί, τίλλων ἐκ κεφαλῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.78}}; τίλλε κόμην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 406; τρίχας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 271, “Her.” 5; ἐρέβινθον “PCair.Zen.” 719.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. στάχυας καὶ ἐσθίειν {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Matt.12.1; τ. χόρτον τοῖς κτῆσι “PFlor.” 321.47 (3rd c.AD):—}}_middle_, Χαίτας τίλλεσθαι **pluck out one's** hair, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.567.}}
**2** with _accusative_ of that **from which** the hair or feathers **are plucked**, τίλλειν πέλειαν, of birds of prey,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|15.527, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76; κίρκον εἰσορῶ.. χηλαῖς κάρα τίλλοντα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 209}}; τίλλουσι τὴν γλαῦκα, of small birds attacking the owl, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609a15}}; so of the cuckoo,{{Refs|[prev. work] 618a29 (_passive_)}}; as a description of an idle fellow, τίλλων ἑαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 546, compare “Ra.” 428}}; of a cook, **pluck** a fowl, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 150.5, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.233a; also τ. λαγών 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 212}}; τ. πλάτανον **pluck** its leaves **off**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 18; τὸν στέφανον τῖλαί με κατ᾽ αὐτίκα λεπτὰ ποησεῖς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus [same place]}}; κῴδια τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.108 = 3p.78 (3rd c.BC)}}; also, **pluck** live sheep, instead of shearing, τοῖς τίλλουσιν τὰ ὑποδίφθερα (i.e. πρόβατα) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 430.3 (3rd c.BC),compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see entry {πεκτῆρες}:—_passive_, **have one's hair plucked out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 593}}; τέφρᾳ τιλθῆναι, as a punishment of adulterers, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1083}}; see at {παρατίλλω, τέφρα}.
**3** with _accusative_ _cognate_, τίλματα τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.48b, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.70.}}
**4** τ. μέλη pluck the harp-strings, **play** harp-tunes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 256 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5** **pick so as to extract fibre**, τετίλκασι στιππύου δέσμας σ {{Refs|“PCair.Zen.” [prev. cited]}}
**6** νεφέλιον παρατεταμένον καὶ τιλλόμενον **cirrous**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 43.}}
**II** since tearing the hair was a usual expression of sorrow, τίλλεσθαί τινα **tear one's hair in sorrow for** any one, τόν γ᾽ ἄλοχός τε φίλη καὶ πότνια μήτηρ τιλλέσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.711: without _accusative_, τιλλόμενοι καὶ κλαίοντες 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.36 W.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **pluck, vex, annoy**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 13 B}}; στέφανον τ., ={τοὺς νόμους λυμαίνεσθαι}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Pythagorae - Porphyrii Opuscula” 42:}} —_passive_, ὑπὸ συκοφαντῶν τίλλεσθαι, with allusion to a bird's feathers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 285. (Not found in _Attic dialect_ Prose.)}}

5091:
** τῑμάω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.788, etc.}}: _future_ τιμήσω {{Refs|9.155, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τιμᾱσεῦντι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 7.4}}: _aorist_ ἐτίμησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.124, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ τίμησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 399, Lyric poetry τίμᾱσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.41, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.194}}: _perfect_ τετίμηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.17, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ τετίμᾱκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).37(55):}} —_middle_, _future_ τιμήσομαι always in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 485, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 581, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 210, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.49, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.15 (reading δι᾽ ἄνδρα with codices DF), “Hier.” 9.9, except in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37b}}, where it is used in a technical sense (see. below {{Refs|111.2):}}_aorist_ ἐτιμησάμην in senses shared by _active_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.280, 20.129, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.235, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; in sense 111.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52c}}:—_passive_, _future_ τιμηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.80, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.223, “IG” 22.1182.9, etc.; τετιμήσομαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.24 codices (τιμήσεται Cobet)}}: _aorist_ ἐτιμήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.5, etc.}}; Lyric poetry _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τίμᾱθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Parth.” 2.41}}: _perfect_ τετίμημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.310, etc.; also}}_middle_ in technical sense, see {{Refs|111.2}}:—**honour, revere, reverence** (in this sense the _middle_ is used only by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}; of the honour rendered to superiors, as by men to gods, by men to their elders, rulers, or guests, περὶ κῆρι θεὸν ὣς τιμήσαντο {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.280, etc.; τίμα τὸν πατέρα σου LXX.Exo.20.12, +others}}; conversely of the honour bestowed by gods upon a man, μερμήριζε (i.e. Ζεὺς).., ὡς Ἀχιλῆα τιμήσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.4, compare 15.612, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.379}}; by a father on his son, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.203, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 532}}; by an elder brother, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.235 (_middle_): also in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, and _Attic dialect_, ἐξόχως τίμας εν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.69; δαιμόνων τιμᾶν γένος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 236; θεοὺς τιμῶντες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 277, compare 1071 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29; σέβεσθαι καὶ τ. τοὺς θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13; ἱλασκομένοις καὶ τιμῶσιν.. Δία Πατρώϊον “SIG” 1044.6 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; τὸν φίλον τιμῶσιν ἐξ ἴσου πατρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 644, compare 516, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 660 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.107, etc.; θεοὶ δ᾽ ὅταν τιμῶσιν, οὐδὲν δεῖ φίλων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1338: _absolutely_, οἱ τύραννοι μάλιστα δύνανται τιμᾶν}} **bestow honours**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.15 (τιμᾶν secl. Bake),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 631e}}: hence simply, **reward**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.42}} (so in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.213, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.64, 19.18)}}; ἐπαινεῖν καὶ τ., τ. καὶ δωρεῖσθαι, δωρεῖσθαί τε καὶ τ., τ. καὶ χαρίζεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.12, 3.2.28, 8.2.10, 2.4.9: with _dative_ modi, δωτίνῃσι θεὸν ὣς τιμήσουσι}} **will honour** him **with** gifts, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.155; ξεῖνον ἐτιμήσασθ᾽ ἐνὶ οἴκῳ εὐνῇ καὶ σίτῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.129}}; τιμᾶν τινα τάφῳ, γόοις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1051, “Supp.” 116 (Lyric poetry); πόλιν τ. συμμάχῳ δορί [prev. author] “Eu.” 773; ἐσθήμασι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; χοροῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 220; δώροις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.14, “HG” 6.1.6; στρεπτοῖς καὶ ψελίοις τ. καὶ κοσμεῖν τινα [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.3:}} —_passive_, mostly in _perfect_ τετίμημαι, which alone is _passive_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **to be honoured, held in honour**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.608, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.69; ἐτιμήθη παρὰ Ξέρξῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105; ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 426c, etc.; τετίμαται πρὸς ἀθανάτων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).59(77); σκήπτρῳ.. δῶκε τετιμῆσθαι περὶ πάντων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.38, compare 12.310; τιμᾶσθαι προεδρίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.4, compare “Cyr.” 8.4.2; ἐκ τοῦ πολεμεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_ attracted to _genitive_, ὥς μευ ἀεὶ μέμνησαι ἐνηέος, οὐδέ σε λήθω, τιμῆς ἧς τέ μ᾽ ἔοικε τετιμῆσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.649 (but with _genitive_, τετειμημένος ὑπὸ τῶν αὐτοκρατόρων τετάρτης στρατείας}} (= Latin _quattuor militiis)_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Supp.epigram 7.145 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD))}}; οἱ τετιμηυένοι **men of rank, men in office**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.9}}; οἱ τιμώμενοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8.8.4, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” [913]}}; τῆς πόλεως τὸ τιμώμενον ἀπὸ τοῦ ἄρχειν **the honour enjoyed by** the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63.}}
**II** of things, **hold in honour** or **esteem, value, prize,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hymni Homerici 25.6, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.72, etc.}}; τί τὴν τυραννίδα τιμᾷς ὑπέρφε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 550}}; νόμους τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1211}}; τὴν εὐσέβειαν, ἀγνωμοσύναν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 1046, “Ba.” 885 (Lyric poetry); ἰσότητα [prev. author] “Ph.” 536, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149c; τὸ σωφρονεῖν τ. τοῦ βίου πλέον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1013.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ pretii, **estimate** or **value at** a certain price, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 917c, 921b, “PCair.Zen.” 269.13, 15 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 67.3 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; πλοῖα τετιμημένα χρημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26: _absolutely_, τετιμῆσθαι ἕκαστον τὴν οὐσίαν χρεών}} that each man should **have** his property **valued** (for assessment), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 955d, etc.; οἱ ὑπὲρ τὰς μυρίας τιμώμενοι δραχμάς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.23.15}}; τὸ τιμηθέν the **estimate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954b}}:—frequently in _middle_, διακοσίων ταλάντων ἐτιμήσατο <τὰ> αὑτοῦ **estimated** his property **at.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.48, compare “PPetr.” 2 _intransitive_ p.33 (3rd c.BC)}}; πρὸ παντὸς τιμᾶσθαί τι, like{περὶ παντὸς ποιεῖσθαι} (see. περί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, compare 1.33}}; πλείονος, μείζονος τιμᾶσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.10, “Cyr.” 2.1.13; τοσούτου τ. τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.45}}; μίαν ἡδονὴν θανάτου τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.5b: also with Preps., ἀντὶ παντὸς ἂν τιμησαίμην εἰπεῖν τοῦ βίου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.214}}: without a _genitive_, ἐτιμήσαντο τήν τε χώραν καὶ τὰς οἰκίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.62.7}}: simply, **value, estimate**, ἐν προικί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.35, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.57 (_passive_), 53.1}}; τινα {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.27.8, NT.Matt.27.9.}}
**II.3** rarely, **award** or **give as an honour**, Παιάν τέ σοι τιμᾷ φάος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270; ταὐτὰ τῇδέ μοι τάδε τιμᾶτε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 688; ἐκείνῳ δυσσεβῆ τιμᾷς χάριν [prev. author] “Ant.” 514; πατρῴαν τιμῶν χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 829 (Lyric poetry): hence}}
**III** as _Attic dialect_ lawterm {{Refs|compare (τίμημα 4):}}
**III.1** in _active_ (later in _middle_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 1.201 (3rd c.BC), 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.41, etc.)}}, of the court, **estimate the amount of punishment due** to the criminal, **award the penalty**, τιμάτω τὸ δικαστήριον, ὅ τι ἂν δέῃ πάσχειν.. τὸν ἡττηθέντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 843b; τὴν ἀξίαν τῆς βλάβης [prev. work] 879b; τ. τὰς βλάβας [prev. work] 843d; τ. τὴν δίκην [prev. work] 880d (compare below 2c)}}; ἅπασι τ. τὴν μακράν (i.e. γραμμήν) **award** them the long line, i.e. sentence of death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 106, ubi see Scholia: _absolutely_, ὡς ἐγὼ τιμᾶν βλέπω}} I carry **penalty** in my eyes, am itching for pains and penalties, {{Refs|[prev. work] 847: the sentence or judgement awarded is added in the _genitive_, τ. τινὶ θανάτου (i.e. δίκην)}}**give sentence** of death **against** a man, **condemn** him to death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.7 (compare 8), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.103 (_passive_), 32.15}}; τ. τινὶ δέκα ταλάντων **mulct** him in ten talents, {{Refs|[prev. author] 58.31}}; τίνος τιμήσειν αὐτῷ προσδοκᾷς τὸ δικαστήριο; at what do you expect the court **to fix** his **penalty?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 21.151, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37c}}; ἡ ἡλιαία τιμάτω περὶ αὐτοῦ ὅτου ἂν δόξῃ ἄξιος εἶναι παθεῖν Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.47: with _accusative_ _person_, τιμάτωσαν αὐτὸν καθ᾽ ὅτι ἂν δοκῇ τῷ κοινῷ “IG” 22.1275.16:}} —_passive_, τιμᾶσθαι ἀργυρίου **to be condemned** to a fine, τινος **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.22, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.47}}; ἐὰν.. ᾖ τῳ θανάτου τετιμημένον if **sentence** of death **has been passed** upon one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946e, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.38.}}
**III.2** in _middle_, of the parties before the court,
**III.2.a** of the accuser, τιμᾶταί μοι ὁ ἀνὴρ θανάτου (i.e. τὴν δίκην) he **estimates** the penalty **at** death (_genitive_ pretii) for me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b; εἰ βούλοιτο θανάτου σοι τιμᾶσθαι [prev. author] “Grg.” 486b, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.74,83, etc.}}
**III.2.b** of the person accused (compare ἀντιτιμάω, ὑποτιμάω), τιμήσεσθαι τοιούτου τινὸς ἐμαυτῷ **estimate** the penalty for myself at so high a rate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37b, compare 38b; ἐξῆν σοι φυγῆς τιμήσασθαι [prev. author] “Cri.” 52c; ἔδησεν ἑαυτὸν τιμησάμενος δεσμοῦ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.21}}: _perfect_ _passive_, θανάτου τετιμημένος ἑαυτῷ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.1:— 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1375a1 uses the}}_active_ in this sense.
**III.2.c** the _accusative_ of δίκη or of the offence is added, πέντε μυριάδων τιμησάμενος τὴν δίκην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 8, compare “Lys.” 13, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.42; θανάτου τιμῶμαι τὰ πεπολιτευμένα ἐμαυτῷ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 34.}}

5092:
** τῑμ-ή**, ἡ,
(τίω, see ad near the end).
**I** **worship, esteem, honour**, and in _plural_ **honours**, such as are accorded to gods or to superiors, or bestowed (whether by gods or men) as a reward for services, τιμῆς ἔμμοροί εἰσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.480; ὄφρ᾽ ἂν Ἀχαιοὶ υἱὸν ἐμὸν τίσωσιν ὀφέλλωσίν τέ ἑ τιμῇ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.510; ἐκ δὲ Διὸς τιμὴ καὶ κῦδος ὀπηδεῖ 17.251; ἐν δὲ ἰῇ τ. ἠμὲν κακὸς ἠδὲ καὶ ἐσθλός 9.319, compare 4.410; ἐν τ. σέβειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 166 (troch.); ἐν τ. ἄγεσθαί τινας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134}}; ἐν τ. τίθεσθαι or ἄγειν τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 538e; ἐν τιμαῖς ἔχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 199; τιμαῖς αὐξήσας τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.24}}; τιμὴν νεῖμαι, ἀπονέμειν τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1062, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 837c}}; τοῖς φίλοις τιμὰς νέμειν pay **due regard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1351}}; τιμὰς ὤπασας, πορών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 30, 946; διδόναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1424, etc.; ἀποδοῦναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415c; τὸ πρᾶγμ᾽ ἐμοὶ τιμὴν φέρει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 329; τινὶ τιμὰς προσάπτειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 356; ἀφύων τιμὴν περιάψας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 640 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τ. εὑρίσκεσθαι, δέκεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.48, 8.5; τιμὴν παρ᾽ ἀνθρώποις φέρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1278; τιμὰς ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.46, etc.; πρός τινος [prev. author] 1.120; ἐν μεγάλῃτιμῇ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.38}}; τιμῆς λαχεῖν, τυχεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 699, “El.” 364 (variant)}}; οἱ γεραίτεροι ταῖς τῶν νέων τιμαῖς ἀγάλλονται **paid to them by** the young, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.33: with _genitive_, χωρὶς ἡ τ. θεῶν}} **the honour due** to them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 637, compare “Ch.” 200; τιμὰς τὰς θεῶν πατεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 745; τιμαὶ δαιμόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 107: τιμῇ}} **with honour, honourably**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 381 codices}}; τιμῆς ἕνεκα as **a mark of honour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.28; τιμῇ προέξουσ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 208.}}
**I.2** **honour, dignity, lordship**, as the attribute of gods or kings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.278, 9.498, etc.; θεῶν ἒξ ἔμμορε τιμῆς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.335; τ. βασιληΐς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.193, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 393, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.108, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 228 (_plural_); Περσονόμος τ. μεγάλη [prev. author] “Pers.” 919 (_anapaest meter_)}}; δίθρονος.. καὶ δίσκηπτρος τ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 44 (_anapaest meter_)}}: generally, like{γέρας}, **prerogative** or **special attribute** of a king, and in _plural_ his **prerogatives**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.117, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 203, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 374, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 909 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; βασιλικαὶ τ. imperial **prerogatives**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.10.5; σκῆπτρον τιμάς τ᾽ ἀποσυλᾶται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 172 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**I.3** **a dignity, office, magistracy**, and in _plural_, **civic honours** (τιμὰς λέγομεν εἶναι τὰς ἀρχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a31), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, etc.}}; ἔν τε ταῖς ἀρχαῖς καὶ ταῖς ἄλλαις τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35b, compare “Ti.” 20a, etc.; μὴ φεύγειν τοὺς πόνους, ἢ μηδὲ τὰς τ. διώκειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63; τιμὴν ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.8, etc.; τὴν τιμὴν εἴληχε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61c}}; οἱ ἐν τιμαῖς men in **office**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 19 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.81; ἐκβαλῶ σε ἐκ τῆς τιμῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.9}}; τιμὰς ἴσχειν hold the **office** of τιμοῦχος (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Jahresh.” 12.136 (Erythrae, 5th-4th c.BC)}}: generally, **office, task**, ἄχαρις τιμή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36:}} —also,
**I.3.b** **a person in authority, an authority**, κλῦτε δὲ Γᾶ (Ahrens for τὰ) χθονίων τε τιμαί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 399 (Lyric poetry)}}; καὶ τὰ καρτερώτατα τιμαῖς ὑπείκει yield to **authorities**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670.}}
**I.4** **present of honour, compliment, offering**, e.g. to the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 142, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 622}}; **reward, present**, ἢ ἀργύριον ἢ τιμή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 347a}}; τιμαὶ καὶ δωρεαί{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 361c; ὅσοι.. ἄλλην τινὰ δωρεὰν ἢ τ. ἔχουσιν παρὰ τῶν Λεβεδίων “SIG” 344.22 (Teos, 4th c.BC)}}; τῶν εὐεργεσιῶν τιμὰς φέρονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113e.}}
**I.5** ἡ Δάου τ. 'the worthy {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator ', 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.68.}}
**II** of things, **worth, value, price,** {{Refs|Hymnus in Cererem 132, “IG” 12.349.10, 15, +others}}; ἐξευρίσκοντες τιμῆς τὰ κάλλιστα at **a price**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.119; τῆς αὐτῆς τ. πωλεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.12; πρίασθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.149; δεκαπλάσιον τῆς τ. ἀποτίνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914c; ἀποδιδόναι τινὶ τὴν τ. [prev. work]a; δύο εἰπεῖν τ}}. to name two **prices**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 917b}}; ἀξιοῦν τι τ. τινός {{Refs|[prev. work]d}}; περὶ τῆς τ. διαφέρεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.15}}; ἐμοὶ δὲ τιμὰ τᾶσδε πᾷ γενήσετα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 895}}; ἑστηκυῖαι τ. fixed **prices,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PTeb. 703.176 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὑπὲρ τιμῆς πυροῦ payment of money representing the **value** of wheat, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Ostr.” 663 (2nd c.AD), +others}}
**II.2** **valuation, estimate**, for purposes of assessment, τοῦ κλήρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 744e: generally, ὁ πλοῦτος οἷον τιμή τις τῆς ἀξίας τῶν ἄλλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1391a1.}}
**III** **compensation, satisfaction, penalty**, τιμὴν ἀρνύμενοι Μενελάῳ.. πρὸς Τρώων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.159, compare 5.552}}; ἀποτινέμεν, τίνειν τιμήν τινι, pay or make **it**, {{Refs|3.286, 288}}; τιμὴν ἄγειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.57; Πάτροκλον, ὃς κεῖται ἐμῆς ἕνεκ᾽ ἐνθάδε τιμῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.92, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.70, 117}}; οὐ σὴ.. ἡ τ. the **penalty** is not yours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497b. (The spelling [τῑ- not τει- “IG” 12.347.33, etc.] and the majority of the senses show that τιμή is _cognate_ with τίω}} 'value, honour'; sense 111 perhaps arose from a later association with τίνω.)

5093:
** τίμι-ος** [τῑ], α, ον,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347c, +others}}; also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 948 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283a36, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.651}}: (τιμή):— **valued**,
**I** **held in honour, worthy**, ὅδε πᾶσι φίλος καὶ τ. ἐστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.38, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 10, 105, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 49, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.71, etc.}}; ἄνδρα τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 556}}; γενεᾷ τίμιος [Δανάη] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus [same place]; τίμιοι ἐν τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 829d; νηὸν.. πᾶσιν μάλα τ. ἀνθρώποισι “hymnus ad Apollinem” 483, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 134.7, etc.}}: frequently in _comparative_ and _superlative_, τιμιώταται θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 967 (Lyric poetry); πασῶν Ἀθῆναι -ωτάτη πόλις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 108; -ώτερον ἐπιστήμη ὀρθῆς δόξης ἐστί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 98a}}; as form of address, **-ώτατε**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1 “Prooem.”; τῷ -ωτάτῳ χαίρειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ep.Sat.” 2.25, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 41.2, “Sammelb.” 7347.29 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** of things, **valuable, prized**, οὐδὲν κτῆμα -ώτερον {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 702, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 301, “Ph.” 439 (_superlative_), NT.Rev.17.4.}}
**II.2** **of high price, costly, dear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23 (_superlative_), 8.105 (_comparative_), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 304b, “Prt.” 347c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 18 (_superlative_)}}; πωλεῖται τίμιος is sold **dearly**,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 31; τίμιον ἀγοράζειν “PCair.Zen.” 160.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; χρυσίον ὅταν πολὺ παραφανῇ, τὸ ἀργύριον -ώτερον ποιεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.10: _comparative_ -έστερος uncertain in “PCair.Zen.” 375.13 (3rd c.BC)}}. _adverb_ -ίως, πρίασθαι {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.35.}}
**II.3** **conferring honour, honourable**, τιμία ἕδρα a seat **of honour**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 854, compare “Th.” 241 (Lyric poetry)}}; τ. γέρας an **honourable** privilege, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 986}}; οὑπιρρέων γὰρ -ώτερος χρόνος ἔσται πολίταις **more full of honour**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 853}}; -ωτέρα χώρα a **higher** place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.10; δῶρα [prev. author] “An.” 1.2.27: τὰ τίμια}} ={τιμαί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 221 (conjecture), “SIG” 659.8 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.9.8, “Supp.epigram” 3.468.10, +others (Thess., 1st c.BC)}}; τὰ τ. τῶν ἱερῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.33 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; also in _singular_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 591.6 (Lampsacus, 2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ -ώτατα, ={τὰ φίλτατα}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.215}}; αἱ καλούμεναι τίμιαι τέσσαρες τέχναι Zos.Alch.p.239 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

5094:
** τιμι-ότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**dignity**, διαφέρειν τιμιότητι καὶ ἀτιμίᾳ ἀλλήλων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 736b31; δυνάμει καὶ τ. ὑπερέχειν [prev. author] “EN” 1178a1.}}
**2** **costliness,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 18.19.}}
**II** as form of address, ἡ σὴ τ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.145 (4th-5th c.AD).}}

5097:
** τῑμωρ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.87, etc.}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 733, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58}}: _aorist_ ἐτιμωρησάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378a}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ τετῑμώρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.15}}: used in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.8, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.32, and probably 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67}}: _aorist_ ἐτιμωρήθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 57.9 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**to be an avenger, exact** or **seek to exact vengeance for, avenge**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 261, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103, 8.144, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 749, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 180a, etc.: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4: with _dative_ et _genitive_, τιμωρήσειν σοι τοῦ παιδός.. ὑπισχνοῦμαι}} uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.8:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, τ. τῷ ἑταίρῳ τὸν φόνον **avenge** his slaughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 935: also τ. ὑπέρ τινος}} (for τινι) **take vengeance for** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 907e, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.1}}; ὑπὲρ τοῦ δήμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]51: with _dative_ _of things_, τῷ θανάτῳ τοῦ πατρός 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.77: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729e}}:—_middle_, **avenge** or **seek to avenge one's own** relative or friend, πατρὶ πάντα τιμωρουμένης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 349, compare 399, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1117:}} — _impersonal_ in _passive_, Λεωνίδῃ τετιμώρηται **vengeance has been taken** for him, he **has been avenged**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.79 (but τετιμωρήσεαι ἐς.. Λεωνίδην must be taken in signification 1.3b}}, **thou wilt have taken vengeance** in respect to L., {{Refs|[prev. work] 78).}}
**2** τιμωρεῖν τινα **take vengeance on** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 140}}; τοὺς αὐτοέντας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 107; κελεύειν τιμωρεῖν ὑπὲρ αὑτοῦ [τὸν Ἀγόρατον] ὡς φονέα ὄντα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.42}}, compare 92; Παλαμήδους σε τιμωρεῖ φόνου (variant{φόνον}) **is taking vengeance on** you **for** the murder of P., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 433}}:—_passive_, **to be visited with vengeance, punished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525b, 525d, etc.; τετιμωρημένος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.15}}; ὧν ἡμάρτομεν τετιμωρήμεθα we **have been punished,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 495.2 (3rd c.BC); ἱκανῶς τετιμώρημαι [prev. work]639.15 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἰς τὸ -ηθῆναι αὐτούς {{Refs|“PMich.Zen.” [same place]:}} —this sense is more frequently in
**3** _middle_, **avenge oneself upon, exact vengeance from**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53, 6.138, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1258, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 756, 882, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121, etc.; ὡς.. οὐχ ὅπως τιμωρήσαιντο, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐπαινέσειαν τὸν Σφοδρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.34}}; Ἑαυτὸν τιμωρούμενος **Self-punisher**, name of a play by Menander,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.15; ἀπαγαγών με εἰς τὸ δεσμωτήριον τετιμώρηταί με εἰσπράσσων δραχμὰς “PCair.Zen.” 492.7 (3rd c.BC):}} with _genitive_ _of things_, τιμωρεῖσθαί τινά τινος **take vengeance on** one **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.145, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 558, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213d, etc.: also τ. τινὰ ἀντί τινων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.135}}; περὶ (perhaps ὑπὲρ) τῶν πεπραγμένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.2:}} less frequently with _accusative_ _of things_, εἰ μή σ᾽ ἀδελφῆς αἷμα τιμωρήσεται **will visit** his sister's blood **on** thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 733, compare “Cyc.” 695.}}
**3.b** _absolutely_, **avenge oneself, seek vengeance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49, 7.8.β, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.19, etc.}}; ταῖς ἐσχάταις τιμωρίαις τ. **visit** with the extreme penalties, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579a}}; τὸ τιμωρησόμενον **the power to exact vengeance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.4}}: the crime is sometimes added in a _relative_ clause, τ. εἴ τι.. ἠδίκησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.6; τ. ὅτι.. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.30: also τιμωρεῖσθαι ὑπὲρ τῆς Ἑλλάδος}} **exact vengeance** for Greece, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.3.4.}}
**II** especially in _present_ and _imperfect_, **succour** one who has been attacked or has suffered injury, **help** him **to retaliate**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, 152, 2.63, 3.32, 65, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 136, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 718, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86, 127, +others; εἰ τύπτοιτό τις αὐτῶν ὑπ᾽ ἐκείνων τινός, ἐβοήθεόν τε πάντες καὶ ἐτιμώρεον ἀλλήλοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.138, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.4}}: _absolutely_, **lend aid, give succour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18.}}
**II.2** of medical aid, τ. τῷ παθήματι **relieve** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11; compare τιμωρία 11.2, and 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.494.}}

5098:
** τῑμωρ-ία**,
Ionic -ίη, ἡ, **retribution, vengeance** (differing from κόλασις, corrective punishment, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1369b12), ἐς τ... παρασκευαζομένοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.90; τ. καὶ τίσις [prev. author] 7.8.ά, cf. NT.Heb.10.29, etc.}}; πατρὸς τ. **vengeance taken for** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 425}}; μητρὸς αἵματος τιμωρίαι **for having shed** a mother's blood, {{Refs|[prev. work] 400}}; ἐπὶ τῇ ἡμετέρᾳ τ. for the purpose of **punishing** us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63; Λεωνίδην ἐάν τις ἀποκτείνῃ.. τὴν τ. εἶναι καθάπερ ἐάν τις Ἀθηναίων ἀποθάνῃ “IG” 12.56.16, compare 154.12; οὐκ ἔχει τιμωρίαν γὰρ τἀδίκημ᾽, ἔγκλημα δέ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 253}}; ἡ κατὰ τῶν προδιδόντων τ. **vengeance** against or upon.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 140, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.274, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.105; τ. ἐσομένη ἔς τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.55; τ. ὑπὲρ τοῦ ἀδικηθέντος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.19: with Verbs, of the avenger, ποιεῖσθαι τιμωρίαν}} execute **vengeance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.26, etc.}}; τινος on one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.18; τ. ὑπὲρ ὧν ἐπεπόνθειν λαβεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.8}}; but παρά τινος λαμβάνειν τ. exact **it** from him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 88.14}}; of the wrongdoer, τ. Ἀθηνῶν ηὗρε found, i.e. suffered, **vengeance** at Athens' hand, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 473}}; τιμωρίας τυγχάνειν **to be punished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472d, “PEnteux.” 50.7 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc. (but also, obtain **vengeance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.7); τ. ἀντιδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53; τίνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905a, etc.; ὑπέχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716b, etc.}}; of persons in authority, αἱ τ. εἰσὶ παρὰ τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120}}; τ. δοῦναί τινι give him **right of vengeance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.7, compare [prev. work]54, 59.86; so τὰς τ. τοῖς ἰδιώταις ἐποίησε βραδείας [prev. author] 26.4}}: _plural_, **penalties**, λαμβάνειν τὰς ἀξίας τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 247}}; ταῖς ἐσχάταις τιμωρεῖσθαι τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579a, compare “Lg.” 943d, +others}}; of state-punishments, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.6.26, +others}}; οἱ ἐπὶ τῶν τ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 14, 17}}; of divine punishments, ἐξορκίζω σε.. κατὰ <τῶν ἐπὶ> τῶν τ. τεταγμένων {{Refs|“PMag.Lond.” 121.303.}}
**II** **succour**, εὑρήσεται τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148}}; ἡ ἀφ᾽ ὑμῶν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, compare 5.112; τ. ποιεῖσθαί τινι [prev. author] 1.124}}; τ. τοῦ τεθνεῶτος **due** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.5.}}
**II.2** of **medical and**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 18 (_plural_); compare τιμωρέω 11.2.}}

5099:
** τίνω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.289, +others, (ἀπο-) “IG” 5(1).1390.76 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}, etc.: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ τίνεσκον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.475:}}_future_ τείσω (ἐκ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.412.6 (4th c.BC), (ἀπο-) “epigram Gr.” 1132 (_Attic dialect_ vase, 4th c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.55 (3rd c.BC), “IG” 7.3073.1 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC), etc.; 5th c.AD: Cypria}} _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πείσει {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.12 H.:}}_aorist_ ἔτεισα (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 47.15 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC), 663.25 (Delos, 3rd-2nd c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.41 (3rd c.BC), etc.: frequently written τίσω ἔτισα}} in Hellenistic and later Inscrr. and Papyrus, and in codices of all authors (_future_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.348, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 277, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 113, etc.}}; _aorist_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.352, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.58, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 810, etc.): in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus confused (both in codices and printed texts) with τίσω ἔτισα}}_future_ and _aorist_ of τίω, and only to be distinguished by the sense: _perfect_ τέτεικα (ἀπο-) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 437.6 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; _participle_ τετεικώς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 765 (τετικώς, τεθεικώς codices) (see. ἐκτίνω)}}:—_middle_, _present_ first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 204 (only τίνυμαι in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _future_ τείσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.15, +others}}: _aorist_ ἐτεισάμην {{Refs|3.197, 15.236, +others (τισ- codices)}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐξ-ετείσθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1613.198, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.15, 59.7}}: _perfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐκ-τέτεισται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.187. [τῑνω (from *τίνϝω)}} in _Epic dialect_, also {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 204, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.51, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.657 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; τῐνω in Trag., as {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 112, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 635, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 7; also in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.24 (_middle_) and 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.31}}; also in some Epigrammatists, as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Simmias 25.1, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.286 (Marc. argument).]}}
**I** _active_, **pay a price** by way of return or recompense, mostly in bad sense, **pay** a penalty, with _accusative_ of the penalty, τ. θωήν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.193; τιμήν τινι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.289; ποινάς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.58, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 112, 4th c.BC: Theodectes Tragicus 8.9; δίκην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 113, “El.” 298, “fragment” 107.9, NT.2Thes.1.9, etc.}}; also τ. ἴσην (i.e. δίκην) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 810; διπλῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946e; τὸ ἥμισυ [prev. work] 767e (assuming variant); μείζονα τὴν ἔκτισίν τινι [prev. work] 933e}}; τὴν προσήκουσαν τιμωρίαν{{Refs|[prev. work] 905a, compare “Tragica Adespota” 490:}} —but also
**I.b** in good sense, **pay** a debt, **acquit oneself of** an obligation, ζωάγρια τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.407; τείσειν αἴσιμα πάντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.348; εὐαγγέλιον (reward for bringing good news) 14.166}}; τ. χάριν τινί **render** one thanks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 985; τ. γῇ δασμόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 635; ἰατροῖς μισθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.54:}} —also simply,
**I.c** **repay**, with _accusative_ _of things_, τροφάς τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 109:}} —in various phrases, τ. ἀντιποίνους δύας **repay** equivalent sorrows, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 268 (Lyric poetry)}}; φόνον φόνου ῥύσιον τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 959}}; αἱμάτων παλαιτέρων τ. μύσος **send** one pollution **in repayment** for another, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 650 (Lyric poetry, Lachm., for τείνει); ἀρᾶς τ. χρέος [prev. author] “Ag.” 457 (Lyric poetry).--Constr.:}}
**I.1** with _accusative_ of the thing paid or of the thing repaid (see. above).
**I.2** less frequently with _dative_, κράατι τείσεις **with** thy head, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.218; ψυχῇ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 277.}}
**I.3** with _dative_ of _person_ to whom payment is made (see. above).
**I.4** with _dative_ of the penalty, τ. θανάτῳ ἅπερ ἦρξεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1529 (_anapaest meter_); τύμμα τύμματι [prev. work] 1430 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.5** with _genitive_ of the thing for which one pays, τ. ἀμοιβὴν βοῶν τινι **pay** him compensation **for** the cows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.382}}; τ. τινὶ ποινήν τινος **pay** one retribution **for.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, 7.134}}; τ. μητρὸς δίκας **for** thy mother, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 531}}; ἀντὶ πληγῆς πληγὴν τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 313 (_anapaest meter_)}}: also with _accusative_ of the thing for which one pays, the price being omitted, **pay** or **atone for** a thing, τείσειαν Δαναοὶ ἐμὰ δάκρυα σοῖσι βέλεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.42; τ. ὕβριν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.352}}; τ. φόνον or λώβην τινός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.134, 11.142; κακά 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 735; διπλᾶ δ᾽ ἔτεισαν Πριαμίδαι θἀμάρτια 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 537: less frequently with _accusative_ _person_, τείσεις γνωτὸν τὸν ἔπεφνες}} **thou shalt make atonement for** the brother thou hast slain, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.34.}}
**I.6** _absolutely_, **make return** or **requital**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.29; παθόντα οὐκ ἐπίστασθαι τίνειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1203, compare 230 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** _middle_, **have a price paid one, make** another **pay for** a thing, **avenge oneself on** him, **punish** him, frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards.--Constr.:}}
**II.1** with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.743, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.197, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, 123, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 996, etc.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ criminis, τείσασθαι Ἀλέξανδρον κακότητος **punish** him **for** his wickedness, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.366, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.206, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 204 (where ἀμπλακίης, variant{-ίας}), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.118, etc.; τ. τινὰ ἐφ᾽ ἁμαρτωλῇ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1248; ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, 73.}}]
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **take vengeance for** a thing, τείσασθαι φόνον, βίην τινός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.116, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.31; λώβην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.208, etc.}}
**II.4** with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, ἐτείσατο ἔργον ἀεικὲς ἀντίθεον Νηλῆα **he made** Neleus **pay for** the misdeed, **visited** it **on** his head, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.236; Ζεῦ ἄνα, δὸς τείσασθαι, ὅ με πρότερος κάκ᾽ ἔοργε, δῖον Ἀλέξανδρον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.351}}; τείσασθαί τινα δίκην **exact** retribution **from** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1316 (uncertain reading).}}
**II.5** with _dative_ modi, τίνεσθαί τινα ἀγαναῖς ἀμοιβαῖς, φυγῇ, **repay** or **requite** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.24, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 638.}}
**II.6** _absolutely_, **repay oneself, indemnify oneself**, ἡμεῖς δ᾽ αὖτε ἀγειρόμενοι κατὰ δῆμον τεισόμεθ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.15}}. (Root **q[uglide][icaron]-** [alternating with **q[uglide]ei-** and **q[uglide]oi-**] 'pay': τῐ-νϝ-ω, τῐ-σις, τεί-σω [[{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria πείσει], ἔ-τει-σα [compare ἀππεισάτου see at {ἀποτίνω], ποι-νή} (which see)}}]: Sanskrit _cáy-ate_ 'avenge, punish': **ápa-ci-tis** 'vengeance':—not related to τίω.)
